export const WEEK_INDEX = {"CATHOLIC":{"1":{"day":1,"title":"the smallest prayer","word":"the sign of the cross","hook":"forehead, chest, shoulder, shoulder: what the hand is tracing","length":"3:00","carry":"head, heart, shoulders — all of you","segments":[{"type":"the bell","duration":null,"voice":"","screen":["`DAY ONE.`"]},{"type":"the hook","duration":"25 sec","voice":"A pitcher does it before the first throw. A grandmother does it when the bus passes a church. Someone does it in a hospital hallway, right after the doctor leaves. Forehead, chest, one shoulder, the other. Two seconds, no words out loud. If you grew up Catholic, your hand may know it better than your head does. It's a prayer, maybe the smallest there is, and the hand is tracing something on purpose.","screen":["`A PITCHER. A GRANDMOTHER. A HOSPITAL HALLWAY.` → `TWO SECONDS.` → `THE SMALLEST PRAYER.`"]},{"type":"the teach","duration":"1 min","voice":"It's old. Around the year 200, a Christian writer in North Africa described believers tracing a tiny cross on their foreheads all day long: getting dressed, sitting down to eat, lighting a lamp. Over the centuries that thumb-sized cross grew into the big one. And it says two things at once. The hand traces the cross, the shape Jesus died on, over your own body. The words name who Catholics believe God is. They come from the end of Matthew's Gospel, where Jesus sends his friends out, in the Douay-Rheims Bible: \"baptizing them in the name of the Father, and of the Son, and of the Holy Ghost.\" One God: Father, Son and Holy Spirit. That's why many Catholics dip their fingers in holy water at the church door first. Those same words were said over them, with water, at their baptism. Now look where the hand goes. The forehead, where you think. The chest, where your heart is. The shoulders, where you carry things. Many hear it that way: nothing left out. Western Catholics go to the left shoulder first; Orthodox Christians go right, then left. Same sign, two old habits.","screen":["`A THUMB ON THE FOREHEAD, AROUND 200.` · `THE CROSS, AND THE NAME.` · `HOLY WATER: A REMINDER OF BAPTISM.` · `THINK, FEEL, CARRY.`"]},{"type":"the practice","duration":"15 sec","voice":"One breath with the bell. If you'd like, make the sign slowly, slower than anyone ever does it: forehead, chest, left shoulder, right. Or just rest a hand on each place and notice it. Then let the breath all the way out.","screen":[]},{"type":"the word","duration":"10 sec","voice":"The sign of the cross. The cross traced over all of you, in the name of the Father, the Son and the Holy Spirit. First word on your strand.","screen":["`THE SIGN OF THE CROSS — your 1st word` → clicks onto an empty word-strand."]},{"type":"the carry","duration":"10 sec","voice":"Your line: head, heart, shoulders — all of you. Two seconds, and nothing left out. See you tomorrow.","screen":[]},{"type":"the close","duration":null,"voice":"","screen":["`DAY 1 COUNTS.` → `TOMORROW: THE CLAIM BEHIND THE BREAD.`"]}]},"2":{"day":2,"title":"the claim behind the bread","word":"the Eucharist","hook":"a white dress, a party, a wafer: and the claim the day was about","length":"3:00","carry":"he stayed","segments":[{"type":"the bell","duration":null,"voice":"","screen":["`DAY TWO.`"]},{"type":"review","duration":"10 sec","voice":"Yesterday: the sign of the cross, all of you in two seconds. Today, the thing at the center of every Mass.","screen":["`THE SIGN OF THE CROSS ✓` → `DAY 2.`"]},{"type":"the hook","duration":"25 sec","voice":"A white dress or a clip-on tie. Photos on the church steps, a cake after. First Communion is easy to remember. What the day was actually about can be fuzzier: in surveys, a lot of Catholics describe the bread as a symbol. The Church's own claim is much bigger than a symbol. So here it is, once, plainly.","screen":["`A WHITE DRESS. A CAKE.` → `A SYMBOL?` → `THE CLAIM, ONCE, PLAINLY.`"]},{"type":"the teach","duration":"1 min","voice":"It starts the night before Jesus died, at a Passover meal. He takes bread, gives thanks, breaks it and says, in the Douay-Rheims Bible: \"This is my body, which is given for you. Do this for a commemoration of me.\" Gave thanks, in Greek, is where the word comes from: eucharistia, thanksgiving. Now the claim. Catholics believe that when the priest says those words at Mass, the bread and wine truly become the body and blood of Christ. They still look, taste and feel like bread and wine. What they are, underneath, has changed. Not a symbol of him: him. The Church calls it the real presence. In John's Gospel, Jesus calls himself the living bread, and many followers find that too hard and walk away. He lets them go. After Mass, the consecrated bread that's left is kept in a small box near the altar, the tabernacle, with a red lamp burning beside it. That's why people bend a knee when they walk into an empty church. They believe he's there. Christians differ here. Orthodox Christians and Lutherans also speak of a real presence; many other Protestants see the meal as a memorial and a sign.","screen":["`THIS IS MY BODY.` · `EUCHARISTIA — THANKSGIVING.` · `STILL LOOKS LIKE BREAD.` · `THE TABERNACLE AND THE LAMP.`"]},{"type":"the practice","duration":"15 sec","voice":"One breath with the bell. Think of a time someone simply stayed with you: in a waiting room, on the end of your bed, on the phone. They didn't fix anything. Breathe in, remembering it. Breathe out slowly.","screen":[]},{"type":"the word","duration":"10 sec","voice":"The Eucharist. Thanksgiving: the bread and wine Catholics believe become Christ himself, his real presence. Second word.","screen":["`THE EUCHARIST — your 2nd word` → clicks onto the strand beside the sign of the cross."]},{"type":"the carry","duration":"10 sec","voice":"Your line: he stayed. It's what the red lamp is saying, in an empty church on a Tuesday afternoon. Day two, done.","screen":[]},{"type":"the close","duration":null,"voice":"","screen":["`DAY 2 COUNTS.` → `TOMORROW: THE MOST MISUNDERSTOOD ROOM IN THE BUILDING.`"]}]},"3":{"day":3,"title":"the most misunderstood room in the building","word":"confession","hook":"the booth, the nerves, and why a person and not just a prayer","length":"3:00","carry":"say it out loud, then it's smaller","segments":[{"type":"the bell","duration":null,"voice":"","screen":["`DAY THREE.`"]},{"type":"review","duration":"10 sec","voice":"Yesterday: the Eucharist, and the claim behind the bread. Today, the room people are most nervous to walk into.","screen":["`THE EUCHARIST ✓` → `DAY 3.`"]},{"type":"the hook","duration":"25 sec","voice":"Bless me, Father, for I have sinned. Plenty of people raised Catholic can still say the opening line, and still remember the nerves: the line outside the booth, the list rehearsed in your head, the little screen. Movies made it a courtroom, or a punch line. And later comes the fair question: why tell a person? Why not just tell God?","screen":["`BLESS ME, FATHER.` → `THE BOOTH. THE NERVES.` → `WHY A PERSON?`"]},{"type":"the teach","duration":"1 min","voice":"Catholics call it the sacrament of reconciliation; most just say confession. The answer to why a person starts on Easter night. In John's Gospel, the risen Jesus breathes on his friends and says, in the Douay-Rheims Bible: \"Whose sins you shall forgive, they are forgiven them.\" Catholics believe that from then on, forgiveness was meant to be spoken out loud, through a person, and heard out loud too. God does the forgiving; the priest speaks for him, and for the Church, since a wrong never touches just one person. What happens is simple. You can kneel behind a screen or sit face to face. You say what you've done, plainly, in your own words. The priest listens, maybe gives advice, and gives a penance, usually a prayer or a small act to make things right. Then he says the words of absolution, forgiving you in the name of the Father, the Son and the Holy Spirit. And one rule is absolute: the seal. A priest may never reveal what he hears, to anyone, for any reason. Part of the gift is just this. Something huge and shapeless at two in the morning gets edges once it's spoken. Then a human voice tells you that you're forgiven.","screen":["`WHOSE SINS YOU SHALL FORGIVE.` · `BEHIND A SCREEN OR FACE TO FACE.` · `THE SEAL: NEVER REVEALED.` · `SPOKEN, IT HAS EDGES.`"]},{"type":"the practice","duration":"20 sec","voice":"Three breaths with the bell. On the first, think of one thing you've been carrying and never said out loud. On the second, name it to yourself in one plain sentence. On the third, breathe out, and notice whether it feels any different now that it has a name.","screen":[]},{"type":"the word","duration":"10 sec","voice":"Confession. Saying it out loud, and hearing out loud that you're forgiven. Third word.","screen":["`CONFESSION — your 3rd word`."]},{"type":"the carry","duration":"10 sec","voice":"Your line: say it out loud, then it's smaller. That's the whole secret of the little room. Day three.","screen":[]},{"type":"the close","duration":null,"voice":"","screen":["`DAY 3 COUNTS.` → `TOMORROW: WHAT THE STRING IS FOR.`"]}]},"4":{"day":4,"title":"what the string is for","word":"the rosary","hook":"beads on a rearview mirror: counting, not magic","length":"3:00","carry":"hands busy, heart free","segments":[{"type":"the bell","duration":null,"voice":"","screen":["`DAY FOUR.`"]},{"type":"review","duration":"10 sec","voice":"Yesterday: confession, the thing said out loud. Today, something that might be hanging from a rearview mirror near you.","screen":["`CONFESSION ✓` → `DAY 4.`"]},{"type":"the hook","duration":"25 sec","voice":"A loop of beads swinging from a rearview mirror. Tangled in a grandmother's purse with the car keys and the cough drops. Laid in someone's hands at a funeral. Some people wear one like a necklace; some call it a lucky charm. People can't even agree how many beads are on it. The string is for something much plainer than luck. It's for counting.","screen":["`ON A MIRROR. IN A PURSE.` → `A LUCKY CHARM?` → `IT'S FOR COUNTING.`"]},{"type":"the teach","duration":"1 min","voice":"Rosary comes from the Latin rosarium, a rose garden: a garland of prayers, like a crown of roses. Here's the layout. A cross, a short tail of beads, then the loop: five sets of ten small beads, called decades, each set apart by a single bead. On the single bead, the Our Father. On each of the ten, a Hail Mary. After each ten, a short line of praise, the Glory Be. That's where the arguments about the count come from: fifty-nine beads on the whole string, fifty-three Hail Marys. Counting prayers is ancient. Stories from the early desert monks tell of one who kept track of his daily prayers with pebbles in his lap. Later, monks prayed all one hundred and fifty psalms, and tradition says people who couldn't read prayed one hundred and fifty short prayers instead. The rosary grew out of that, and was called the poor man's psalter. And here's the plain secret. While the fingers and lips are busy with the words, the mind is free to think through scenes from the lives of Jesus and Mary, called the mysteries. That's the real prayer. The beads just keep the hands busy.","screen":["`ROSARIUM — A ROSE GARDEN.` · `FIVE DECADES OF TEN.` · `PEBBLES IN A MONK'S LAP.` · `THE MIND ON THE MYSTERIES.`"]},{"type":"the practice","duration":"20 sec","voice":"Three breaths with the bell. Rest one hand in your lap. On each breath out, touch your thumb to the next finger: one, two, three. Don't count out loud. Let the fingers keep track, so the mind doesn't have to.","screen":[]},{"type":"the word","duration":"10 sec","voice":"The rosary. A garland of prayers on a string that keeps count, so the mind can rest on the life of Jesus. Fourth word.","screen":["`THE ROSARY — your 4th word`."]},{"type":"the carry","duration":"10 sec","voice":"Your line: hands busy, heart free. The beads hold the count, so the mind can go somewhere better. That's day four.","screen":[]},{"type":"the close","duration":null,"voice":"","screen":["`DAY 4 COUNTS.` → `TOMORROW: HONOR, NOT WORSHIP.`"]}]},"5":{"day":5,"title":"honor, not worship","word":"the saints","hook":"\"isn't praying to Mary idolatry?\" — the answer is one difference","length":"3:00","carry":"the company you're in","segments":[{"type":"the bell","duration":null,"voice":"","screen":["`DAY FIVE.`"]},{"type":"review","duration":"10 sec","voice":"Yesterday: the rosary, a string that keeps count. Today, the question a friend may have asked you about Mary.","screen":["`THE ROSARY ✓` → `DAY 5.`"]},{"type":"the hook","duration":"25 sec","voice":"Sooner or later a friend asks it, usually kindly: why do Catholics pray to Mary? Isn't that worshiping a statue? Walk into an old church and you can see why they'd wonder. Plaster figures in niches. Faces in stained glass. Candles flickering in front of a woman in blue and a man holding keys. The answer turns on one difference, and it's a difference people already live by.","screen":["`WHY PRAY TO MARY?` → `A WOMAN IN BLUE. A MAN WITH KEYS.` → `ONE DIFFERENCE.`"]},{"type":"the teach","duration":"1 min","voice":"Catholics draw a sharp line between worship and honor. Worship belongs to God alone. Honor can be given to people: the way you'd honor a grandmother, a teacher, a hero. Saints get honor. Mary, the mother of Jesus, gets the highest honor of any saint, but still honor, not worship. So why pray to them? Because what Catholics are doing is asking: pray for me. It's what you'd ask a friend. Catholics believe in what the old creed calls the communion of saints: everyone who belongs to Christ, living and dead, is one family, and the family can still pray for each other. Death doesn't end the friendship. So a statue works like a family photo. Nobody is praying to the plaster. And the saints were never perfect. The man with the keys is Peter, who said three times, the night Jesus was arrested, that he didn't know him, then went out and wept. Catholics count him the first pope. A saint isn't someone who never fell. It's someone who kept getting up. Other Christians see it differently: many Protestants honor the saints as examples, and don't ask for their prayers.","screen":["`WORSHIP: GOD ALONE.` · `HONOR: MARY AND THE SAINTS.` · `PRAY FOR ME.` · `THE COMMUNION OF SAINTS.`"]},{"type":"the practice","duration":"20 sec","voice":"Three breaths with the bell. Think of someone, alive or gone, who failed at something and kept going. On the first breath, picture their face. On the second, remember what they got wrong. On the third, remember that they kept going.","screen":[]},{"type":"the word","duration":"10 sec","voice":"The saints. Holy ones: honored, not worshiped, and still one family with the living. Fifth word.","screen":["`THE SAINTS — your 5th word`."]},{"type":"the carry","duration":"10 sec","voice":"Your line: the company you're in. Flawed people, still going, still in the family. See you tomorrow.","screen":[]},{"type":"the close","duration":null,"voice":"","screen":["`DAY 5 COUNTS.` → `TOMORROW: ASHES, AND FORTY DAYS.`"]}]},"6":{"day":6,"title":"ashes, and forty days","word":"Lent","hook":"a smudge on the forehead, fish on Friday: giving something up is a way of paying attention","length":"3:00","carry":"less, on purpose","segments":[{"type":"the bell","duration":null,"voice":"","screen":["`DAY SIX.`"]},{"type":"review","duration":"10 sec","voice":"Yesterday: the saints, honored, not worshiped. Today, the one day a year Catholics walk around with a smudge on their foreheads.","screen":["`THE SAINTS ✓` → `DAY 6.`"]},{"type":"the hook","duration":"25 sec","voice":"One Wednesday a year, a coworker shows up with a black smudge on her forehead. Then the barista. Then a kid on the train. Somebody leans over to say: you've got something on your face. It's Ash Wednesday, and it starts a season. For the next forty days, a Catholic friend might be off chocolate, or off social media, or eating fish on Fridays. It can look like a diet. It isn't about the chocolate.","screen":["`A SMUDGE ON THE FOREHEAD.` → `IT'S ASH WEDNESDAY.` → `IT ISN'T ABOUT THE CHOCOLATE.`"]},{"type":"the teach","duration":"1 min","voice":"Lent is the season of forty days before Easter. Forty is the number the Bible keeps reaching for when someone is being tested: Noah's rain, Moses on the mountain, and Jesus fasting in the desert for forty days before he begins his work. Lent walks alongside those forty days. It opens with ash. A cross is traced on the forehead, often with words from the Bible's first pages, in the Douay-Rheims: \"dust thou art, and into dust thou shalt return.\" It sounds grim. Catholics hear it as honest: you won't be here forever, so what will you do with the time? Then come three old practices: prayer, fasting, and giving to the poor. Catholics fast on Ash Wednesday and Good Friday, and skip meat on the Fridays of Lent, because Friday is the day Jesus died. The rule doesn't count fish as meat. Hello, Friday fish fry. Many also give something up. Here's the idea underneath: give up something small, and every reach for it becomes a moment of noticing. The empty space where the chocolate was becomes a little room for prayer, or for someone else. And the forty days only make sense because of where they end: Easter morning.","screen":["`FORTY: NOAH, MOSES, THE DESERT.` · `DUST THOU ART.` · `PRAYER, FASTING, GIVING.` · `THE REACH BECOMES A REMINDER.`"]},{"type":"the practice","duration":"20 sec","voice":"Three breaths with the bell. Think of one small thing you reach for without thinking: your phone, a snack, a complaint. Picture yourself reaching for it and stopping, hand in the air. Stay in that pause for three slow breaths, and see what you notice there instead.","screen":[]},{"type":"the word","duration":"10 sec","voice":"Lent. Forty days before Easter, of prayer, fasting and giving, where less becomes a way of paying attention. Sixth word.","screen":["`LENT — your 6th word`."]},{"type":"the carry","duration":"10 sec","voice":"Your line: less, on purpose. The ash says the time is short; the empty space makes room. Day six, done.","screen":[]},{"type":"the close","duration":null,"voice":"","screen":["`DAY 6 COUNTS.` → `TOMORROW: THE THINGS YOUR FAMILY KEPT.`"]}]},"7":{"day":7,"title":"the things your family kept","word":"sacramentals","hook":"a medal on a keychain, a palm behind the crucifix, an abuela's altarcito: the Church's one word for all of it","length":"3:00","carry":"faith you can hold","segments":[{"type":"the bell","duration":null,"voice":"","screen":["`DAY SEVEN.`"]},{"type":"review","duration":"10 sec","voice":"Yesterday: Lent, less on purpose. Six words so far: the sign of the cross, the Eucharist, confession, the rosary, the saints, Lent. Today, the seventh.","screen":["`THE SIGN OF THE CROSS · THE EUCHARIST · CONFESSION · THE ROSARY · THE SAINTS · LENT`"]},{"type":"the hook","duration":"30 sec","voice":"A Saint Christopher medal on a keychain. A palm from Palm Sunday tucked behind the crucifix, drying out for a year. A bottle of holy water in the kitchen cabinet. An abuela's altarcito, with candles, a rosary and photos of the people she's lost. A Santo Niño, the child Jesus, watching over a Filipino living room. A medal blessed at a quinceañera Mass. Every family's version looks different. The Church has one word for all of it.","screen":["`A MEDAL. A PALM. HOLY WATER.` → `AN ALTARCITO. A SANTO NIÑO.` → `ONE WORD FOR ALL OF IT.`"]},{"type":"the teach","duration":"1 min","voice":"Sacramentals. The word is a cousin of sacrament. The seven sacraments, like baptism, confession and the Eucharist, are the big moments Catholics believe Christ himself gave the Church. Sacramentals are the smaller things the Church added around them: blessings, blessed objects, words and gestures. Look back at this week and you'll find you've been holding them all along. Holy water and the sign of the cross. The ashes. The rosary. A saint's medal. The Church is careful about what they are. They aren't magic, and they aren't lucky charms. They're reminders that help a person get ready for grace, the way a photo on the fridge reminds you to call. Around them grows what the Church calls popular piety: the customs each people builds, which it welcomes as long as they lead back to Christ. So a Filipino family getting up before dawn for nine Masses before Christmas, called Simbang Gabi. A Mexican family's altar for its dead in early November. An Irish grandmother who won't eat meat on any Friday of the year. An Italian town's feast for its patron saint. Different shapes, one faith. Whatever your family's version was, it was a way in.","screen":["`SACRAMENTS: THE BIG SEVEN.` · `SACRAMENTALS: THE SMALL SIGNS.` · `NOT MAGIC: REMINDERS.` · `DIFFERENT SHAPES, ONE FAITH.`"]},{"type":"the practice","duration":"20 sec","voice":"Three breaths with the bell. Bring to mind one object from your family's faith: a medal, a picture, a candle, a rosary in a drawer. On the first breath, see it. On the second, see whose it was. On the third, let it remind you of them.","screen":[]},{"type":"the word","duration":"10 sec","voice":"Sacramentals. Small blessed things and gestures: reminders that point to grace. Seventh word: a week's worth.","screen":["`SACRAMENTALS — your 7th word`."]},{"type":"the carry","duration":"10 sec","voice":"Your line: faith you can hold. That's week one: seven words, and you were already holding most of them. Tomorrow, your grandmother before dinner.","screen":[]},{"type":"the close","duration":null,"voice":"","screen":["`DAY 7 COUNTS.` → `TOMORROW: YOUR GRANDMOTHER BEFORE DINNER.`"]}]},"8":{"day":8,"title":"your grandmother before dinner","word":"grace","hook":"your grandmother before dinner; a gift you didn't earn","length":"6:15","carry":"you are already held","segments":[{"type":"the bell","duration":null,"voice":"","screen":["`DAY EIGHT.`"]},{"type":"review","duration":"15 sec","voice":"Yesterday: sacramentals, faith you can hold. Week one is behind you. Week two starts with the thing all those small signs point to. Day eight.","screen":["`FAITH YOU CAN HOLD.` → `WEEK TWO.`"]},{"type":"the hook","duration":"45 sec","voice":"The food is on the kitchen table, steam coming off the potatoes, and somebody's already reaching for the bread. Then a hand on the wrist. A grandmother, saying: wait. Grace first. Heads go down. A few quick words, a quick amen, and everybody eats. A kid at a table like that could grow up thinking grace means the little speech before dinner. It does. But the word is much bigger than the speech. It's the reason for the speech.","screen":["`A KITCHEN TABLE.` → `WAIT. GRACE FIRST.` → `THE WORD IS BIGGER THAN THE SPEECH.`"]},{"type":"the teach","duration":"2 min","voice":"Grace comes from the Latin gratia: a favor, a gift, something given freely, not paid for. It's the same root that gives English gratitude, and gratis, free of charge. The New Testament was written in Greek, and there the word is charis, which also means a gift freely given, and a kind of loveliness. So when Catholics talk about grace, they mean God's gift of himself, given before anybody earned it. The letter to the Ephesians says it plainly, in the Douay-Rheims Bible: \"For by grace you are saved through faith, and that not of yourselves, for it is the gift of God.\" Not of yourselves. That's the surprise at the heart of the word. You don't climb up to grace. It comes down to you. Catholics say it arrives in many ways: in baptism and the other sacraments, in prayer, in an ordinary Tuesday when a friend calls at exactly the right moment. Yesterday's small signs are there to get a person ready for it. And here's where the dinner table comes back in. The traditional Catholic grace before meals goes: \"Bless us, O Lord, and these thy gifts, which we are about to receive from thy bounty, through Christ our Lord. Amen.\" Listen to what it notices. Thy gifts. Thy bounty. Somebody grew the food, somebody cooked it, and before any of that, Catholics say, somebody gave the world to begin with. So saying grace isn't paying a fee before eating. It's noticing that you were given something.","screen":["`GRATIA — A FAVOR, A GIFT.` · `CHARIS — GIVEN FREELY.` · `NOT OF YOURSELVES.` · `THE TABLE IS A SMALL PICTURE.` · then, large: `A GIFT YOU DIDN'T EARN.`"]},{"type":"the practice","duration":"1 min 30","voice":"Here's the practice, and it's small on purpose. Think of one thing you have today that you didn't earn. The air, maybe. A person. A roof. Let it come into focus, and stay with it while you breathe slowly. If you pray, you can say thank you with each breath out. If you don't, just let yourself notice the gift.","screen":[]},{"type":"the word","duration":"20 sec","voice":"Grace. A favor, a gift freely given. Catholics say it comes first, before anyone earns it. Eighth word.","screen":["`GRACE — your 8th word`."]},{"type":"the carry","duration":"15 sec","voice":"Your line: you are already held. Grace means the gift came first. That's day eight.","screen":[]},{"type":"the close","duration":null,"voice":"","screen":["`DAY 8 COUNTS.` → `TOMORROW: A YOUNG WOMAN SAYS YES.`"]}]},"9":{"day":9,"title":"a young woman says yes","word":"Mary","hook":"a young woman says yes","length":"6:15","carry":"let it be","segments":[{"type":"the bell","duration":null,"voice":"","screen":["`DAY NINE.`"]},{"type":"review","duration":"15 sec","voice":"Yesterday: grace, a gift you didn't earn. Your line was you are already held. Today the word comes back, in an angel's mouth. Day nine.","screen":["`YOU ARE ALREADY HELD.`"]},{"type":"the hook","duration":"45 sec","voice":"A small town in Galilee called Nazareth. A young woman there is promised in marriage to a man named Joseph. Luke doesn't give her age, and he doesn't describe the house. An ordinary day, as far as we know. And then, Luke's Gospel says, the angel Gabriel comes in to her, telling her she's going to have a child, and that he'll be called the Son of the Most High. She isn't married yet. Today, a yes the whole Catholic story leans on, and the young woman who said it.","screen":["`A TOWN IN GALILEE.` → `AN ANGEL IN THE ROOM.` → `SHE ISN'T MARRIED YET.`"]},{"type":"the teach","duration":"2 min","voice":"Her name, in Hebrew, was Miriam, like the sister of Moses. We say Mary. And the first thing Gabriel says to her, after the greeting, is what the angels in Luke say again and again: \"Fear not, Mary, for thou hast found grace with God.\" Grace: yesterday's word, back again. Then she does something people skip over. She asks a question. How can this be? She isn't passive. She wants to understand. The angel answers, and then she gives the reply Catholics have treasured ever since. In the Douay-Rheims Bible: \"Behold the handmaid of the Lord; be it done to me according to thy word.\" In Latin, be it done is fiat, and Catholics still call her answer Mary's fiat. Her yes. Here's why it matters so much. Catholics say God didn't force anything on her. He asked, and waited, and the whole story of Jesus turns on a young woman saying yes. That's why they call her the Mother of God, because they believe her son is God, and why she gets the highest honor of any saint: honor, as day five said, not worship. Other Christians differ on how much to say about her. Many Protestants honor her as a faithful believer and a model of trust, and don't pray to her. And Luke's page is the same for all of them: a young woman, a question, and then yes.","screen":["`MIRIAM — FEAR NOT.` · `SHE ASKS A QUESTION.` · `FIAT — BE IT DONE.` · `THE MOTHER OF GOD.` · then, large: `BEHOLD THE HANDMAID OF THE LORD.`"]},{"type":"the practice","duration":"1 min 30","voice":"Here's the practice. Think of one thing in your life right now that you're resisting: something you can't change and keep pushing against. Name it. Notice how tightly you're holding it. Then, breath by breath, let your hands open a little on your lap. If you pray, you can borrow Mary's words: be it done to me.","screen":[]},{"type":"the word","duration":"20 sec","voice":"Mary. Miriam of Nazareth, the mother of Jesus, whose yes, her fiat, Catholics honor above every other saint. Ninth word.","screen":["`MARY — your 9th word`."]},{"type":"the carry","duration":"15 sec","voice":"Your line: let it be. But notice the order: first she asked her honest question. Day nine.","screen":[]},{"type":"the close","duration":null,"voice":"","screen":["`DAY 9 COUNTS.` → `TOMORROW: THE WORDS, LINE BY LINE, FROM LUKE.`"]}]},"10":{"day":10,"title":"the words, line by line, from Luke","word":"the Hail Mary","hook":"the words, line by line, from Luke","length":"6:15","carry":"a greeting, then a request","segments":[{"type":"the bell","duration":null,"voice":"","screen":["`DAY TEN.`"]},{"type":"review","duration":"15 sec","voice":"Yesterday: Mary, a question, and then a yes. Your line was let it be. Today, the words on the rosary's ten beads, and who said them first. Day ten.","screen":["`LET IT BE.`"]},{"type":"the hook","duration":"45 sec","voice":"At a wake, a room full of voices runs it together until it sounds like one long word. A kid in Catholic school rattles it off before anyone can blink. In football, a desperate pass thrown deep with no time left still gets called a Hail Mary. Said that fast, it's easy to miss where the words came from. Half the prayer isn't new at all. It's lifted, line by line, from the first chapter of Luke's Gospel. Today, slowly.","screen":["`ONE LONG WORD AT A WAKE.` → `A HAIL MARY PASS.` → `LIFTED FROM LUKE.`"]},{"type":"the teach","duration":"2 min","voice":"The first line belongs to an angel: yesterday's Gabriel, in the room in Nazareth. In the Douay-Rheims Bible, his first words are: \"Hail, full of grace, the Lord is with thee: blessed art thou among women.\" Hail is just an old hello. Full of grace is day eight's word, filled to the brim. The next line belongs to a relative. A little later in Luke, Mary visits her cousin Elizabeth, who's also pregnant, and Elizabeth cries out: \"Blessed art thou among women, and blessed is the fruit of thy womb.\" Over the centuries Christians put those two greetings side by side, an angel's and a cousin's, and added two names: Mary, right after the angel's hail, and Jesus at the end. That's the whole first half. Every word of it, apart from those two names, is from Luke. Then the prayer turns. The second half isn't scripture. It's the Church speaking, and it came into the prayer later: Holy Mary, Mother of God, pray for us sinners, now and at the hour of our death. Amen. Listen to the shape. The first half says hello. The second half asks for something, small and huge at once: pray for us. Now, and at the very end. Catholics aren't asking Mary to do what only God does. They're asking her to pray with them. On the rosary it comes ten times in each decade, a greeting, then a request, like a heartbeat. Say it fast and it's a blur. Say it slowly and you're standing in two rooms in Luke's Gospel.","screen":["`AN ANGEL'S HELLO.` · `A COUSIN'S BLESSING.` · `THEN THE CHURCH ASKS.` · `PRAY FOR US, NOW AND AT THE END.` · then, large: `A GREETING, THEN A REQUEST.`"]},{"type":"the practice","duration":"1 min 30","voice":"Here's the practice. Let your breath slow down. On the breath in, think of a greeting: hello to someone you love. On the breath out, think of a request: one thing you need right now. In, a greeting. Out, a request. If you pray the Hail Mary, you can say it once, as slowly as you've ever said it.","screen":[]},{"type":"the word","duration":"20 sec","voice":"The Hail Mary. An angel's hello and a cousin's blessing from Luke, then the Church's request: pray for us. Tenth word.","screen":["`THE HAIL MARY — your 10th word`."]},{"type":"the carry","duration":"15 sec","voice":"Your line: a greeting, then a request. Two rooms in Luke, ten times a decade. Day ten.","screen":[]},{"type":"the close","duration":null,"voice":"","screen":["`DAY 10 COUNTS.` → `TOMORROW: THE FAMILY WORD HE CHOSE.`"]}]},"11":{"day":11,"title":"the family word he chose","word":"the Our Father","hook":"the family word he chose","length":"6:15","carry":"not a stranger","segments":[{"type":"the bell","duration":null,"voice":"","screen":["`DAY ELEVEN.`"]},{"type":"review","duration":"15 sec","voice":"Yesterday: the Hail Mary, a greeting, then a request. Today, the prayer on the single beads between the tens. Day eleven.","screen":["`A GREETING, THEN A REQUEST.`"]},{"type":"the hook","duration":"45 sec","voice":"A boss gets the best email voice. A judge gets your honor. A king, in the old days, got your majesty, said from the knees. The more powerful someone is, the more careful and formal the words. In Luke's Gospel, a disciple asks Jesus: teach us to pray. How do you talk to the one who made the stars? Titles, you might expect. A long list of them. Instead he gives them a prayer that begins with a family word.","screen":["`YOUR HONOR. YOUR MAJESTY.` → `TEACH US TO PRAY.` → `A FAMILY WORD.`"]},{"type":"the teach","duration":"2 min","voice":"In Matthew's Gospel, in the Douay-Rheims Bible, it starts: \"Our Father who art in heaven, hallowed be thy name.\" Father. Behind the Greek of the Gospels, some scholars hear the Aramaic word abba. Mark's Gospel keeps that word untranslated, on the night before Jesus died, when he prays in the garden: \"Abba, Father, all things are possible to thee.\" Abba is the word a son or daughter uses. Not a title. A relationship. And then look at the word before it: our. Not my Father. Our. Read it closely and there isn't a single I or me in the whole prayer. Give us. Forgive us. Lead us. Deliver us. Someone praying it alone at night still prays in the plural, standing in a crowd they can't see. That's the family word doing its work twice: God is not a stranger, and neither is the person next to you. At Mass, the whole room prays it together, just before communion. For a long time Catholics prayed it in Latin: Pater noster, our Father. One difference shows up in other churches. Churches that learned the prayer from the King James Bible end with: for thine is the kingdom, the power and the glory. The King James prints that line in Matthew; the Douay-Rheims, translated from the Latin, ends at deliver us from evil, and so do Catholics. At Mass, the priest adds a short prayer after it, and then everyone says a version of that ending together. Same prayer, two old habits.","screen":["`ABBA — FATHER, AT HOME.` · `OUR, NOT MY.` · `NO I, NO ME.` · `PATER NOSTER.` · then, large: `NOT A STRANGER.`"]},{"type":"the practice","duration":"1 min 30","voice":"Here's the practice. Say just the first two words, slowly, out loud or silently: our Father. On our, think of someone you'd rather not include in that word, and include them anyway. On Father, notice how different it sounds from a title. If you pray the whole prayer, you can pray it once, very slowly.","screen":[]},{"type":"the word","duration":"20 sec","voice":"The Our Father. The prayer Jesus taught, opening with a family word, with no I or me in it anywhere. Eleventh word.","screen":["`THE OUR FATHER — your 11th word`."]},{"type":"the carry","duration":"15 sec","voice":"Your line: not a stranger. Not God, and not the person beside you. See you tomorrow.","screen":[]},{"type":"the close","duration":null,"voice":"","screen":["`DAY 11 COUNTS.` → `TOMORROW: WHY PEOPLE STAND UP WHEN THE BOOK COMES IN.`"]}]},"12":{"day":12,"title":"why people stand up when the book comes in","word":"the Gospel","hook":"why people stand up when the book comes in","length":"6:15","carry":"the story enters the room","segments":[{"type":"the bell","duration":null,"voice":"","screen":["`DAY TWELVE.`"]},{"type":"review","duration":"15 sec","voice":"Yesterday: the Our Father, a prayer with no I or me in it. Your line was not a stranger. Today, back inside the Mass. Day twelve.","screen":["`NOT A STRANGER.`"]},{"type":"the hook","duration":"45 sec","voice":"A guest at a Catholic Mass spends a lot of it half a second behind. Everyone else seems to know when to sit, when to stand, when to kneel, and the guest is watching the person in front. One moment in particular catches visitors off guard. People have been sitting quietly, listening to readings. Then music starts, often the word alleluia, and the whole room stands up at once. Someone at the front lifts a book up high. Why stand for a book? Day two was the table at the center of the Mass. Today, the first half: the story.","screen":["`SIT, STAND, KNEEL.` → `THE WHOLE ROOM STANDS.` → `FIRST, THE STORY.`"]},{"type":"the teach","duration":"2 min","voice":"First, the name. The word Mass is usually traced to the old Latin words at the end of the service, ite, missa est, roughly: go, you are sent. So the whole thing may be named for the moment people walk back out the door. Now, the first half. Catholics call it the Liturgy of the Word, and it's mostly listening. On a Sunday there's usually a reading from the Old Testament, then a psalm, often sung back and forth, then a reading from one of the letters of the apostles. People sit for those. Then comes the Gospel, a reading from one of the four books about the life of Jesus, and everyone stands. The English word Gospel goes back to an Old English phrase for good news, and it translates the Greek euangelion. Catholics say that when the Gospel is read, Christ himself is speaking to the people gathered there. So you stand the way you'd stand when someone important walks into the room. Right before it, people trace three little crosses with a thumb: on the forehead, on the lips, on the heart. It's day one's sign, shrunk down. One common way to explain it: may these words be in my mind, on my lips, in my heart. Standing for the reading isn't new, either. In the Bible itself, after the exile, the scribe Ezra opens the book of the law in front of the whole people, and when he opens it, they all stand up. Then everyone sits again, and a priest or deacon preaches: the homily. The story has entered the room.","screen":["`MASS — GO, YOU ARE SENT.` · `READINGS, A PSALM, THEN THE GOSPEL.` · `THREE SMALL CROSSES.` · `EZRA OPENS THE BOOK. THEY STAND.` · then, large: `THE STORY ENTERS THE ROOM.`"]},{"type":"the practice","duration":"1 min 30","voice":"Here's the practice. If you're sitting, stand up, just for this, the way the room stands for the Gospel. Touch your forehead, your lips, your heart. Then sit back down, breathe slowly, and let each of those three places get ready to listen. You don't have to say anything.","screen":[]},{"type":"the word","duration":"20 sec","voice":"The Gospel. The good news: the reading about the life of Jesus that everyone stands to hear, the high point of the Mass's first half. Twelfth word.","screen":["`THE GOSPEL — your 12th word`."]},{"type":"the carry","duration":"15 sec","voice":"Your line: the story enters the room. That's why everyone gets to their feet. Day twelve.","screen":[]},{"type":"the close","duration":null,"voice":"","screen":["`DAY 12 COUNTS.` → `TOMORROW: \"PEACE BE WITH YOU.\"`"]}]},"13":{"day":13,"title":"\"peace be with you\"","word":"the sign of peace","hook":"\"peace be with you\" — the handshake in the middle","length":"6:15","carry":"turn to the stranger","segments":[{"type":"the bell","duration":null,"voice":"","screen":["`DAY THIRTEEN.`"]},{"type":"review","duration":"15 sec","voice":"Yesterday: the Gospel, and the whole room standing for the story. Today, the moment the whole room turns around. Day thirteen.","screen":["`THE STORY ENTERS THE ROOM.`"]},{"type":"the hook","duration":"45 sec","voice":"The Mass is quiet and formal. Candles, a priest at the altar, everyone facing forward, nobody talking. And then, out of nowhere, everyone turns around. The person behind you sticks out a hand. The teenager next to you, who hasn't looked up from the floor, says peace be with you. A couple kisses. A little kid waves at the whole row. For a few moments, a room full of people who mostly don't know each other shakes hands. Then everyone turns back to the front as if nothing happened. Today, the handshake in the middle of the Mass.","screen":["`EVERYONE FACING FORWARD.` → `THEN EVERYONE TURNS AROUND.` → `THE HANDSHAKE IN THE MIDDLE.`"]},{"type":"the teach","duration":"2 min","voice":"After the story comes the table. Bread and wine are carried up to the altar, the priest prays over them, and the whole room says the Our Father, from day eleven. Then, just before people come forward for communion, the sign of peace. Where does it come from? Start with Easter night. In John's Gospel, the frightened friends of Jesus are hiding behind locked doors, and the risen Jesus stands among them. His first words, in the Douay-Rheims Bible: \"Peace be to you.\" Not a scolding for running away. Peace. In the Greek of John's Gospel it's eirene, a word that carries the old Hebrew greeting shalom, which means more than quiet. It means whole, complete, nothing missing. The first Christians greeted each other with more than a handshake: early Christian writers describe a kiss of peace shared in worship. There's a reason it sits right before communion in the Catholic Mass. Jesus once said that if you're bringing a gift to the altar and remember that someone has something against you, leave the gift there, go and make peace first, then come back. So before people go up to the table, they turn to whoever's beside them. Not their best friend. Whoever happens to be there. It's short on purpose. It isn't a chat. It's a sign: before we go any further, we're at peace with each other. The gesture varies from place to place: a bow, a nod, a handshake. The meaning holds.","screen":["`AFTER THE STORY, THE TABLE.` · `PEACE BE TO YOU — EASTER NIGHT.` · `SHALOM — WHOLE, NOTHING MISSING.` · `MAKE PEACE, THEN COME TO THE ALTAR.` · then, large: `TURN TO THE STRANGER.`"]},{"type":"the practice","duration":"1 min 30","voice":"Here's the practice. Bring three people to mind, one at a time. Someone you love. Someone you barely know: a neighbor, a coworker, the person who sold you coffee. Then someone you're not at peace with right now. With each one, picture turning to them and offering your hand. If you pray, you can wish each of them peace.","screen":[]},{"type":"the word","duration":"20 sec","voice":"The sign of peace. Turning to whoever is beside you, just before the table, to say peace be with you. Thirteenth word.","screen":["`THE SIGN OF PEACE — your 13th word`."]},{"type":"the carry","duration":"15 sec","voice":"Your line: turn to the stranger. Not your best friend: whoever happens to be beside you. Day thirteen, done.","screen":[]},{"type":"the close","duration":null,"voice":"","screen":["`DAY 13 COUNTS.` → `TOMORROW: THE OBLIGATION NOBODY EXPLAINS AS A GIFT.`"]}]},"14":{"day":14,"title":"the obligation nobody explains as a gift","word":"Sunday","hook":"the obligation nobody explains as a gift","length":"6:15","carry":"one day that isn't yours to fill","segments":[{"type":"the bell","duration":null,"voice":"","screen":["`DAY FOURTEEN.`"]},{"type":"review","duration":"20 sec","voice":"Yesterday: the sign of peace. Your line was turn to the stranger. And that's two weeks. This week: grace, Mary, the Hail Mary, the Our Father, the Gospel, the sign of peace. Day fourteen.","screen":["`TURN TO THE STRANGER.` → `TWO WEEKS.`"]},{"type":"the hook","duration":"45 sec","voice":"Being woken up too early. Scratchy church clothes. A parent at the bottom of the stairs saying: we're going, it's an obligation. That's how some people who grew up Catholic remember Sunday. Catholics are asked to go to Mass every Sunday, and to a kid it can sound like homework for the soul. Meanwhile the rest of the week is full. The phone that never stops. The laundry. The emails that arrive on Saturday night. What if the obligation was never meant as homework?","screen":["`SCRATCHY CHURCH CLOTHES.` → `HOMEWORK FOR THE SOUL?` → `SUNDAY, EXPLAINED AS A GIFT.`"]},{"type":"the teach","duration":"2 min","voice":"Start at the very beginning of the Bible. In Genesis, God makes the world in six days, and then, in the Douay-Rheims Bible, \"he rested on the seventh day from all his work which he had done.\" God rests. Not, the tradition says, because he's tired. In the story, the day of rest is built into the world like a beam into a house. For the Jewish people it became the Sabbath, the seventh day, kept from sunset Friday to sunset Saturday, and Jewish families keep it still. Then something happened on a different day. All four Gospels put the empty tomb on the first day of the week, the day after the Sabbath. Christians began gathering on that first day to break bread, and came to call it the Lord's day. In Latin, dies Domini. In Spanish it's still domingo, in Italian domenica. An early Christian named Justin Martyr described it for outsiders: on the day called Sunday, everyone gathers, the memoirs of the apostles or the writings of the prophets are read, bread and wine are brought and blessed, and there's a collection for widows and orphans. That's recognizably the Mass. So why call it an obligation? Catholics say it's the way the Church makes sure the gift actually gets opened. Left alone, a week fills every hour. The obligation holds one day open: for worship, for family, for rest, for a long meal. Sunday Mass can also be kept on Saturday evening, at what's called the vigil. The idea underneath is simple. One day a week isn't yours to fill.","screen":["`HE RESTED ON THE SEVENTH DAY.` · `THE FIRST DAY: THE EMPTY TOMB.` · `DIES DOMINI — THE LORD'S DAY.` · `JUSTIN: ON THE DAY CALLED SUNDAY.` · then, large: `THE GIFT GETS OPENED.`"]},{"type":"the practice","duration":"1 min 30","voice":"Here's the practice, and for this sit you have nothing to do. Nothing to fill. Put the phone face down, or out of reach. Let your hands rest open. Every time your mind reaches for a task, notice it, and set it down for later. If you pray, you can offer the time to God. If not, just let it be empty.","screen":[]},{"type":"the word","duration":"20 sec","voice":"Sunday. The Lord's day, the day of the empty tomb, the day the Church keeps open for worship and rest. Fourteenth word.","screen":["`SUNDAY — your 14th word`."]},{"type":"the carry","duration":"15 sec","voice":"Your line: one day that isn't yours to fill. That's what the obligation was protecting all along. Two weeks, done.","screen":[]},{"type":"the close","duration":null,"voice":"","screen":["`DAY 14 COUNTS.` → `TOMORROW: WHY THERE'S FIRE IN EVERY CHURCH.`"]}]},"15":{"day":15,"title":"why there's fire in every church","word":"candles","hook":"why there's fire in every church","length":"6:30","carry":"light a candle for someone","segments":[{"type":"the bell","duration":null,"voice":"","screen":["`DAY FIFTEEN.`"]},{"type":"review","duration":"15 sec","voice":"Yesterday: Sunday, the gift inside the obligation. Your line was one day that isn't yours to fill. Week three starts with fire. Day fifteen.","screen":["`ONE DAY THAT ISN'T YOURS TO FILL.` → `WEEK THREE.`"]},{"type":"the hook","duration":"45 sec","voice":"Every building has lights now. Switches, LEDs, the glow of exit signs. And yet step into a Catholic church at noon, in a city with perfect electricity, and there's still fire. Two candles on the altar. A tall one near the front. Rows of little flames in a side corner, flickering, somebody's coins in the box beside them. And one small red lamp that never seems to go out. It isn't for seeing. Nobody needs a candle to read anymore. So why is there fire in every church?","screen":["`PERFECT ELECTRICITY.` → `AND STILL, FIRE.` → `IT ISN'T FOR SEEING.`"]},{"type":"the teach","duration":"2 min","voice":"Start with what Jesus says in John's Gospel, in the Douay-Rheims Bible: \"I am the light of the world: he that followeth me, walketh not in darkness.\" So a candle in a church is a small sermon without words. Light in the dark. And every candle there has its own job. The candles on or near the altar are lit for Mass. The tall one is the Easter candle, the paschal candle. On the night before Easter, Catholics gather outside in the dark, light a new fire, and carry this one candle into a black church while someone sings, three times, light of Christ. Then the flame is passed from candle to candle through the whole crowd until the room is full of light. The same candle stands near the font at baptisms, and beside the coffin at funerals. The little red lamp is the one from day two: the sanctuary lamp, burning near the tabernacle as a sign that Christ is present there. And the rows in the corner? Votive candles, from the Latin votum, a vow or a prayer. Someone lights one for a sick mother, a job interview, a child who's struggling, a friend who died. The flame keeps burning after they walk out the door. Some people put it this way: the candle keeps praying after I leave. In Catholic teaching, the wax does nothing by itself. It's a way of saying: this person matters, and I'm not done hoping.","screen":["`I AM THE LIGHT OF THE WORLD.` · `THE EASTER CANDLE.` · `THE RED LAMP: HE IS HERE.` · `VOTIVE — A VOW, A PRAYER.` · then, large: `THIS PERSON MATTERS.`"]},{"type":"the practice","duration":"2 min","voice":"Here's the practice. If there's a candle nearby, you can light it. If not, just picture one. Think of one person who could use some light right now. See their face above the flame. You don't have to fix anything. Just hold them there while it burns, breathing slowly. If you pray, you can pray for them.","screen":[]},{"type":"the word","duration":"25 sec","voice":"Candles. Light in the dark: the Easter candle, the sanctuary lamp, and the votive flame that keeps praying after you leave. Fifteenth word.","screen":["`CANDLES — your 15th word`."]},{"type":"the carry","duration":"15 sec","voice":"Your line: light a candle for someone. The flame keeps hoping after you've gone. Day fifteen.","screen":[]},{"type":"the close","duration":null,"voice":"","screen":["`DAY 15 COUNTS.` → `TOMORROW: THE WOMEN WENT FIRST.`"]}]},"16":{"day":16,"title":"the women went first","word":"Easter","hook":"the women went first","length":"6:30","carry":"the stone is already moved","segments":[{"type":"the bell","duration":null,"voice":"","screen":["`DAY SIXTEEN.`"]},{"type":"review","duration":"15 sec","voice":"Yesterday: candles, and the Easter flame carried into a dark church. Your line was light a candle for someone. Today, the morning that flame is for. Day sixteen.","screen":["`LIGHT A CANDLE FOR SOMEONE.`"]},{"type":"the hook","duration":"45 sec","voice":"Very early on a Sunday morning, near Jerusalem. Jesus has been executed and buried. The men who followed him are nowhere in this scene. A few women are walking to a tomb cut in the rock, carrying spices to anoint a body. That's what you did for someone you loved. On the way, they realize they have a problem. A great stone has been rolled across the entrance. In Mark's Gospel, they ask each other: \"Who shall roll us back the stone from the door of the sepulchre?\" They go anyway. They don't have a plan. They just go.","screen":["`VERY EARLY. A SUNDAY.` → `A FEW WOMEN, WITH SPICES.` → `WHO WILL ROLL BACK THE STONE?`"]},{"type":"the teach","duration":"2 min","voice":"When they get there, the stone is already rolled away. The tomb is empty. In one telling, two men in shining clothes ask them why they're looking for the living among the dead, and say, in the Douay-Rheims Bible: \"He is not here, but is risen.\" Every Gospel tells it a little differently: how many women, how many angels, who sees what first. But all four agree on one thing: women were the first witnesses at the empty tomb. Some scholars point out that a woman's word counted for less in that world, which makes the detail stand out. And the men, one Gospel says, didn't believe them at first: the women's words seemed like idle tales. In another telling, Mary Magdalene stays behind at the tomb, crying, and meets the risen Jesus himself, and doesn't recognize him until he says her name. Then he sends her to tell the others. That's why Catholic tradition calls her the apostle to the apostles: the one sent to the ones who would be sent. Catholics believe the resurrection is the center of everything: that Jesus truly rose from the dead, and that death is not the end of the story. It's why, as day fourteen said, Christians gather on Sunday: every Sunday keeps a little of Easter. Easter itself isn't just one day. It's a season of fifty days, longer than Lent. In Spanish, Italian and French, the feast's name comes from Pascha, the Passover: Pascua, Pasqua, Pâques. And look at the order of that morning. The women worried about the stone the whole way there. By the time they arrived, it had already been moved.","screen":["`HE IS NOT HERE, BUT IS RISEN.` · `WOMEN WERE THE FIRST WITNESSES.` · `THE APOSTLE TO THE APOSTLES.` · `FIFTY DAYS, NOT ONE.` · then, large: `THE STONE IS ALREADY MOVED.`"]},{"type":"the practice","duration":"2 min","voice":"Here's the practice. Think of one stone you're worried about: something ahead that feels too heavy to move. A conversation, a bill, a diagnosis, a decision. Picture yourself walking toward it anyway, like the women did, without a plan. Breathe slowly as you walk. You don't have to solve it. Just keep walking. If you pray, you can bring it to God.","screen":[]},{"type":"the word","duration":"25 sec","voice":"Easter. The feast of the resurrection, the empty tomb, and the women who were sent to tell. Sixteenth word.","screen":["`EASTER — your 16th word`."]},{"type":"the carry","duration":"15 sec","voice":"Your line: the stone is already moved. The women only found out because they went anyway. Day sixteen.","screen":[]},{"type":"the close","duration":null,"voice":"","screen":["`DAY 16 COUNTS.` → `TOMORROW: THE RICH KID WHO GAVE HIS CLOTHES BACK.`"]}]},"17":{"day":17,"title":"the rich kid who gave his clothes back","word":"Francis","hook":"the rich kid who gave his clothes back","length":"6:30","carry":"lord, make me an instrument","segments":[{"type":"the bell","duration":null,"voice":"","screen":["`DAY SEVENTEEN.`"]},{"type":"review","duration":"15 sec","voice":"Yesterday: Easter, the women, and the stone already moved. Today, one of the saints from day five, up close. Day seventeen.","screen":["`THE STONE IS ALREADY MOVED.`"]},{"type":"the hook","duration":"45 sec","voice":"Italy, the Middle Ages. A town on a hill called Assisi. There's a young man there everyone knows: the son of a rich cloth merchant, good clothes, good parties, the one who pays for everybody's dinner. He dreams of being a knight. He goes off to a war with the next town, gets captured, spends a long stretch in a prison cell, and comes home sick. And something in him has changed. He starts giving things away. Money, cloth from his father's shop. His father is furious, and drags him in front of the bishop of Assisi, in the town square, to get his property back. What the son does next is why people still know his name.","screen":["`ASSISI, A TOWN ON A HILL.` → `THE RICH MERCHANT'S SON.` → `DRAGGED BEFORE THE BISHOP.`"]},{"type":"the teach","duration":"2 min","voice":"His name was Francesco. We say Francis. According to the stories written down by his early followers, he stood in front of the bishop and the whole town, took off his clothes, every piece, and handed them back to his father. And he said, in effect: until now I called you my father. From now on I'll say our Father, who art in heaven. The bishop covered him with his own cloak. Francis walked out of town with nothing. What came next is the part people forget. He didn't become a hermit. He went toward the people everyone avoided. The stories say that once, riding outside town, he met a man with leprosy, a disease that made people outcasts, and instead of riding past, he got down and kissed his hand. Later he wrote that what had seemed bitter to him was turned into sweetness. He gathered friends who lived poor, preached in the streets and cared for the sick. They became the Franciscans, who still carry his name. Near the end of his life he wrote a song, the Canticle of the Creatures, praising God for brother sun and sister moon, even sister death. Now today's line: lord, make me an instrument. It opens the prayer that bears his name, make me an instrument of your peace. Here's the honest part. As far as anyone can tell, Francis didn't write it. Its first known printing is in a small French magazine in 1912, with no author's name. His name was attached to it later. Francis's own words are in the Canticle.","screen":["`HE GAVE THE CLOTHES BACK.` · `TOWARD THE PEOPLE EVERYONE AVOIDED.` · `BROTHER SUN, SISTER MOON.` · `THE PRAYER HE PROBABLY DIDN'T WRITE.` · then, large: `MAKE ME AN INSTRUMENT.`"]},{"type":"the practice","duration":"2 min","voice":"Here's the practice. Get comfortable and let your breath slow down. Think of one thing you're holding on to a little too tightly. It doesn't have to be big. A grudge counts. With each breath out, imagine loosening your grip on it, the way Francis handed his clothes back. If you pray, you can say the line: lord, make me an instrument.","screen":[]},{"type":"the word","duration":"25 sec","voice":"Francis. Francis of Assisi, the rich kid who gave it all back and went toward the outcasts. Seventeenth word.","screen":["`FRANCIS — your 17th word`."]},{"type":"the carry","duration":"15 sec","voice":"Your line: lord, make me an instrument. The prayer borrowed his name; the life was his own. See you tomorrow.","screen":[]},{"type":"the close","duration":null,"voice":"","screen":["`DAY 17 COUNTS.` → `TOMORROW: THE WORD POPE FRANCIS KEPT SAYING.`"]}]},"18":{"day":18,"title":"the word Pope Francis kept saying","word":"mercy","hook":"the word Pope Francis kept saying","length":"6:30","carry":"start with yourself","segments":[{"type":"the bell","duration":null,"voice":"","screen":["`DAY EIGHTEEN.`"]},{"type":"review","duration":"15 sec","voice":"Yesterday: Francis of Assisi, the rich kid who gave it all back. Your line was lord, make me an instrument. Today, the pope who took his name. Day eighteen.","screen":["`MAKE ME AN INSTRUMENT.`"]},{"type":"the hook","duration":"45 sec","voice":"In 2013, a man from Buenos Aires who used to ride the bus to work became pope and took a name no pope had ever used: Francis, after the saint from Assisi you met yesterday. One word kept coming up, in sermons, in interviews, off the cuff to crowds. He called a whole year after it. He titled a book after it. Asked once, early on, to describe himself, he said, in effect: a sinner the Lord has looked upon. Pope Francis led the Church until his death in 2025. Today, his word: mercy.","screen":["`A POPE WHO RODE THE BUS.` → `ONE WORD KEPT COMING UP.` → `A SINNER THE LORD LOOKED UPON.`"]},{"type":"the teach","duration":"2 min","voice":"The Latin word is misericordia. Inside it are two smaller words: miser, wretched, in trouble, and cor, the heart. Mercy is a heart that goes out to someone in misery. Not a verdict. A heart. The Bible's Hebrew has a word for it that comes from the word for a mother's womb, the kind of love you feel for a child you carried. Luke's Gospel puts it at the center of what Jesus asks, in the Douay-Rheims Bible: \"Be ye therefore merciful, as your Father also is merciful.\" Pope Francis took his motto from an old sermon by Bede, an English monk, about the day Jesus called Matthew, a tax collector everyone despised. Jesus looked at him, Bede says, with mercy, and chose him. In Latin: miserando atque eligendo. By having mercy, and by choosing. Francis said that was his own story. That's where today's line comes from. Francis kept saying that people who can't show mercy have often never let themselves receive it. You can't give away what you've never been handed. So the first step is to accept it: to let God, or someone, look at you with mercy and choose you anyway, the way Jesus looked at Matthew. For some people, the hardest one to show mercy to is themselves. Then it flows outward: forgiving, feeding, visiting, going toward people in trouble. Catholics call these the works of mercy. Francis of Assisi kissing the leper's hand was the same heart. And confession, from day three, is the Church saying it out loud: you're forgiven.","screen":["`MISERICORDIA — A HEART FOR THE WRETCHED.` · `BE YE THEREFORE MERCIFUL.` · `BY HAVING MERCY, AND BY CHOOSING.` · `YOU CAN'T GIVE WHAT YOU WERE NEVER HANDED.` · then, large: `START WITH YOURSELF.`"]},{"type":"the practice","duration":"2 min","voice":"Here's the practice. Bring to mind one thing you haven't quite forgiven yourself for, if there is one. Something small is fine. Picture someone who loves you looking at it, and at you, without flinching. Not excusing it. Just looking at you with a heart that goes out to you. Breathe slowly, and let yourself be looked at like that. If you pray, you can let that look be God's.","screen":[]},{"type":"the word","duration":"25 sec","voice":"Mercy. Misericordia: a heart that goes out to someone in trouble, received first, then given away. Eighteenth word.","screen":["`MERCY — your 18th word`."]},{"type":"the carry","duration":"15 sec","voice":"Your line: start with yourself. Mercy received is the only kind you can pass on. Day eighteen.","screen":[]},{"type":"the close","duration":null,"voice":"","screen":["`DAY 18 COUNTS.` → `TOMORROW: ONE, HOLY, CATHOLIC.`"]}]},"19":{"day":19,"title":"one, holy, catholic","word":"the Church","hook":"one, holy, catholic — the small-c word means universal","length":"6:30","carry":"everywhere at once","segments":[{"type":"the bell","duration":null,"voice":"","screen":["`DAY NINETEEN.`"]},{"type":"review","duration":"15 sec","voice":"Yesterday: mercy, a heart that goes out to someone in trouble. Your line was start with yourself. Today, the small word in the Church's own name. Day nineteen.","screen":["`START WITH YOURSELF.`"]},{"type":"the hook","duration":"45 sec","voice":"A Catholic Mass in Lagos. In Manila. In a tiny village in Poland. In a strip-mall parish in Texas. The language is different. The music is wildly different: drums, organs, guitars, choirs in five-part harmony. But a regular would know where they were almost at once. The readings, the peace, the table. The same shape, in a language you don't speak. Every Sunday, Catholics say a creed that calls the Church one, holy, catholic and apostolic. Today, the small-c word in that line, and what it actually means.","screen":["`LAGOS, MANILA, POLAND, TEXAS.` → `THE SAME SHAPE.` → `ONE, HOLY, CATHOLIC.`"]},{"type":"the teach","duration":"2 min","voice":"Start with church. In the New Testament the Greek word is ekklesia, from a verb meaning to call out. It meant an assembly, people called out of their houses to gather. So the Church, first of all, isn't a building. It's the people who were called. Now the small-c word. Catholic comes from the Greek katholikos, from kath' holou: according to the whole. Universal. Early in Christian history, a bishop named Ignatius of Antioch was being taken to Rome under guard, to be executed. On the way he wrote letters to the churches. In one of them he writes that wherever Jesus Christ is, there is the catholic Church. He meant the whole Church, everywhere, gathered around Christ. The creed said at Sunday Mass puts four words side by side. One: many countries, one faith. Holy: set apart for God, even though, as day five said, its people are far from perfect. Catholic: for everyone, everywhere. Apostolic: going back to the apostles. Catholics believe their Church is one family under the pope, the bishop of Rome, successor of Peter. And that family is more varied than it looks. Alongside the Latin Church there are Eastern Catholic Churches, with their own liturgies and traditions, united with Rome. Other Christians say these creed words too. Orthodox Christians say catholic and mean the Orthodox Church. In Protestant churches that say the creed, it usually means the whole Church of Christ across every denomination. For Catholics, Ignatius's line still sets the size of the word: wherever Christ is.","screen":["`EKKLESIA — THE ONES CALLED OUT.` · `KATHOLIKOS — ACCORDING TO THE WHOLE.` · `IGNATIUS, ON THE ROAD TO ROME.` · `ONE, HOLY, CATHOLIC, APOSTOLIC.` · then, large: `EVERYWHERE AT ONCE.`"]},{"type":"the practice","duration":"2 min","voice":"Here's the practice. Picture the globe, slowly turning. Somewhere it's morning, and people are walking into a church. Somewhere it's midnight, and someone is praying alone by a candle. Somewhere a baby is being baptized, and somewhere a funeral is ending. Let the picture be as big as it wants to be. You're one person on it, breathing. If you pray, you can pray for all of them at once.","screen":[]},{"type":"the word","duration":"25 sec","voice":"The Church. The ekklesia, the people called out, and catholic, meaning according to the whole: universal. Nineteenth word.","screen":["`THE CHURCH — your 19th word`."]},{"type":"the carry","duration":"15 sec","voice":"Your line: everywhere at once. Lagos, Manila, Texas, and wherever you're sitting. Day nineteen, done.","screen":[]},{"type":"the close","duration":null,"voice":"","screen":["`DAY 19 COUNTS.` → `TOMORROW: THE SAINT WHO WROTE ABOUT PRAYER LIKE A FRIENDSHIP.`"]}]},"20":{"day":20,"title":"the saint who wrote about prayer like a friendship","word":"Teresa of Ávila","hook":"the saint who wrote about prayer like a friendship","length":"6:30","carry":"talk to him like a friend","segments":[{"type":"the bell","duration":null,"voice":"","screen":["`DAY TWENTY.`"]},{"type":"review","duration":"15 sec","voice":"Yesterday: the Church, the ones called out, and catholic, meaning universal. Your line was everywhere at once. Today, one woman in one walled Spanish city. Day twenty.","screen":["`EVERYWHERE AT ONCE.`"]},{"type":"the hook","duration":"45 sec","voice":"Sit down to pray, or just to be quiet, and within a minute the mind has gone to lunch, or an argument from last week, or whether the car is locked. A woman in Spain, long ago, admitted in writing that for years prayer went like that for her. She would listen for the clock to strike, wishing the hour were over. She was a nun, and prayer was her whole job. Later the Church would call her a teacher of prayer for everyone.","screen":["`SIT DOWN TO PRAY.` → `WITHIN A MINUTE: LUNCH.` → `SHE LISTENED FOR THE CLOCK.`"]},{"type":"the teach","duration":"2 min","voice":"Her name was Teresa, from the walled city of Ávila in Spain. She grew up in a big, lively family, and as a girl, by her own account, she cared more for books of knights and adventure, and for nice clothes, than for holiness. As a young woman she entered a Carmelite convent. And for years, she says in her autobiography, prayer was a struggle. Distracted, dry, going through the motions. Then, after a long time, something changed, and the way she described prayer afterward is the reason she's still read. Prayer, as she understands it, is nothing else than friendship: spending time often, alone, with the one we know loves us. Not a performance. Not a speech. Time with a friend. She wrote the way she talked: funny, blunt, full of kitchen pictures. She said God walks among the pots and pans. In another book, she pictures the soul as a castle made of a single diamond, with many rooms, and God living in the center. Prayer is the door you go in by. She also got a lot done. She founded new convents across Spain, traveling on bad roads in rough carts, arguing with bishops, and working with a young friar we know as John of the Cross. There's a story people love to tell: that after a cart tipped her into a muddy river, she complained to God, if this is how you treat your friends, no wonder you have so few. Nobody knows whether she really said it. But it sounds exactly like someone who talked to God like a friend. Long after her death, the Church named her a Doctor of the Church: a teacher for everyone.","screen":["`ÁVILA. BOOKS OF KNIGHTS.` · `YEARS OF DRY PRAYER.` · `PRAYER IS FRIENDSHIP.` · `A CASTLE WITH MANY ROOMS.` · then, large: `TALK TO HIM LIKE A FRIEND.`"]},{"type":"the practice","duration":"2 min","voice":"Here's the practice, with no speech to give. Imagine a friend sitting across from you, one who knows you well and likes you anyway. Tell them, silently, one plain thing about your day. Not the polished version. Then just sit together without talking. When your mind wanders, and it will, come back the way you'd turn back to a friend after a distraction. If you pray, you can make that friend God.","screen":[]},{"type":"the word","duration":"25 sec","voice":"Teresa of Ávila. The Carmelite nun who struggled with prayer for years, then described it as friendship with the one who loves us. Twentieth word.","screen":["`TERESA OF ÁVILA — your 20th word`."]},{"type":"the carry","duration":"15 sec","voice":"Your line: talk to him like a friend. No speech, no polish: just time together. Day twenty.","screen":[]},{"type":"the close","duration":null,"voice":"","screen":["`DAY 20 COUNTS.` → `TOMORROW: THE MOUNTAIN AHEAD.`"]}]},"21":{"day":21,"title":"the mountain ahead","word":"the Psalms","hook":"the mountain ahead; the prayers Jesus himself prayed","length":"6:30","carry":"150 doors","segments":[{"type":"the bell","duration":null,"voice":"","screen":["`DAY TWENTY-ONE.`"]},{"type":"review","duration":"15 sec","voice":"Yesterday: Teresa of Ávila, and prayer as friendship. Your line was talk to him like a friend. Today is the last day of camp one. Day twenty-one.","screen":["`TALK TO HIM LIKE A FRIEND.` → `THE LAST DAY OF CAMP ONE.`"]},{"type":"the hook","duration":"50 sec","voice":"Look back down the hill for a second. Three weeks ago you started with the sign of the cross. Then the Eucharist. Confession. The rosary. The saints. Lent. Sacramentals. Grace. Mary. The Hail Mary. The Our Father. The Gospel. The sign of peace. Sunday. Candles. Easter. Francis. Mercy. The Church. Teresa. Twenty words: the words a Catholic hears every week. Now look up. There's a mountain ahead, and it's made of stories. But first, one more word, and it's the prayer book Jesus himself grew up praying.","screen":["`TWENTY WORDS, DOWN THE HILL.` → `THE WORDS OF EVERY WEEK.` → `A MOUNTAIN MADE OF STORIES.`"]},{"type":"the teach","duration":"2 min","voice":"The Psalms are a book of one hundred and fifty songs and prayers in the middle of the Bible. The Hebrew name is Tehillim: praises. Our word psalm comes from Greek, a song sung to a plucked string. They were Israel's prayer book and hymnal, and Jesus, a Jewish boy in Nazareth, likely grew up hearing them. Here's what surprises people: they aren't all nice. Some are joyful. Some are furious. Some are people in the dark asking God where he's gone. On the cross, in Matthew's Gospel, Jesus cries out the first line of one of those, in the Douay-Rheims Bible: \"My God, my God, why hast thou forsaken me?\" That's a psalm. In that hour, he prays in words that were already old. And in another Gospel, his last words are from a psalm too: \"Father, into thy hands I commend my spirit.\" One detail, if you ever look them up in an older Catholic Bible: the numbers are often off by one. The Douay-Rheims follows the ancient Greek and Latin count, so the psalm Jesus cried on the cross is Psalm 21 there, and Psalm 22 in Bibles that follow the Hebrew. Same words, different count. The Church still prays them every day. At every Mass there's a psalm between the readings, the one from day twelve, sung back and forth. Priests, monks and nuns pray the psalms through the day in what's called the Liturgy of the Hours. And the rosary from day four, the poor man's psalter, grew up in their shadow. One hundred and fifty psalms, one for nearly every feeling a person can have. One hundred and fifty doors.","screen":["`TEHILLIM — PRAISES.` · `NOT ALL OF THEM NICE.` · `MY GOD, WHY HAST THOU FORSAKEN ME?` · `THE NUMBERS ARE OFF BY ONE.` · then, large: `150 DOORS.`"]},{"type":"the practice","duration":"2 min","voice":"Here's the practice for the end of camp one. Ask yourself what you're actually feeling today, honestly. Glad. Tired. Angry. Lost. Grateful. Whatever it is, the Psalms have a door for it, and you don't have to clean it up first. Name the feeling in one word. Then breathe with it, not against it. If you pray, you can hand God that one word, just as it is.","screen":[]},{"type":"the word","duration":"25 sec","voice":"The Psalms. One hundred and fifty praises, laments and songs, the prayer book Jesus himself prayed. Twenty-first word, and the last of camp one.","screen":["`THE PSALMS — your 21st word` → the strand complete → `CAMP 1. FIRST WORDS. DONE.`"]},{"type":"the carry","duration":"15 sec","voice":"Your line: 150 doors. Tomorrow, camp two: the stories, starting with two pregnant women. Day twenty-one.","screen":[]},{"type":"the close","duration":null,"voice":"","screen":["`DAY 21 COUNTS.` → `TOMORROW: TWO PREGNANT WOMEN (LUKE 1:39–56).`"]}]}},"HINDUISM":{"1":{"day":1,"title":"the one at every doorway","word":"Ganesha","hook":"an elephant-headed figure on the dashboard, by the door, at the top of the page","length":"3:00","carry":"clear the road, then begin","segments":[{"type":"the bell","duration":null,"voice":"","screen":["`DAY ONE.`"]},{"type":"the hook","duration":"25 sec","voice":"Every Hindu beginning starts with him, so this one does too. A small statue on a taxi dashboard. A picture above a shop counter. His name printed at the top of a wedding invitation, before anyone else's. Elephant head, round belly, one tusk broken. He sits wherever things start, and that's the clue to who he is.","screen":["`EVERY BEGINNING STARTS WITH HIM.` → `DASHBOARDS. DOORWAYS. INVITATIONS.`"]},{"type":"the teach","duration":"1 min","voice":"Start with the name. Ganesha is gana plus isha: lord of the ganas, the attendants of the god Shiva. But the title that explains the doorways is Vighnaharta. Vighna, an obstacle; harta, one who removes. The remover of obstacles. That's why he's honored first: before a ceremony, a wedding, a new shop or an exam. Some people write shri ganeshaya namah at the top of a page before they begin: namah means a bow. There's a popular way of reading his body: big ears, listen more than you talk; small eyes, focus; a big belly, take in what life serves, good and hard. And the broken tusk: tradition says he wrote down the Mahabharata as the sage Vyasa dictated it, and one story says that when his pen gave out, he snapped off his own tusk and kept writing.","screen":["`LORD OF THE GANAS.` · `VIGHNAHARTA — REMOVER OF OBSTACLES.` · `SHRI GANESHAYA NAMAH.` · `THE BROKEN TUSK.`"]},{"type":"the practice","duration":"15 sec","voice":"One breath with the bell. Rest a hand on something that marks a start: a door, a laptop, a notebook. Name, once, what's in the way of the next thing you're beginning. Then breathe out and lift your hand.","screen":[]},{"type":"the word","duration":"10 sec","voice":"Ganesha. Lord of the ganas, remover of obstacles, the one at the door. First word on your strand, and the first name.","screen":["`GANESHA — your 1st word` → clicks onto an empty word-strand."]},{"type":"the carry","duration":"10 sec","voice":"Your line: clear the road, then begin. It's why his name comes first on so many invitations, and why he came first here. See you tomorrow.","screen":[]},{"type":"the close","duration":null,"voice":"","screen":["`DAY 1 COUNTS.` → `TOMORROW: THE SOUND THE UNIVERSE HUMS.`"]}]},"2":{"day":2,"title":"the sound the universe hums","word":"om","hook":"the sound a room makes together; it's three sounds and a silence","length":"3:00","carry":"one sound, before words","segments":[{"type":"the bell","duration":null,"voice":"","screen":["`DAY TWO.`"]},{"type":"review","duration":"10 sec","voice":"Yesterday: Ganesha, the one who goes first. Today, the sound that opens almost every Hindu prayer.","screen":["`GANESHA ✓` → `DAY 2.`"]},{"type":"the hook","duration":"20 sec","voice":"Om. A yoga class ends with it, and a lot of Hindu prayers begin with it. Most people treat it as one long hum. One of the oldest Hindu texts hears four parts in it: three sounds, and a fourth that isn't a sound at all. First, a guess.","screen":["`OM.` → `ONE HUM?` → `FOUR PARTS.`"]},{"type":"the teach","duration":"1 min","voice":"Say it slowly and you can feel three sounds. A, with the mouth open. U, as the lips round. M, as they close. That's why it's sometimes written aum. An old text of just twelve verses starts by saying om is all of this: past, present, future, and whatever lies beyond time. Then it maps the sounds onto your day. A is waking, the world you can see and touch. U is dreaming, the world made of the mind. M is deep, dreamless sleep, where nothing is wanted. The fourth part has no sound. It's the silence after the m runs out, and the text calls it simply the fourth: the self, at peace. So om isn't a sign-off. It's a map of a whole day in one breath, with the quiet underneath it. The Bhagavad Gita says acts of offering, giving and discipline begin with it.","screen":["`A — WAKING.` · `U — DREAMING.` · `M — DEEP SLEEP.` · `THE FOURTH — THE SILENCE.`"]},{"type":"the practice","duration":"20 sec","voice":"One breath with the bell. Breathe in. Let it out as the sound, aloud or under your breath: a, u, m. Let the m run out on its own, then stay a second in the quiet after it. That quiet is the fourth part.","screen":[]},{"type":"the word","duration":"10 sec","voice":"Om. A, u and m for waking, dreaming and deep sleep, and the silence after it for the fourth. Second word on your strand.","screen":["`OM — your 2nd word` → clicks onto the strand beside namaste."]},{"type":"the carry","duration":"10 sec","voice":"Your line: one sound, before words. Not a task. Something to notice the next time a room goes quiet before it begins. Day two, done.","screen":[]},{"type":"the close","duration":null,"voice":"","screen":["`DAY 2 COUNTS.` → `TOMORROW: THE PRAYER YOU KNOW BY HEART.`"]}]},"3":{"day":3,"title":"the prayer you know by heart","word":"Gayatri","hook":"the prayer you can say start to finish, and what it asks for","length":"3:00","carry":"a prayer for a clear mind","segments":[{"type":"the bell","duration":null,"voice":"","screen":["`DAY THREE.`"]},{"type":"review","duration":"10 sec","voice":"Yesterday: om, three sounds and a silence. Today it opens a prayer you might already know by heart.","screen":["`OM.` → `DAY 3.`"]},{"type":"the hook","duration":"20 sec","voice":"Om bhur bhuvah svah. If you grew up in a Hindu home, there's a good chance you can say the rest without thinking. Maybe someone said it every morning. Maybe it played at every puja. Now the hard part: what does it mean? Most people go quiet. Today, line by line. First, a guess.","screen":["`OM BHUR BHUVAH SVAH…` → `YOU CAN SAY IT.` → `WHAT DOES IT MEAN?`"]},{"type":"the teach","duration":"1 min","voice":"It's called the Gayatri mantra, and it's one of the oldest verses Hindus still say every day. Take it a line at a time. Om: the sound from yesterday. Bhur bhuvah svah: earth, sky, and everything beyond: the whole world. Tat savitur varenyam: that bright, worthy light of the sun. Bhargo devasya dhimahi: we meditate on that divine radiance. Dhiyo yo nah prachodayat: may it wake up and guide our minds. Notice what it asks for. Not money. Not luck. Not to win. A clear mind. That's what a mantra is: the word comes from man, to think, and tra, a tool. A tool for the mind, said on purpose. Many families say the Gayatri at dawn, as the light comes up, and some say it a set number of times on beads. Some traditions teach it at a coming-of-age ceremony; in many homes, everyone simply says it. If you grew up saying it, you weren't doing it wrong. Once you know the lines, it stops being a sound you repeat and becomes a request you mean.","screen":["`OM — THE SOUND FROM YESTERDAY.` · `EARTH, SKY, AND BEYOND.` · `MAY IT WAKE UP OUR MINDS.` · `A TOOL FOR THE MIND.`"]},{"type":"the practice","duration":"20 sec","voice":"Three breaths with the bell. Breathe in. As you breathe out, say the Gayatri once, slowly, out loud or in your head, or just its last line: dhiyo yo nah prachodayat, may it wake up and guide our minds. This time, mean the words.","screen":[]},{"type":"the word","duration":"10 sec","voice":"Gayatri. The morning prayer for a clear mind, and a mantra: a tool for the mind. Third word on your strand.","screen":["`GAYATRI — your 3rd word`."]},{"type":"the carry","duration":"10 sec","voice":"Your line: a prayer for a clear mind. Next time you hear it, you'll know what's being asked. Day three.","screen":[]},{"type":"the close","duration":null,"voice":"","screen":["`DAY 3 COUNTS.` → `TOMORROW: THE OLDEST HELLO.`"]}]},"4":{"day":4,"title":"the oldest hello","word":"namaste","hook":"you've said it after yoga class; the words inside say more than hello","length":"3:00","carry":"the light in me sees the light in you","segments":[{"type":"the bell","duration":null,"voice":"","screen":["`DAY FOUR.`"]},{"type":"review","duration":"10 sec","voice":"Yesterday: the Gayatri, a prayer for a clear mind. Today, a word you've said a thousand times as hello.","screen":["`A PRAYER FOR A CLEAR MIND.` → `DAY 4.`"]},{"type":"the hook","duration":"25 sec","voice":"You've said it at the end of a yoga class, and you've probably heard it means the light in me sees the light in you. The word itself says something much simpler. The longer meaning is a reading of it, and that reading comes from somewhere specific. First, a guess.","screen":["`NAMASTE.` → `WHAT IT SAYS.` → `WHAT IT'S READ AS.`"]},{"type":"the teach","duration":"1 min 10","voice":"Namaste is Sanskrit, and it splits in two. Namas: a bow. Te: to you. Word for word, I bow to you. That's the whole dictionary meaning. So where does the light come from? From a reading of the word. One of the oldest Hindu texts describes a light that shines above the heavens, above everything, and then says that same light is the light inside a person. Out there and in here: one light. Put that next to a bow and you get the popular line: the light in me sees the light in you. Not a translation. An interpretation, with a real source behind it. What the reading changes is who you're bowing to. Not someone's job, mood or résumé, but whatever in them is the same as what's in you. And nobody has to earn it first. In this reading, it's already there. The same bow goes further at home. When a kid touches a grandparent's feet at the door, that's pranam, from pra, forward, and nam, to bend: the same root as namaste. And the elder's hand comes down on the child's head: a blessing, given back. Respect goes one way, a blessing comes the other.","screen":["`NAMAS — A BOW.` · `TE — TO YOU.` · `THE SAME LIGHT, INSIDE A PERSON.` · `A BLESSING, GIVEN BACK.`"]},{"type":"the practice","duration":"20 sec","voice":"Three breaths with the bell. Palms together if you like, or just sit up straight. Breathe in. As you breathe out, tip your head slightly forward: the bow the word is named for. Nothing to feel, nothing to get right. Just the gesture, done once, on purpose.","screen":[]},{"type":"the word","duration":"10 sec","voice":"Namaste. Word for word, I bow to you. Read deeper, the light in me sees the light in you. Fourth word on your strand.","screen":["`NAMASTE — your 4th word`."]},{"type":"the carry","duration":"10 sec","voice":"Your line: the light in me sees the light in you. Not a task. Just a way to look at the next person you meet. That's day four.","screen":[]},{"type":"the close","duration":null,"voice":"","screen":["`DAY 4 COUNTS.` → `TOMORROW: LIGHT PASSED HAND TO HAND.`"]}]},"5":{"day":5,"title":"light passed hand to hand","word":"arti","hook":"a flame on a plate, moved in circles, then carried around the room","length":"3:00","carry":"light passed hand to hand","segments":[{"type":"the bell","duration":null,"voice":"","screen":["`DAY FIVE.`"]},{"type":"review","duration":"10 sec","voice":"Yesterday: namaste, I bow to you, and the bow that touches an elder's feet. Today, the flame that comes around on a plate.","screen":["`I BOW TO YOU.` → `DAY 5.`"]},{"type":"the hook","duration":"50 sec","voice":"Evening at a temple. Or the steps down to a river, as dusk comes in. Or a living room, when the lamp comes out. A small flame sits on a metal plate, and someone moves it in slow circles in front of the shrine. A bell rings. People sing. Then the plate is carried around the room, and one by one, people pass their hands over the flame and bring their palms to their eyes and forehead. It takes a few minutes, start to finish. Today's word is that flame, and what happens when it leaves the shrine.","screen":["`A FLAME ON A PLATE.` → `SLOW CIRCLES. A BELL.` → `THEN IT COMES TO THE PEOPLE.`"]},{"type":"the teach","duration":"1 min 5","voice":"Arti. The waving of a lamp; the offering of light. After the flowers and food are offered, the lamp is circled before the image of God, and a song is sung. The best known begins: Om jai jagadish hare. Victory to you, lord of the world. An old Hindu text has a line that helps with what the lamp might mean: the sun doesn't shine there, nor the moon and stars, let alone this little fire; everything shines after that one shining. So a small flame held up before God is light offered back to where light comes from. Then the flame leaves the shrine and goes out to the people. Each person draws its warmth toward their face. The same small fire touches every face in the room, and nobody gets more of it. Often there's one more step: prasad. Food or sweets offered before God, then handed out. The sweet you got after temple as a kid was that: an offering, given back as a blessing.","screen":["`ARTI — THE OFFERING OF LIGHT` · `EVERYTHING SHINES AFTER THAT ONE` · `THE SAME FIRE TOUCHES EVERY FACE` · `PRASAD — GIVEN BACK AS A BLESSING`"]},{"type":"the practice","duration":"25 sec","voice":"Three breaths with the bell. If there's a candle or a lamp near you, look at it; any light in the room will do. Cup your hands toward it, as if gathering warmth, and bring them to your eyes and forehead. Then bring one person to mind and turn your cupped hands toward them, as if passing the plate.","screen":[]},{"type":"the word","duration":"10 sec","voice":"Arti. The offering of light, and then the light shared with everyone. Fifth word.","screen":["`ARTI — your 5th word`."]},{"type":"the carry","duration":"10 sec","voice":"Your line: light passed hand to hand. Next time the plate comes around, you'll know what you're doing. Day five.","screen":[]},{"type":"the close","duration":null,"voice":"","screen":["`DAY 5 COUNTS.` → `TOMORROW: WHY SO MANY GODS?.`"]}]},"6":{"day":6,"title":"why so many gods?","word":"ishta devata","hook":"why there are so many gods, and which one is yours","length":"3:00","carry":"one truth, many names","segments":[{"type":"the bell","duration":null,"voice":"","screen":["`DAY SIX.`"]},{"type":"review","duration":"10 sec","voice":"Yesterday: arti, light passed hand to hand. Today, the question every Hindu kid gets asked at school.","screen":["`LIGHT PASSED HAND TO HAND.` → `DAY 6.`"]},{"type":"the hook","duration":"25 sec","voice":"Someone at school or work asks it, half curious, half teasing: how many gods do you have? Ganesha on the dashboard, Krishna in the calendar, Durga at Navratri, Lakshmi at Diwali, a whole shelf at your grandparents' place. It's a fair question, and most people never got a good answer to give. Here's one. First, a guess.","screen":["`HOW MANY GODS DO YOU HAVE?` → `A FAIR QUESTION.` → `HERE'S AN ANSWER.`"]},{"type":"the teach","duration":"1 min","voice":"One of the oldest Hindu verses says it in five words: ekam sat vipra bahudha vadanti. Truth is one; the wise call it by many names. That line is how a lot of Hindus answer the question. The many gods are many faces and names of one reality, the way one person can be a mother, a boss and a friend and still be one person. Not every Hindu puts it the same way. Some families give their whole devotion to Vishnu, or Shiva, or the Goddess, and see the others as part of that one. Some honor many, each for a different part of life. Almost everyone has an ishta devata: a chosen deity, the form of God that feels like yours. Often it comes from your family. Sometimes you find your own. So the answer to the question at school can be simple: one truth, many names, and one of them feels like home.","screen":["`TRUTH IS ONE.` · `THE WISE CALL IT BY MANY NAMES.` · `MANY FACES, ONE REALITY.` · `THE ONE THAT FEELS LIKE YOURS.`"]},{"type":"the practice","duration":"20 sec","voice":"Three breaths with the bell. Think of the image of God you saw most growing up: on a wall, a dashboard, a shelf. Breathe in. As you breathe out, say its name once, quietly, as if it were the name of someone who knows you.","screen":[]},{"type":"the word","duration":"10 sec","voice":"Ishta devata. Your chosen deity: the form of God that feels like yours. Sixth word.","screen":["`ISHTA DEVATA — your 6th word`."]},{"type":"the carry","duration":"10 sec","voice":"Your line: one truth, many names. Next time someone asks how many gods you have, you've got an answer. Day six.","screen":[]},{"type":"the close","duration":null,"voice":"","screen":["`DAY 6 COUNTS.` → `TOMORROW: NOT FATE, NOT PAYBACK.`"]}]},"7":{"day":7,"title":"not fate, not payback","word":"karma","hook":"the word everyone borrows, and what it actually says","length":"3:00","carry":"what you do, you become","segments":[{"type":"the bell","duration":null,"voice":"","screen":["`DAY SEVEN.`"]},{"type":"review","duration":"10 sec","voice":"Yesterday: one truth, many names, and the one that's yours. Six words so far: Ganesha, om, Gayatri, namaste, arti, ishta devata. Today, the seventh.","screen":["`GANESHA · OM · GAYATRI · NAMASTE · ARTI · ISHTA DEVATA`"]},{"type":"the hook","duration":"50 sec","voice":"Karma. Someone says it when the car that cut them off gets stopped at the next light. Someone says it when a person who was unkind finally has a bad week. It turns up in song lyrics, on coffee mugs, under photos. Used like that, it means payback: the universe keeping a ledger and settling it on schedule, ideally where you can watch. It's a satisfying idea. It is also not quite what the word means. The older meaning is less about getting even and more about getting made, and it turns the whole thing around to face the one who's using it.","screen":["`KARMA.` → `THE CAR AT THE RED LIGHT.` → `PAYBACK?` → `NOT QUITE.`"]},{"type":"the teach","duration":"1 min 10","voice":"Karma is the Sanskrit word for action. A deed. Something done. In the Bhagavad Gita, Krishna points out that no one can stay without acting, even for a moment: breathing, eating, speaking, choosing, all of it is action. So the first thing to know is that karma isn't something that happens to you. It's what you do. The second thing is what action does back. One of the oldest Hindu texts says it like a proverb: as a person acts, so that person becomes. Not: so the person is punished. Becomes. Patience, practiced, turns into a patient person. Sharpness, practiced, turns into a sharp one. A mango seed isn't judged worthy of a mango tree; it grows one because that's what the seed is. The payback version keeps your eyes on other people: did they get what's coming? The older version turns you around: what am I growing with my next word? Hindu teachers also speak of karma across lifetimes, and that's a bigger conversation for later. For today: as you act, so you become.","screen":["`KARMA — ACTION, A DEED` · `NO ONE STAYS WITHOUT ACTING` · `AS ONE ACTS, SO ONE BECOMES` · `WHAT AM I GROWING?`"]},{"type":"the practice","duration":"20 sec","voice":"Three breaths with the bell. Bring to mind one small thing you did this week: a kind word, a sharp one, a promise kept or skipped. Don't grade it. Just ask what it's growing. Then pick one small action you'd be glad to see grow.","screen":[]},{"type":"the word","duration":"10 sec","voice":"Karma. Action, and what action makes of the one who does it. Not payback. Seventh word: a week's worth.","screen":["`KARMA — your 7th word`."]},{"type":"the carry","duration":"10 sec","voice":"Your line: what you do, you become. That's week one: seven words, and what's inside each. Tomorrow, what your grandmother was actually doing.","screen":[]},{"type":"the close","duration":null,"voice":"","screen":["`DAY 7 COUNTS.` → `TOMORROW: WHAT YOUR GRANDMOTHER WAS ACTUALLY DOING.`"]}]},"8":{"day":8,"title":"what your grandmother was actually doing","word":"puja","hook":"a flower, a flame, a bell: what's happening at the home shrine","length":"6:00","carry":"attention is an offering","segments":[{"type":"the bell","duration":null,"voice":"","screen":["`DAY EIGHT.`"]},{"type":"review","duration":"10 sec","voice":"Yesterday: karma. Your line was what you do, you become. Week one is behind you. Week two starts at home. Day eight.","screen":["`WHAT YOU DO, YOU BECOME.` → `WEEK TWO.`"]},{"type":"the hook","duration":"50 sec","voice":"Before the kettle goes on, someone lights a small brass lamp. Beside it: a marigold, fresh this morning, a bell with a handle worn smooth, a stick of incense, a little water in a cup, a sweet on a tiny plate. Some Hindu homes keep a shelf like this, or a corner, or a whole small room, and someone stops there each morning. Walk past it on an ordinary morning and it can look like decoration. Stand and watch for a minute, though, and the person in front of it is busy: pouring, lighting, ringing, setting things down. Their hands are working through something in order. Today's word is the name for what they're doing.","screen":["`A LAMP. A FLOWER. A BELL.` → `SOMEONE STOPS THERE EACH MORNING.` → `WHAT ARE THEY DOING?`"]},{"type":"the teach","duration":"2 min 15","voice":"Puja. It's usually translated worship, and that's not wrong, but in English the word can sound formal and far away. A home puja is close and small, and it's done with the hands. Watch the steps. Water is offered. A flower is set down. Incense is lit. A lamp is waved. A bell is rung. Food is offered, and afterward it comes back as prasad, a gift to be eaten. Families and regions do it differently, and some teachers describe the whole thing as welcoming God as an honored guest. Now listen to the Bhagavad Gita. Krishna, speaking as God, says that whoever offers him a leaf, a flower, a fruit, or water, with devotion, he accepts it. In Sanskrit it begins: patram pushpam phalam toyam. A leaf. A flower. A fruit. Water. Nothing on that list costs much. What the verse cares about is the devotion, the heart that comes with the gift. So here's one way in, for anyone, whatever they believe. Puja gives a person's attention one place to land, and walks it through one sense after another: the cool of the water, the color of the flower, the smell of the smoke, the sound of the bell, the taste of the sweet. For those few minutes, the whole person is in one place. Maybe that's why the shrine can be so simple: a picture, a small stone, a lamp. The size of the offering was never the point. The care is. Seen that way, a grandmother at that shelf every morning isn't only keeping a custom. She's spending her attention on purpose, before anything else can claim it.","screen":["`WATER, FLOWER, FLAME, BELL, FOOD` · `A LEAF, A FLOWER, A FRUIT, WATER` · `ONE SENSE AFTER ANOTHER` · then, large: `ATTENTION IS AN OFFERING.`"]},{"type":"the practice","duration":"2 min 30","voice":"Here's the practice: honor one ordinary thing. No shelf needed. Look around and choose one object you can see: a cup, a plant, your own open hand. Give it your eyes first, its color and its edges. Then, if you can reach it, your touch. Then close your eyes and keep the picture of it. If you pray, you can offer this small attention to God. If you don't, just let one thing have all of you for a little while. The bell holds the time.","screen":[]},{"type":"the word","duration":"25 sec","voice":"Puja. Honor, offered with the hands and the senses: a leaf, a flower, a fruit, water. Eighth word.","screen":["`PUJA — your 8th word`."]},{"type":"the carry","duration":"15 sec","voice":"Your line: attention is an offering. Today, give one ordinary thing your whole attention: a meal, a face, a cup of tea. One minute, nothing else in your hands. Day eight.","screen":[]},{"type":"the close","duration":null,"voice":"","screen":["`DAY 8 COUNTS.` → `TOMORROW: THE THING THAT'S YOURS TO DO.`"]}]},"9":{"day":9,"title":"the thing that's yours to do","word":"dharma","hook":"a warrior puts down his bow and says he no longer knows what's right","length":"3:00","carry":"do the thing that's yours to do","segments":[{"type":"the bell","duration":null,"voice":"","screen":["`DAY NINE.`"]},{"type":"review","duration":"10 sec","voice":"Yesterday: puja. Your line was attention is an offering. Today, the word at the center of the Bhagavad Gita.","screen":["`ATTENTION IS AN OFFERING.` → `DAY 9.`"]},{"type":"the hook","duration":"25 sec","voice":"The Bhagavad Gita opens on a battlefield. A warrior named Arjuna asks his charioteer to drive him out between the two armies, and he sees his own family on the other side: teachers, uncles, cousins. His bow slips from his hand and he sits down in the chariot. The word he reaches for, to say what he's lost, is today's word.","screen":["`KURUKSHETRA.` → `THE BOW SLIPS.` → `WHAT HE'S LOST.`"]},{"type":"the teach","duration":"1 min","voice":"Dharma gets translated as duty, law or religion, and each of those catches only a piece. It comes from a Sanskrit root meaning to hold, or to support. Dharma is what holds things up: teachers say the dharma of fire is to burn and the dharma of water is to flow. It's what a thing is for, the part it holds in the whole. In the Gita's second chapter, Arjuna says his mind is confused about dharma, and he asks his charioteer, Krishna, to teach him. The rest of the Gita is that teaching. One line from the third chapter shows where it starts: better your own dharma, even done imperfectly, than someone else's done well. Your own dharma is svadharma. So dharma isn't only rules handed down. It's also a question: what is mine to do here? Arjuna isn't missing information. He's lost the thread of what's his to do.","screen":["`DHARMA — WHAT HOLDS THINGS UP.` · `I AM YOUR STUDENT. TEACH ME.` · `YOUR OWN, EVEN IMPERFECTLY.` · `WHAT IS MINE TO DO HERE?`"]},{"type":"the practice","duration":"15 sec","voice":"Three breaths with the bell. On the first, bring to mind one thing you've been putting off. On the second, ask it once, silently: what's mine to do here? On the third, let the question go. No plan needed yet.","screen":[]},{"type":"the word","duration":"10 sec","voice":"Dharma. From a root meaning to hold: what holds things up, and your share of the holding. Ninth word.","screen":["`DHARMA — your 9th word`."]},{"type":"the carry","duration":"10 sec","voice":"Your line: do the thing that's yours to do. It's the question the whole Gita answers, and this path comes back to it. Day nine.","screen":[]},{"type":"the close","duration":null,"voice":"","screen":["`DAY 9 COUNTS.` → `TOMORROW: THE ONE BEHIND YOUR EYES.`"]}]},"10":{"day":10,"title":"the one behind your eyes","word":"atman","hook":"what stays when everything else about you changes","length":"6:00","carry":"someone is in there","segments":[{"type":"the bell","duration":null,"voice":"","screen":["`DAY TEN.`"]},{"type":"review","duration":"10 sec","voice":"Yesterday: dharma. Your line was do the thing that's yours to do. Today we turn from what gets done to the one doing it. Day ten.","screen":["`DO THE THING THAT'S YOURS TO DO.` → `DAY 10.`"]},{"type":"the hook","duration":"50 sec","voice":"A question with no trick in it. Who is hearing this sentence? Not what. Who. The quick answer is: me. But try pointing at the me. The body? It has been changing since childhood. The mood? It may have shifted twice since breakfast. The thoughts? They're moving faster than this voice. Everything you can point to keeps moving. And yet, the Upanishads say, something has been there for all of it, the one all of it happened to. A teacher in an old Hindu text was pressed on exactly this, and his answer turned the question around. Today's word is what he was pointing at.","screen":["`WHO IS HEARING THIS?` → `NOT THE BODY. NOT THE MOOD.` → `SOMETHING HAS BEEN THERE FOR ALL OF IT.`"]},{"type":"the teach","duration":"2 min 15","voice":"Atman. The self. Not the self in selfie, the personality you show the world. The Upanishads use atman for the deepest you: the one that is aware. And they notice something strange about it. You can't look straight at it. In the Brihadaranyaka Upanishad, a questioner keeps pressing the teacher Yajnavalkya: show me this self, plainly, the way you'd point and say, that's a cow, that's a horse. And Yajnavalkya answers, in effect: you can't see the seer of seeing. You can't hear the hearer of hearing. The one who looks can't become one more thing on the table to be looked at. So, in other passages, the same Upanishad describes the self by saying what it is not: neti, neti. Not this, not this. Whatever you can point to, the body, the breath, the busy mind, the self is not that. One way to put the Upanishad's point: take away everything that can be watched, and what's left is the watching. Put plainly: someone is in there. Today that someone gets its name. Here's where it turns practical. When worry comes, it comes as something seen: a thought, a tight chest, a story about tomorrow. The Upanishad points past it, to the one who sees. Hindu schools describe the atman in different ways. Some teach that the self is one with the whole. Others teach that it is a distinct soul, forever dear to God and dependent on God. Tomorrow brings the word for the whole. Today, just the turn this word makes: away from what's being watched, toward the one who is watching.","screen":["`ATMAN — THE SELF THAT IS AWARE` · `THE SEER OF SEEING` · `NETI, NETI — NOT THIS, NOT THIS` · `THE WATCHING, NOT THE WATCHED` · then, large: `SOMEONE IS IN THERE.`"]},{"type":"the practice","duration":"2 min 30","voice":"Today's practice is to notice the noticer. Let your eyes close or soften. Let a thought arrive, any thought. Don't follow it. Just see that it's there. Now notice that something saw it. Rest there for a moment, not in the thought, in the seeing. Another thought will come; that's what thoughts do. Same move: see it, then notice the one who saw. No need to get anywhere. The bell holds the time.","screen":[]},{"type":"the word","duration":"25 sec","voice":"Atman. The self: not the body, not the thoughts, but the one aware of them. Tenth word.","screen":["`ATMAN — your 10th word`."]},{"type":"the carry","duration":"15 sec","voice":"Your line: someone is in there. When worry shows up today, ask once, quietly: who is seeing this? Just the question. Day ten.","screen":[]},{"type":"the close","duration":null,"voice":"","screen":["`DAY 10 COUNTS.` → `TOMORROW: THE OCEAN THE WAVE FORGETS IT IS.`"]}]},"11":{"day":11,"title":"the ocean the wave forgets it is","word":"Brahman","hook":"salt in a cup of water, and rivers that reach the sea","length":"6:15","carry":"not two","segments":[{"type":"the bell","duration":null,"voice":"","screen":["`DAY ELEVEN.`"]},{"type":"review","duration":"20 sec","voice":"Yesterday: atman, the one behind your eyes. Your line was someone is in there. Did you catch yourself watching a thought? Today the word gets bigger, so we'll go slow. Day eleven.","screen":["`SOMEONE IS IN THERE.` → `DAY 11.`"]},{"type":"the hook","duration":"50 sec","voice":"A father hands his son a lump of salt and says: put this in a cup of water, and come back in the morning. The son does. Next morning the father says: bring me the salt. The boy looks. It's gone. Sip from the top, says the father. Salty. From the middle. Salty. From the bottom. Salty. The salt is in every sip, and he can't find it anywhere. The father is Uddalaka, the son is Shvetaketu, and the story is told in the Chandogya Upanishad. It's one of the old ways of pointing at today's word, and the word is a big one.","screen":["`A LUMP OF SALT. A CUP OF WATER.` → `WHERE DID IT GO?` → `IT'S IN EVERY SIP.`"]},{"type":"the teach","duration":"2 min 15","voice":"Brahman. Not Brahma, the creator god in the stories, and not Brahmin, the priestly class. Different words. Brahman is the name the Upanishads give to the whole: the reality underneath everything, what everything comes from and returns to. Like the salt, it isn't one more thing you can pick out. It's in every taste. The same chapter of the Chandogya has another picture. Rivers flow, some east, some west, and in the end they reach the sea and become the sea. And once there, the Upanishad says, they don't know: I am this river, I am that one. Now the line the father keeps saying to his son, again and again in that chapter: tat tvam asi. That's what you are. Yesterday's word, atman, the self, and today's word, Brahman, the whole: the Upanishad brings them face to face. Hindu thinkers read the line in more than one way. Advaita Vedanta, taught by Shankara, reads it as identity: the self and the whole are not two. Ramanuja's school reads it as one whole with real differences inside it, the self living in God and God as the self's inner ruler. Madhva's school keeps the self and God distinct, the self always dependent on God. These readings sit side by side in the tradition, and nobody here has to choose. Just notice what the picture does. In the Upanishad, the rivers don't dry up somewhere along the way. They become the sea. And today's title borrows the picture for one way of hearing it: a wave that is ocean the whole time, even while it forgets.","screen":["`BRAHMAN — THE WHOLE` · `SALT IN WATER, RIVERS IN THE SEA` · `TAT TVAM ASI — THAT'S WHAT YOU ARE` · `READ MORE THAN ONE WAY` · then, large: `NOT TWO.`"]},{"type":"the practice","duration":"2 min 30","voice":"Here's the practice: let the river reach the sea. Sit and breathe. Bring a river to mind, any one you've seen or can imagine. Let it carry this life, this name, today's worries. Follow it as it moves. Then let it reach the sea, and let the edges go. Not lost: arrived. Rest there, in the big water. Some rest there with one name of God in mind. The bell holds the time.","screen":[]},{"type":"the word","duration":"25 sec","voice":"Brahman. The whole, the reality under everything. Not Brahma, not Brahmin. Eleventh word.","screen":["`BRAHMAN — your 11th word`."]},{"type":"the carry","duration":"15 sec","voice":"Your line: not two. When you feel cut off from someone today, try the rivers and the sea for one breath. Day eleven.","screen":[]},{"type":"the close","duration":null,"voice":"","screen":["`DAY 11 COUNTS.` → `TOMORROW: ANYONE WHO TURNS A LIGHT ON.`"]}]},"12":{"day":12,"title":"anyone who turns a light on","word":"guru","hook":"the word got borrowed for salesmen; the old meaning is closer to a lamp","length":"6:00","carry":"thank a teacher today","segments":[{"type":"the bell","duration":null,"voice":"","screen":["`DAY TWELVE.`"]},{"type":"review","duration":"20 sec","voice":"Yesterday: Brahman. Your line was not two. If it felt too big, that's all right; it's a word to grow into. Today is smaller and closer to home. Day twelve.","screen":["`NOT TWO.` → `DAY 12.`"]},{"type":"the hook","duration":"50 sec","voice":"Marketing guru. Fitness guru. Some guru with an online course and a very confident voice. In everyday English the word has drifted toward a person with something to sell. In Hindu tradition it names something far more personal, and the thing it describes may already be in your life without the word ever being used. A grandparent at the kitchen table. A coach. A teacher who stayed after class. A friend who said one true sentence at exactly the right time. Today's word is for that person.","screen":["`MARKETING GURU. FITNESS GURU.` → `THE OLDER MEANING.` → `ONE TRUE SENTENCE, AT THE RIGHT TIME.`"]},{"type":"the teach","duration":"2 min 15","voice":"Guru. A teacher, but in this tradition more than a teacher of facts. A guru is someone who helps you see. An Upanishad called the Advaya Taraka plays with the sound of the word: the syllable gu, it says, means darkness, and ru is what stops it. Wordplay, not a dictionary entry, but a vivid one. A guru is the one who removes the dark. Yesterday you met one without the title. Uddalaka, with the cup of salt water, was a father acting as a guru, teaching his son by letting him taste. In stories like that one, learning happens up close, in a relationship, one person to another. The Mundaka Upanishad says that someone who wants to know should go to a teacher with fuel in hand, firewood for the teacher's fire, a small gift and a sign of willingness to serve. And the teacher, it says, should be learned in the texts and steady in Brahman. Two things there. The student comes humbly. The teacher has to be the real thing. Hindu traditions honor gurus in more than one way. Some follow one teacher for life, inside a long line of teachers. For some, God is the first guru, or the guru within. And on Guru Purnima, a full-moon festival, students thank their teachers. But keep the Advaya Taraka's picture close: someone stood where you were stuck, and the dark lifted. Today's title says it: anyone who turns a light on. A guru isn't only a job title. It can be a moment.","screen":["`GU — DARKNESS, RU — WHAT STOPS IT` · `A FATHER WITH A CUP OF SALT WATER` · `GO WITH FUEL IN HAND` · `A MOMENT, NOT JUST A TITLE` · then, large: `ANYONE WHO TURNS A LIGHT ON.`"]},{"type":"the practice","duration":"2 min 30","voice":"Today, remember one light. Rest your hands in your lap. Bring to mind one time someone helped you see. One sentence, one afternoon, one look. Let their face come. Hear it again, if you can. Then, silently, thank them. If you pray, you can thank God for them too. Sit for a moment in what the room looked like once the light was on. The bell holds the time.","screen":[]},{"type":"the word","duration":"25 sec","voice":"Guru. A teacher who helps you see; in one Upanishad's play on the sound, the one who removes the dark. Twelfth word.","screen":["`GURU — your 12th word`."]},{"type":"the carry","duration":"15 sec","voice":"Your line: thank a teacher today. The actual person, if you can reach them. Two lines in a message are plenty. Day twelve.","screen":[]},{"type":"the close","duration":null,"voice":"","screen":["`DAY 12 COUNTS.` → `TOMORROW: WHY 108.`"]}]},"13":{"day":13,"title":"why 108","word":"japa","hook":"beads moving through someone's fingers, and the number on the string","length":"6:00","carry":"count what you want to keep","segments":[{"type":"the bell","duration":null,"voice":"","screen":["`DAY THIRTEEN.`"]},{"type":"review","duration":"20 sec","voice":"Yesterday: guru. Your line was thank a teacher today. Did a name come to mind? Today a mantra gets a counter. Day thirteen.","screen":["`THANK A TEACHER TODAY.` → `DAY 13.`"]},{"type":"the hook","duration":"50 sec","voice":"Ask why a mala has a hundred and eight beads and you may hear a different answer each time. Watch one in use first. Beads move through someone's fingers, one at a time: wooden beads, or seeds, or stones, on a string, with one larger bead at the top that, by custom, the fingers don't pass over. The lips move without sound. The thumb draws the next bead forward. By tradition, a full Hindu mala carries a hundred and eight beads, plus that one at the top. The number can wait. A better question comes first: what is the person with the beads actually doing?","screen":["`BEADS ON A STRING.` → `LIPS MOVING, NO SOUND.` → `WHY 108?`"]},{"type":"the teach","duration":"2 min 15","voice":"Japa. Repeating a mantra, or a name of God, again and again, often counted. On day three, the Gayatri showed what a mantra is: a tool for the mind. Japa is using that tool again and again. The beads are the counter: one bead, one repetition. The hand keeps count so the mind doesn't have to, and the mind is free to stay with the sound. The Bhagavad Gita names japa among offerings. Here is the verse around it. In the tenth chapter, Krishna names where he can be found. Among things that don't move, the Himalaya. Among words, the one syllable, om. And among offerings, he says, I am the offering of japa. The Sanskrit is yajnanam japa-yajno 'smi. Of all the ways to make an offering, here is one that needs no fire and no flowers. Just a name, said again. Now, why a hundred and eight? People give answers from the sky, from lists of divine names, from the body. None of them comes from a text this lesson can put in front of you, so we'll leave the question open, honestly. But there's something simpler worth noticing about any fixed count. It gives the practice an end. You know when you're done. You don't have to decide, halfway through, whether you've done enough; the string decides. And a practice that can be finished today can be picked up again tomorrow, and the day after. That's how a practice gets kept. So the number on the string may hold mysteries, but it also does a plain job: it turns a good intention into something you can count.","screen":["`JAPA — A NAME, SAID AGAIN` · `THE HAND COUNTS, THE MIND RESTS` · `I AM JAPA, AMONG OFFERINGS` · `A FIXED COUNT GIVES AN END` · then, large: `COUNT WHAT YOU WANT TO KEEP.`"]},{"type":"the practice","duration":"2 min 30","voice":"Here's the practice: count on your fingers. No beads needed. Each finger has three sections. Touch your thumb to the top section of your index finger: one. The middle section: two. The base: three. Then on to the next finger. With each touch, say om silently on the out-breath, or, if you pray, a name of God you use. Go slowly. You won't reach a hundred and eight today, and you don't need to. The bell holds the time.","screen":[]},{"type":"the word","duration":"25 sec","voice":"Japa. Repeating a mantra or a name, counted, so the hand keeps count and the mind can rest. Thirteenth word.","screen":["`JAPA — your 13th word`."]},{"type":"the carry","duration":"15 sec","voice":"Your line: count what you want to keep. Once today, under a desk or on a train, count a few quiet oms on your fingers. Nobody will know. Day thirteen.","screen":[]},{"type":"the close","duration":null,"voice":"","screen":["`DAY 13 COUNTS.` → `TOMORROW: WHY IT'S SAID THREE TIMES.`"]}]},"14":{"day":14,"title":"why it's said three times","word":"shanti","hook":"om shanti shanti shanti: three times, never once, and there's a reason","length":"3:00","carry":"peace, peace, peace","segments":[{"type":"the bell","duration":null,"voice":"","screen":["`DAY FOURTEEN.`"]},{"type":"review","duration":"10 sec","voice":"Yesterday: japa. Your line was count what you want to keep. Two weeks today. Today, a word you've probably heard said three times in a row.","screen":["`COUNT WHAT YOU WANT TO KEEP.` → `TWO WEEKS.`"]},{"type":"the hook","duration":"20 sec","voice":"Om shanti shanti shanti. It closes a lot of Hindu prayers, and more than a few yoga classes. Not once, not twice: three times. That isn't just for the rhythm. Traditional teachers read the three as a list. First, a guess at the word.","screen":["`OM SHANTI SHANTI SHANTI.` → `WHY THREE?`"]},{"type":"the teach","duration":"55 sec","voice":"Shanti means peace, the settled kind: calm water with nothing stirring it. The Bhagavad Gita puts it bluntly: for someone without peace, how can there be happiness? Now the three. Traditional teachers read the three shantis as three kinds of trouble, the three places a bad day can come from. Trouble from forces beyond anyone's control: storms, floods, the world. Trouble from other beings: the friend who didn't call, the driver who cut in. And trouble from within: your own body and your own mind. Three sources, three shantis. The teachers add something practical. There's little anyone can do about the first, more about the second, and the third is the one most within reach, and the easiest to skip. So the chant isn't padding. It goes down the list: peace out there, peace between us, peace in here.","screen":["`SHANTI — THE SETTLED KIND.` · `THE WORLD.` · `OTHER PEOPLE.` · `YOUR OWN MIND.`"]},{"type":"the practice","duration":"15 sec","voice":"Three breaths with the bell, one shanti on each, said silently. First out-breath: toward the world. Second: toward one person you're not at peace with. You don't have to forgive them. Third: toward your own mind.","screen":[]},{"type":"the word","duration":"10 sec","voice":"Shanti. Peace, the settled kind, said three times: the world, other people, your own mind. Fourteenth word. Puja, dharma, atman, Brahman, guru, japa, shanti: that's week two.","screen":["`SHANTI — your 14th word` → `WEEK TWO. DONE.`"]},{"type":"the carry","duration":"10 sec","voice":"Your line: peace, peace, peace. Next time something rattles you, there are three places it could have come from. Next week brings the doing words. Day fourteen.","screen":[]},{"type":"the close","duration":null,"voice":"","screen":["`DAY 14 COUNTS.` → `TOMORROW: HANUMAN'S WHOLE PERSONALITY.`"]}]},"15":{"day":15,"title":"Hanuman's whole personality","word":"seva","hook":"a mountain carried across the sky, because one herb was needed","length":"6:30","carry":"do something for someone, quietly","segments":[{"type":"the bell","duration":null,"voice":"","screen":["`DAY FIFTEEN.`"]},{"type":"review","duration":"20 sec","voice":"Yesterday, shanti. Your line was peace, peace, peace. Which of the three rattled you most? Week three starts now, and it opens with words you do with your hands. Day fifteen.","screen":["`PEACE, PEACE, PEACE.`"]},{"type":"the hook","duration":"50 sec","voice":"On a battlefield in Lanka, Lakshmana, Rama's brother, lies wounded, barely breathing. A healer says there is a cure: certain herbs that grow on a mountain far to the north, and they have to be here fast. Hanuman goes. He crosses the sky, finds the mountain, and then he stops. The herbs are hiding from him, the story says, and he can't tell which ones are needed. There's no time to go back and ask. So he solves it another way. He lifts the whole peak out of the ground and carries it back through the air, herbs and all. Lakshmana breathes. Hanuman has a monkey's face, the strength of the wind, and a picture of him hangs in homes and shops. Today's word is why.","screen":["`A BROTHER WOUNDED. NO TIME TO LOSE.` → `HE CAN'T TELL WHICH HERB.` → `SO HE BRINGS THE MOUNTAIN.`"]},{"type":"the teach","duration":"2 min 15","voice":"Seva. Service. Not service as a job title, and not help with a receipt attached. Seva is doing what's needed because it's needed, with the self stepped out of the way. Hanuman is the tradition's living picture of it. Notice what the mountain story leaves out. There's no speech. No moment where he explains how hard it was. The whole story points at one thing: a man on the ground starts breathing again. The strength is enormous, and it's all aimed outward. The Ramayana does this with him more than once. When he leaps the ocean and finds Sita in Lanka, what he finally puts in Rama's hand is hers, not his: her jewel, and her message. Later devotional stories push the picture even further; people tell of him opening his own chest to show that Rama and Sita live inside it. Whatever he has, it's for someone else. And seva doesn't need a mountain. In temples and homes it's very ordinary work. Washing the floor before the festival. Chopping vegetables for a meal that strangers will eat. Sitting with a sick neighbor so the family can sleep. Nobody hands out credit for it, and that's part of the point. There's something else folded into the word. Some who practice it say seva done quietly works on the one doing it too: for as long as the hands are busy with someone else's need, the head gets a rest from itself. Hanuman's gifts were huge. The practice works with whatever size yours are.","screen":["`SEVA — SERVICE, SELF OUT OF THE WAY.` · `NO SPEECH. A MAN BREATHES AGAIN.` · `HER JEWEL, NOT HIS ADVENTURE.` · `A FLOOR, A MEAL, A NIGHT'S SLEEP.` · then, large: `WHATEVER HE HAS, IT'S FOR SOMEONE ELSE.`"]},{"type":"the practice","duration":"2 min 30","voice":"Today's practice: find your herb. Let your shoulders drop. Bring one thing to mind that you're good at. Cooking. Listening. Fixing a bike. Staying calm in a mess. Now let one person come to mind who could use exactly that this week. Don't plan it all out. Just decide: I'll do it, and I won't mention it. Then rest in the decision for the length of the sit. If you pray, you can offer the work before you do it. The bell holds the time.","screen":[]},{"type":"the word","duration":"25 sec","voice":"Seva. Service done because it's needed, with the self out of the way. Hanuman carrying the mountain. Fifteenth word.","screen":["`SEVA — your 15th word`."]},{"type":"the carry","duration":"15 sec","voice":"Your line: do something for someone, quietly. One small thing today. No post. No mention. Just a mountain moved, and nobody told. Day fifteen.","screen":[]},{"type":"the close","duration":null,"voice":"","screen":["`DAY 15 COUNTS.` → `TOMORROW: LOVE AS A METHOD.`"]}]},"16":{"day":16,"title":"love as a method","word":"bhakti","hook":"a leaf, a flower, a fruit, a little water: what the Gita says is enough","length":"6:30","carry":"devotion is a verb","segments":[{"type":"the bell","duration":null,"voice":"","screen":["`DAY SIXTEEN.`"]},{"type":"review","duration":"20 sec","voice":"Yesterday, seva, and Hanuman with a mountain in his hands. Your line was do something for someone, quietly. If you did it, did some part of you want to tell somebody? Day sixteen.","screen":["`DO SOMETHING FOR SOMEONE, QUIETLY.`"]},{"type":"the hook","duration":"50 sec","voice":"A leaf. A flower. A piece of fruit. A little water. That list came up on day eight, at the home shrine, and back then the point was how small the gifts are. Today, read the rest of the sentence. In the ninth chapter of the Bhagavad Gita, Krishna says that whoever offers him one of these with love, he accepts it, because it was offered with love. The gift isn't what makes it count. The love is. And the Sanskrit word for that love sits right inside the verse. A whole way of walking this path is built on it, a way full of songs, drums, bells and late nights of singing. It's today's word.","screen":["`A LEAF, A FLOWER, AGAIN.` → `OFFERED WITH LOVE.` → `THE WORD INSIDE THE VERSE.`"]},{"type":"the teach","duration":"2 min 15","voice":"Bhakti. Devotion. Love turned toward God and practiced on purpose, the way you'd practice an instrument. You've spent some of this camp with the head: atman, Brahman, the self and the whole. Bhakti is the heart, and the Gita treats it as a full path of its own. In the Gita's telling, nobody has to master the ideas first. The way in is love. Devotees aim that love at God in different forms, Krishna, Rama, Shiva, the Goddess, and the move is the same. Tradition remembers a woman who showed it. Mirabai was a princess in what is now Rajasthan, and the stories say that from childhood she thought of Krishna as her own. Married into a royal family, she kept singing to him in the temple, in public, with ordinary devotees. Her in-laws, the stories go, were furious. They sent her poison, and it did her no harm. Whatever you make of those details, her songs are still sung. Here's why bhakti is a method and not only a mood. Moods come and go. The lamp gets lit anyway. The song gets sung on the tired night too. Some who practice it say the feeling catches up with the doing, not the other way around. Go back to the leaf: what the verse looks for is the love it's offered with, and love, on this path, is something you keep doing. That's part of why this path can be so loud. Bells, drums, a crowd singing the same name over and over. That's bhakti with the volume up.","screen":["`BHAKTI — LOVE, PRACTICED ON PURPOSE.` · `THE HEART IS A PATH TOO.` · `MIRABAI KEPT SINGING.` · `THE LAMP GETS LIT ANYWAY.` · then, large: `DEVOTION IS A VERB.`"]},{"type":"the practice","duration":"2 min 30","voice":"The practice today: offer a leaf. Find one small thing within reach. A sip of water, a flower, a breath, even just your open hand. Hold it for a moment. If you pray, you can offer it, with love, to God in whatever form you know. If you don't, offer it to whatever you love most in the world, and let the feeling be as big as it wants to be. Nobody's watching. The bell holds the time.","screen":[]},{"type":"the word","duration":"25 sec","voice":"Bhakti. Devotion. Love you practice, not only love you feel. A leaf, offered whole. Sixteenth word.","screen":["`BHAKTI — your 16th word`."]},{"type":"the carry","duration":"15 sec","voice":"Your line: devotion is a verb. Today, do one loving thing on a day you don't feel like it. That's the method, all of it. Day sixteen.","screen":[]},{"type":"the close","duration":null,"voice":"","screen":["`DAY 16 COUNTS.` → `TOMORROW: THE COURAGE NOT TO STRIKE.`"]}]},"17":{"day":17,"title":"the courage not to strike","word":"ahimsa","hook":"the yoga list that starts before any pose: not-harming","length":"6:30","carry":"do no harm — start with your mouth","segments":[{"type":"the bell","duration":null,"voice":"","screen":["`DAY SEVENTEEN.`"]},{"type":"review","duration":"20 sec","voice":"Yesterday, bhakti, love practiced like an instrument. Your line was devotion is a verb. Did you do the loving thing on a day you didn't feel like it? Today's word asks for a different kind of strength. Day seventeen.","screen":["`DEVOTION IS A VERB.`"]},{"type":"the hook","duration":"50 sec","voice":"A message comes in late at night. It's unfair, and it stings, and your thumbs already know the reply. The sharp one. The one that would land. Here's a strange fact about yoga. Before the Yoga Sutras of Patanjali teach anything about posture, they set out a short list of restraints, the ground the rest is built on, and the first word on that list isn't stretch, or breathe. It's not-harming. So the first restraint in that text has something to say to those thumbs.","screen":["`A MESSAGE. A SHARP REPLY READY.` → `YOGA BEFORE THE POSES.` → `THE FIRST WORD: NOT-HARMING.`"]},{"type":"the teach","duration":"2 min 15","voice":"Ahimsa. Himsa is harm, violence. Ahimsa is its opposite: not-harming. And it's easy to hear that as a soft word, a word for people who never stand up for anything. Gandhi, who put this word beside truth at the center of his public life, heard it the other way around. He was not a quiet man. He confronted an empire, and he refused the one weapon everyone expected him to pick up. Ahimsa takes nerve. When something hits a person, the body often wants to hit back, and ahimsa is the half-second where you don't add more harm to a moment that already has some. The Jains, close neighbors on the same soil, hold ahimsa at the heart of their path too. Now look at what Patanjali says comes of it. The Yoga Sutras teach that when someone is truly settled in not-harming, hostility falls away in their presence. Not because they won the argument. Because they stopped feeding it. Some teachers name three places ahimsa lives: in thought, in word, and in deed. Deeds are the obvious ones. Today's line starts with the middle one, words, because that's where the half-second is easiest to miss. The cutting joke. The thing said about someone who isn't in the room. The reply sent at night. One more layer. The vow points inward too. A voice in the head that calls a person worthless is himsa as well. Not-harming includes the one doing the not-harming.","screen":["`AHIMSA — NOT-HARMING.` · `NOT SOFT. IT TAKES NERVE.` · `HOSTILITY FALLS AWAY.` · `THOUGHT, WORD, DEED.` · then, large: `START WITH YOUR MOUTH.`"]},{"type":"the practice","duration":"2 min 30","voice":"Now, find the half-second. Bring back the last time you said something sharp, to someone or about someone. Don't punish yourself for it; that would be harm too. Just replay it, slowly, and this time stop at the moment before the words came out. Feel the push to strike. Let it pass through without acting on it. What could have been said instead? Maybe nothing. Rest there. The bell holds the time.","screen":[]},{"type":"the word","duration":"25 sec","voice":"Ahimsa. Not-harming, in thought, word and deed. The courage not to strike. Seventeenth word.","screen":["`AHIMSA — your 17th word`."]},{"type":"the carry","duration":"15 sec","voice":"Your line: do no harm — start with your mouth. Today, let one sharp sentence go unsaid, and notice what happens to the room. Day seventeen.","screen":[]},{"type":"the close","duration":null,"voice":"","screen":["`DAY 17 COUNTS.` → `TOMORROW: WHY THE WORLD LOOKS SOLID.`"]}]},"18":{"day":18,"title":"why the world looks solid","word":"maya","hook":"a rope on a dark path, and the snake that was never there","length":"6:30","carry":"hold it lightly","segments":[{"type":"the bell","duration":null,"voice":"","screen":["`DAY EIGHTEEN.`"]},{"type":"review","duration":"20 sec","voice":"Yesterday, ahimsa, the courage not to strike. Your line was do no harm — start with your mouth. Did a sharp sentence go unsaid? Today we go back to the big ideas for a day. Day eighteen.","screen":["`DO NO HARM — START WITH YOUR MOUTH.`"]},{"type":"the hook","duration":"50 sec","voice":"Dusk, on a path, and something is coiled across it. Your heart jumps before your mind has a word for it. Snake. You freeze. Your pulse is real. The fear is real. Then someone brings a lamp, and it's a rope. Just a rope, lying where it always was. Nothing about the rope changed. The snake was never there. And yet for a few seconds it ran your whole body. Teachers in this tradition tell that small story because it holds a very big word. A thing that is not what it seems, and works on you anyway.","screen":["`SOMETHING COILED ON THE PATH.` → `THE FEAR IS REAL.` → `A LAMP. A ROPE.`"]},{"type":"the teach","duration":"2 min 15","voice":"Maya. It often gets translated as illusion, and that one word causes a lot of confusion. It sounds like the tradition is saying the world is fake. Your kitchen, your friends, your job, none of it real. That isn't what it says. The Svetasvatara Upanishad puts it this way: know that nature is maya, and that the great Lord is the one who works the maya. So maya isn't nothing. It's the power that makes the world appear the way it does, so convincingly that we stop looking past it. Schools of the tradition read this differently, and they've argued about it with great care. Advaita Vedanta, the school of Shankara, says the world we see is an appearance laid over Brahman, the way the snake is laid over the rope. The rope is what's really there. Other schools, like those of Ramanuja and Madhva, hold that the world is fully real, made by God, and that maya names God's wondrous power. Both readings agree on one thing. The surface is not the whole story. And that's where the word walks into an ordinary day. A post makes someone's life look perfect. A comment feels like an attack on all of you. A bad morning looks like a bad life. Each one is a snake on the path, until the lamp comes. Neither reading asks anyone to stop living in the world. Only to stop gripping it as if the surface were everything. Hold it lightly.","screen":["`NOT FAKE. NOT THE WHOLE STORY.` · `THE POWER THAT MAKES IT APPEAR.` · `SNAKE ON A ROPE, OR GOD'S POWER?` · `A POST. A COMMENT. A BAD MORNING.` · then, large: `HOLD IT LIGHTLY.`"]},{"type":"the practice","duration":"2 min 30","voice":"Today's practice: bring the lamp. Breathe once, slowly. Recall one thing this week that made your heart jump. A message, a look, a number, a picture of someone else's life. Feel the jump again, just a little. Don't push it away. Now look at it again, slowly, as if someone had brought a lamp. What is actually there? Let the tightness loosen as much as it wants to. The bell holds the time.","screen":[]},{"type":"the word","duration":"25 sec","voice":"Maya. The power that makes the world appear, so well that we stop looking past the surface. The snake that turns out to be rope. Eighteenth word.","screen":["`MAYA — your 18th word`."]},{"type":"the carry","duration":"15 sec","voice":"Your line: hold it lightly. Today, when something makes you jump, take one breath and ask: snake, or rope? Day eighteen.","screen":[]},{"type":"the close","duration":null,"voice":"","screen":["`DAY 18 COUNTS.` → `TOMORROW: THE EXIT DOOR.`"]}]},"19":{"day":19,"title":"the exit door","word":"moksha","hook":"a boy at Death's door turns down every treasure for one question","length":"6:30","carry":"freedom is the point","segments":[{"type":"the bell","duration":null,"voice":"","screen":["`DAY NINETEEN.`"]},{"type":"review","duration":"20 sec","voice":"Yesterday, maya, and the snake that was a rope. Your line was hold it lightly. Did you catch a snake on the path? Today, the word for where this whole path is headed. Day nineteen.","screen":["`HOLD IT LIGHTLY.`"]},{"type":"the hook","duration":"50 sec","voice":"A boy named Nachiketa is sitting at the door of Death's house. In the Katha Upanishad, his father, in a burst of temper, has said: I give you to Death. So the boy goes. Yama, the lord of death, is away, and Nachiketa waits on the doorstep without food for three nights. When Yama comes home, he's embarrassed to have kept a guest waiting, and he offers the boy three wishes. The first two are easy. The third is a question: what happens to a person after death? Yama tries to talk him out of it. Ask for something else, he says. Ask for long life, sons and grandsons, cattle, gold, a kingdom. Nachiketa says no.","screen":["`A BOY AT DEATH'S DOOR.` → `THREE WISHES.` → `TAKE THE GOLD INSTEAD.` → `NO.`"]},{"type":"the teach","duration":"2 min 15","voice":"Nachiketa's answer is the heart of today's word. He tells Death that all those treasures wear out, that wealth will never satisfy a person, that even a long life is short. Keep your horses, he says. I want to know. And Yama, impressed, begins to teach him. That turn, from the list of things to the thing under the list, points straight at today's word. Moksha means release. Liberation. Freedom. The tradition speaks of the soul moving through birth after birth, bound by its own actions and desires, and moksha is the knot finally coming untied. Hindu thought names several aims of a good life: dharma, doing what's yours to do; artha, making a living; kama, love and pleasure. None of those are bad. Moksha sits beyond them, as the freedom the others point toward. What that freedom is, the schools describe differently. Advaita Vedanta says it's waking up to what day ten and day eleven already hinted: that atman, the self, is Brahman, and always was. Devotional schools, such as those centered on Krishna, describe it as living forever in the loving presence of God, never dissolved, never alone. Some schools teach that a person can be free before death, liberated while still living. Notice the shape of Nachiketa's choice. He doesn't hate gold. He just won't trade the real question for it. That's the word in an ordinary day too: the moment you see that the next thing on the list was never going to be enough.","screen":["`KEEP YOUR HORSES. I WANT TO KNOW.` · `MOKSHA — RELEASE. THE KNOT UNTIED.` · `BEYOND DUTY, WEALTH AND PLEASURE.` · `ATMAN IS BRAHMAN, OR LOVE WITH GOD.` · then, large: `FREEDOM IS THE POINT.`"]},{"type":"the practice","duration":"2 min 30","voice":"Here's the practice: set one thing down. Sit and breathe. Notice one thing you want right now. A message back, a better week, a number on a screen. Let the wanting be there. Now, just for the length of this sit, set it down beside you. Not forever. Just: for now, I don't need it. Breathe, and notice what's left when the wanting rests. If you pray, you can hold that space open to God. The bell holds the time.","screen":[]},{"type":"the word","duration":"25 sec","voice":"Moksha. Release. Liberation. The knot untied, the freedom the other aims of life point toward. Nineteenth word.","screen":["`MOKSHA — your 19th word`."]},{"type":"the carry","duration":"15 sec","voice":"Your line: freedom is the point. Once today, when you catch yourself chasing the next thing, set it down for one breath, the way Nachiketa set down the gold. Day nineteen.","screen":[]},{"type":"the close","duration":null,"voice":"","screen":["`DAY 19 COUNTS.` → `TOMORROW: GOOD COMPANY.`"]}]},"20":{"day":20,"title":"good company","word":"satsang","hook":"an old chain of verses that starts with who you sit with and ends in freedom","length":"6:30","carry":"good company changes you","segments":[{"type":"the bell","duration":null,"voice":"","screen":["`DAY TWENTY.`"]},{"type":"review","duration":"20 sec","voice":"Yesterday, moksha, and a boy who told Death to keep his gold. Your line was freedom is the point. Did you set something down, even for a breath? One more word before the camp ends. Day twenty.","screen":["`FREEDOM IS THE POINT.`"]},{"type":"the hook","duration":"50 sec","voice":"Yesterday's word was the top of the mountain: freedom. Fair question, then. Where do you start? There's an old Sanskrit song called Bhaja Govindam, traditionally credited to the teacher Shankara, and one of its verses answers that question like a row of dominoes. Each step knocks over the next, all the way up to freedom. You'd expect the first domino to be something hard. Years of study. Fasting. A cave in the mountains. It isn't. The very first domino is who you spend your time with.","screen":["`WHERE DO YOU START?` → `A ROW OF DOMINOES.` → `THE FIRST ONE: YOUR COMPANY.`"]},{"type":"the teach","duration":"2 min 15","voice":"Satsang. Sat means true, real, good. Sang means company, being together. Satsang is good company, the company of what's true and of people turned toward it. Here's the chain from Bhaja Govindam, in plain words. From good company comes letting go. From letting go comes freedom from delusion. From freedom from delusion comes a mind that stays steady. And from a steady mind comes freedom while you're still alive. Look at what's in that chain. Delusion: think of the snake on the rope from day eighteen. Freedom is moksha from yesterday. And the thing that starts it all isn't a technique. It's a room. In Hindu life, a satsang is often an actual gathering. People meet in a home or a temple hall to sing, to hear a teacher, to read a text together, to sit in silence side by side. Nobody has to be advanced. You come, you sit, you listen. The idea underneath the verse is simple and a little uncomfortable: company shapes a person. A friend's worries can become your worries; a grandmother's patience can become your patience. The verse doesn't say go find perfect people. It puts company first in the chain, and leaves the question with you: what is your company doing to you, and how much time do you spend near what's true? That first domino has a name and a face.","screen":["`SAT — TRUE. SANG — COMPANY.` · `GOOD COMPANY, THEN LETTING GO, THEN FREEDOM.` · `A ROOM, NOT A TECHNIQUE.` · `WE TAKE THE SHAPE OF OUR COMPANY.` · then, large: `GOOD COMPANY CHANGES YOU.`"]},{"type":"the practice","duration":"2 min 30","voice":"Here's the practice: sit in company. Sit and breathe. Bring to mind one person whose presence makes you steadier. A friend, a grandparent, a teacher, someone in a story. Let their face come. Notice what happens to your breathing near them. Then widen it, to anyone, anywhere, who stops today to pray or to sit still. Let yourself be one of them. If you pray, you can hold them all before God. The bell holds the time.","screen":[]},{"type":"the word","duration":"25 sec","voice":"Satsang. Good company. The company of what's true, and the first domino on the way to freedom. Twentieth word.","screen":["`SATSANG — your 20th word`."]},{"type":"the carry","duration":"15 sec","voice":"Your line: good company changes you. Today, spend ten unhurried minutes with one person who steadies you. In person, if you can. Day twenty.","screen":[]},{"type":"the close","duration":null,"voice":"","screen":["`DAY 20 COUNTS.` → `TOMORROW: THE MOUNTAIN AHEAD.`"]}]},"21":{"day":21,"title":"the mountain ahead","word":"the Gita","hook":"back to the battlefield: a warrior drops his bow, and the conversation begins","length":"6:45","carry":"you have the right to act. Not to the fruit","segments":[{"type":"the bell","duration":null,"voice":"","screen":["`DAY TWENTY-ONE.`"]},{"type":"review","duration":"20 sec","voice":"Yesterday, satsang. Your line was good company changes you. Now, the whole camp, quickly: Ganesha, om, Gayatri, namaste, arti, ishta devata, karma, puja, dharma, atman, Brahman, guru, japa, shanti, seva, bhakti, ahimsa, maya, moksha, satsang. Twenty words. Today makes twenty-one. Day twenty-one.","screen":["`GOOD COMPANY CHANGES YOU.` → the strand, twenty words lit."]},{"type":"the hook","duration":"50 sec","voice":"Go back to day nine. A battlefield, two armies drawn up, conch shells blowing. Arjuna, a great archer, asks his charioteer to drive him out between the lines so he can see who he's about to fight. He looks across and sees teachers, uncles, cousins, friends. His mouth goes dry. His bow slips from his hand. And he sits down in the chariot and says he will not fight. We left him there, with his driver, Krishna, beside him. Today we find out what happens next. Because the whole conversation that follows has a name, and it's the last word of this camp.","screen":["`BACK TO THE BATTLEFIELD.` → `HIS BOW SLIPS.` → `WHAT DOES THE DRIVER SAY?`"]},{"type":"the teach","duration":"2 min 15","voice":"The Bhagavad Gita. The Song of the Lord. It sits inside the great epic, the Mahabharata, and it's a conversation between two people in a chariot. On day nine you heard that the driver is Krishna, and a great deal more than a driver. Krishna does more than give orders. He teaches. He talks about the self that cannot be killed, which you met as atman. He talks about acting without grabbing, about loving God, about seeing clearly. Look back at this week and you'll hear echoes. Action with the self out of the way, the way seva works. Love as a path of its own, bhakti. The Gita holds these paths side by side. Then the line this camp saved for last. In the second chapter Krishna says, in plain words: your right is to the action alone, never to its fruits. Don't let the fruit be your reason for acting. And the verse keeps going: don't let that become a reason to do nothing, either. It isn't a way out of the fight. It's a way to stand up and act without being owned by the result. And near the end of the Gita comes a surprise. After everything, Krishna tells Arjuna to think it over fully, and then do as he chooses. He hands the choice back. In the fourth camp you'll read this text itself, line by line. For now you get the door, and the sentence that opens it.","screen":["`THE SONG OF THE LORD, IN A CHARIOT.` · `THE WHOLE CAMP IS IN THERE.` · `NOT A WAY OUT. A WAY TO STAND UP.` · `THINK IT OVER. THEN CHOOSE.` · then, large: `YOU HAVE THE RIGHT TO ACT. NOT TO THE FRUIT.`"]},{"type":"the practice","duration":"2 min 30","voice":"Here's the practice: pick up the bow. Sit and breathe. Press your palms together at your chest, the way you did on day one. Bring to mind one thing that's yours to do this week, the thing you've been sitting down in the chariot about. Picture doing it, fully. Then open your hand, and let go of how it turns out. The action is yours. The fruit isn't. If you pray, you can offer the result to God. The bell holds the time.","screen":[]},{"type":"the word","duration":"25 sec","voice":"The Gita. The Song of the Lord, a conversation in a chariot that gets a warrior back on his feet. Twenty-first word, and the door to the mountain.","screen":["`THE GITA — your 21st word` → the strand complete → `CAMP 1. FIRST WORDS. DONE.`"]},{"type":"the carry","duration":"15 sec","voice":"Your line: you have the right to act. Not to the fruit. Do one thing today that's yours to do, and let the result go. Tomorrow the stories begin, with a goddess, a bath, and a boy made of earth. Day twenty-one.","screen":[]},{"type":"the close","duration":null,"voice":"","screen":["`DAY 21 COUNTS.` → `TOMORROW: THE BOY AT THE DOOR.`"]}]}},"JUDAISM":{"1":{"day":1,"title":"the words on the doorpost","word":"the mezuzah","hook":"the little case on the doorframe; what's actually inside it","length":"3:00","carry":"take the words with you","segments":[{"type":"the bell","duration":null,"voice":"","screen":["`DAY ONE.`"]},{"type":"the hook","duration":"25 sec","voice":"That little case on the doorframe. Right side as you walk in, about shoulder height, often tilted. Some people touch it on the way out and kiss their fingertips, as quick as checking for keys. Most people assume the case is the thing. It isn't. The case is just a case. First, a guess: what's inside?","screen":["`THE CASE ON THE DOORFRAME.` → `TOUCHED ON THE WAY OUT.` → `WHAT'S INSIDE?`"]},{"type":"the teach","duration":"1 min 10","voice":"Inside is a tiny scroll, written by hand on parchment by a trained scribe with a quill. It carries a passage from the Torah that opens with six famous words, the Shema: hear, O Israel, the Lord our God, the Lord is one. The same passage says to write these words on the doorposts of your house. So people did, literally. Mezuzah is simply Hebrew for doorpost: the word for the frame became the word for the scroll. On the back, many scribes write one of God's names, Shaddai, which some read as the initials of guardian of the doors of Israel. Now the tilt. A famous medieval teacher, Rashi, said hang it upright. His grandson said lay it flat. Many Ashkenazi Jews, whose families come from central and eastern Europe, hang it at a slant: a little of both. Many Sephardi Jews hang it upright. And the touch? Not a lucky charm; the great teacher Maimonides warned against treating it as one. It's a reminder. Every time you cross the threshold, going out or coming home, you pass the words.","screen":["`A SCROLL, WRITTEN BY HAND.` · `HEAR, O ISRAEL.` · `UPRIGHT OR FLAT? A SLANT.` · `A REMINDER, NOT A CHARM.`"]},{"type":"the practice","duration":"15 sec","voice":"One breath with the bell. Rest your fingertips on the nearest doorframe, or just picture your own front door. Breathe in. As you breathe out, think of one thing you'd like to carry through that door today. Then lift your hand.","screen":[]},{"type":"the word","duration":"10 sec","voice":"The mezuzah. Doorpost, and the handwritten words fixed to it: hear, O Israel. First word on your strand.","screen":["`THE MEZUZAH — your 1st word` → clicks onto an empty word-strand."]},{"type":"the carry","duration":"10 sec","voice":"Your line: take the words with you. That's what the touch on the way out means. See you tomorrow.","screen":[]},{"type":"the close","duration":null,"voice":"","screen":["`DAY 1 COUNTS.` → `TOMORROW: TWO FLAMES, EIGHTEEN MINUTES BEFORE SUNSET.`"]}]},"2":{"day":2,"title":"two flames, eighteen minutes before sunset","word":"the candles","hook":"Friday afternoon, a match, two flames, and hands over the eyes","length":"3:00","carry":"light, then rest","segments":[{"type":"the bell","duration":null,"voice":"","screen":["`DAY TWO.`"]},{"type":"review","duration":"10 sec","voice":"Yesterday: the mezuzah, the words on the doorpost. Your line was take the words with you. Today, two small flames on a Friday.","screen":["`TAKE THE WORDS WITH YOU.` → `DAY 2.`"]},{"type":"the hook","duration":"25 sec","voice":"Friday afternoon, and the clock is running. The oven's on, someone's still in the shower, a kid can't find their shoes. Then, at a minute printed on a calendar on the fridge, someone strikes a match and lights two candles. They circle their hands over the flames, cover their eyes, and whisper a blessing. When they look up, the week is over.","screen":["`FRIDAY, THE CLOCK RUNNING.` → `TWO CANDLES.` → `HANDS OVER THE EYES.`"]},{"type":"the teach","duration":"1 min 10","voice":"This is how Shabbat begins: the day of rest, from Friday sunset until the stars come out on Saturday night. Why sunset? In the Torah's first chapter, each day closes the same way: there was evening and there was morning. Evening comes first, so a day starts in the dark. And since lighting a fire is one of the kinds of work that stop on Shabbat, the candles go on while it's still light, in many towns eighteen minutes early, as a margin. Why two? The rest day is commanded twice, in two wordings: remember the day, and observe it. Zachor and shamor. One flame for each. Then the hands over the eyes. Usually you say a blessing and then do the thing. But once this blessing is said, Shabbat has begun, and no new fire can be lit. So people light first, cover their eyes, bless, and then look, as if the light were new. And why light at all? The rabbis said it's for shalom bayit, peace in the home: a light so the family can see each other's faces at dinner.","screen":["`EVENING FIRST, THEN MORNING.` · `REMEMBER AND OBSERVE.` · `LIGHT, COVER, BLESS, LOOK.` · `PEACE IN THE HOME.`"]},{"type":"the practice","duration":"15 sec","voice":"One breath with the bell. Look at the nearest light: a lamp, a window, even this screen. Breathe in. Close your eyes, the way people do after lighting. As you breathe out, open them slowly, as if the light were new.","screen":[]},{"type":"the word","duration":"10 sec","voice":"The candles. Two flames before sunset on Friday: remember, and observe. Second word.","screen":["`THE CANDLES — your 2nd word`."]},{"type":"the carry","duration":"10 sec","voice":"Your line: light, then rest. First the flame, then the week can stop. That's day two.","screen":[]},{"type":"the close","duration":null,"voice":"","screen":["`DAY 2 COUNTS.` → `TOMORROW: YOU ALREADY SPEAK A LITTLE YIDDISH.`"]}]},"3":{"day":3,"title":"you already speak a little Yiddish","word":"mensch","hook":"mazel tov, oy vey, schlep: a whole language hiding in English","length":"3:00","carry":"be a mensch","segments":[{"type":"the bell","duration":null,"voice":"","screen":["`DAY THREE.`"]},{"type":"review","duration":"10 sec","voice":"Yesterday: the candles, two flames before sunset. Your line was light, then rest. Today, words you may have been saying for years.","screen":["`LIGHT, THEN REST.` → `DAY 3.`"]},{"type":"the hook","duration":"25 sec","voice":"Mazel tov. Oy vey. Schlep, kvetch, chutzpah, nosh. Plenty of people say these without a second thought, Jewish or not. They aren't slang. They're Yiddish, a whole language with its own songs, newspapers and novels, and a lot of English speakers have been using a little of it all along.","screen":["`MAZEL TOV. OY VEY. SCHLEP.` → `NOT SLANG.` → `A WHOLE LANGUAGE.`"]},{"type":"the teach","duration":"1 min 10","voice":"Yiddish was the everyday language of Ashkenazi Jews in central and eastern Europe for centuries. It's built mostly from old German, with Hebrew and Slavic words mixed in, and it's written in Hebrew letters. Immigrants carried it to American cities, and English kept a handful. Schlep: to drag. Kvetch: to complain, at length. Nosh: a little snack. Chutzpah: nerve, rude or brave depending on who's telling it. Oy vey is short for oy vey iz mir: oh, woe is me. And mazel tov? Mazel comes from a Hebrew word for a star, and luck; tov means good. Good luck, word for word. But it isn't said before the exam. It's said after the good news: a birth, a wedding, a new job. It means: a good thing happened. Then there's the word worth keeping. In German, Mensch just means a person. In Yiddish, a mensch is a real human being: decent, honest, someone who does the right thing when nobody's checking. The great rabbi Hillel taught it long before Yiddish existed: where nobody is acting like a person should, try to be that person.","screen":["`A LANGUAGE IN HEBREW LETTERS.` · `SCHLEP, KVETCH, NOSH.` · `MAZEL TOV — AFTER, NOT BEFORE.` · `A MENSCH — A REAL HUMAN BEING.`"]},{"type":"the practice","duration":"20 sec","voice":"Three breaths with the bell. First breath: someone you'd call a mensch. Second breath: one thing they did when nobody was checking. Third breath: let the word land on them, quietly, like a compliment they'll never hear.","screen":[]},{"type":"the word","duration":"10 sec","voice":"Mensch. A real human being: decent, honest, there when it counts. Third word.","screen":["`MENSCH — your 3rd word`."]},{"type":"the carry","duration":"10 sec","voice":"Your line: be a mensch. Of everything Yiddish lent to English, that may be the best one. Day three, done.","screen":[]},{"type":"the close","duration":null,"voice":"","screen":["`DAY 3 COUNTS.` → `TOMORROW: THE PLATE THAT TELLS A STORY.`"]}]},"4":{"day":4,"title":"the plate that tells a story","word":"seder","hook":"a bone, an egg, parsley and horseradish: the Passover plate, and why the answer starts with we","length":"3:00","carry":"we were slaves","segments":[{"type":"the bell","duration":null,"voice":"","screen":["`DAY FOUR.`"]},{"type":"review","duration":"10 sec","voice":"Yesterday: mensch, a decent human being. Your line was be a mensch. Today, a plate where every food is part of a story.","screen":["`BE A MENSCH.` → `DAY 4.`"]},{"type":"the hook","duration":"25 sec","voice":"A spring night, a long table, chairs borrowed from the neighbors. In the middle sits a plate with odd little things on it: a roasted bone, an egg, some parsley, horseradish, a brown sweet paste. Nobody's eating them yet. Then the youngest kid stands up, a little nervous, and asks: why is this night different from all other nights?","screen":["`A BONE, AN EGG, PARSLEY.` → `THE YOUNGEST STANDS UP.` → `WHY IS THIS NIGHT DIFFERENT?`"]},{"type":"the teach","duration":"1 min 10","voice":"Seder means order. Passover night follows a set order of steps, read from a little book called the Haggadah, which means the telling. The story it tells is the Exodus: slaves in Egypt, a stubborn king, ten plagues, and a night of leaving so rushed the bread had no time to rise. That's the matzah. The plate is the story laid out as food. Bitter herbs, often horseradish, for how bitter slavery was. Charoset, a sweet paste often made of apples, nuts and wine, looks like the mortar the slaves used for bricks. A green vegetable, often parsley, dipped in salt water, like tears. The roasted bone recalls the lamb of the first Passover; the egg, an offering brought on festivals long ago. You taste the story before anyone explains it. Then comes the surprise. The answer to the child's question doesn't start with once upon a time. It starts: we were slaves. We. The old rule is that in every generation, each person should see themselves as if they personally walked out of Egypt. Not a history lesson. A memory you're handed.","screen":["`SEDER — ORDER.` · `THE BREAD THAT DIDN'T RISE.` · `THE STORY LAID OUT AS FOOD.` · `WE WERE SLAVES.`"]},{"type":"the practice","duration":"20 sec","voice":"Three breaths with the bell. First breath: something bitter from this year. Second breath: something sweet that came anyway. Third breath: hold the two together, the bitter and the sweet, the way the plate does.","screen":[]},{"type":"the word","duration":"10 sec","voice":"Seder. Order: Passover night told step by step, with the story laid out on a plate. Fourth word.","screen":["`SEDER — your 4th word`."]},{"type":"the carry","duration":"10 sec","voice":"Your line: we were slaves. Not they. We. That one word carries the whole night. Day four.","screen":[]},{"type":"the close","duration":null,"voice":"","screen":["`DAY 4 COUNTS.` → `TOMORROW: WHY THE GLASS BREAKS.`"]}]},"5":{"day":5,"title":"why the glass breaks","word":"the glass","hook":"one stomp, a crunch, and everyone shouts mazel tov: why break something at a wedding?","length":"3:00","carry":"even in joy, remember","segments":[{"type":"the bell","duration":null,"voice":"","screen":["`DAY FIVE.`"]},{"type":"review","duration":"10 sec","voice":"Yesterday: the seder, a story laid out as food. Your line was we were slaves. Today, the loudest second of a Jewish wedding.","screen":["`WE WERE SLAVES.` → `DAY 5.`"]},{"type":"the hook","duration":"25 sec","voice":"The end of the ceremony. The couple stands under a canopy. Someone sets a glass, wrapped in a cloth, on the floor. One stomp, a crunch, and the whole room shouts mazel tov. Plenty of people have cheered for it without knowing why it happens. And there's more than one answer: families tell it different ways.","screen":["`A GLASS IN A CLOTH.` → `ONE STOMP.` → `MAZEL TOV!`"]},{"type":"the teach","duration":"1 min 10","voice":"Start with the canopy. It's called a chuppah: four poles and a cloth, open on every side, like the roof of a home that's just beginning. Then the glass. Its roots go back to rabbis in ancient Babylon who broke a precious cup at a wedding feast, on purpose, when the celebrating got out of hand. Later teachers tied the custom to Jerusalem, and the Temple that stood there, destroyed nearly two thousand years ago. An old psalm promises to keep Jerusalem above even your highest joy. So at the happiest moment, something breaks: a reminder that even on the best day, something in the world is still broken. Families add their own readings. Love is precious and breakable, so handle it with care. A glass, once broken, stays broken; the promise is meant to be just as permanent. In some old customs, the noise scared off bad luck. Traditionally the groom breaks it; at many weddings today, either partner does, or both together. More than one meaning can be true at once, and a Jewish wedding is happy to hold them all. Then comes the shout, the words you met two days ago: mazel tov.","screen":["`THE CHUPPAH — OPEN ON EVERY SIDE.` · `A PRECIOUS CUP, BROKEN.` · `REMEMBER JERUSALEM.` · `MORE THAN ONE MEANING.`"]},{"type":"the practice","duration":"20 sec","voice":"Three breaths with the bell. First breath: the happiest moment you can remember. Second breath: someone or something missing from it. Third breath: let the two sit side by side, without either one canceling the other.","screen":[]},{"type":"the word","duration":"10 sec","voice":"The glass. Broken at the height of the joy, so the joy remembers. Fifth word.","screen":["`THE GLASS — your 5th word`."]},{"type":"the carry","duration":"10 sec","voice":"Your line: even in joy, remember. The crunch comes right before the cheering, never instead of it. That's day five.","screen":[]},{"type":"the close","duration":null,"voice":"","screen":["`DAY 5 COUNTS.` → `TOMORROW: NOT JUST A PARTY.`"]}]},"6":{"day":6,"title":"not just a party","word":"mitzvah","hook":"a thirteen-year-old, an ancient scroll, then the DJ: which part is the real thing?","length":"3:00","carry":"it's yours now","segments":[{"type":"the bell","duration":null,"voice":"","screen":["`DAY SIX.`"]},{"type":"review","duration":"10 sec","voice":"Yesterday: the glass, broken right before the cheering. Your line was even in joy, remember. Today, a day many people remember for the DJ.","screen":["`EVEN IN JOY, REMEMBER.` → `DAY 6.`"]},{"type":"the hook","duration":"25 sec","voice":"A thirteen-year-old in a suit that doesn't quite fit, or a dress picked out months ago, stands in front of everyone they know and chants from a handwritten scroll, in Hebrew they've practiced for a year. Then comes the party: the DJ, the candy bar, the chair lifted into the air. So which part was the real thing? Neither one, it turns out, makes it happen.","screen":["`THIRTEEN, AND A SCROLL.` → `THEN THE DJ.` → `WHICH PART IS REAL?`"]},{"type":"the teach","duration":"1 min 10","voice":"Bar mitzvah means son of the commandment. Bat mitzvah, daughter of the commandment. A mitzvah is one of the things the Torah asks a person to do, and in everyday talk it's also a good deed: that was a real mitzvah. Here's the twist. You don't have a bar or bat mitzvah. You become one. In traditional Jewish law it happens on its own, on a birthday: thirteen for boys, twelve for girls, and many communities mark both at thirteen. Ceremony or no ceremony, party or no party, the day comes. An old teaching lays out a life by ages: five for starting scripture, ten for the rabbis' teachings, thirteen for the commandments. Until then, the responsibility sat with your parents. From that birthday on, it's yours. So the ceremony isn't what makes it happen. It's the first time it's shown in public: called up to read from the Torah, often with a short talk about what that week's reading means to you. And the party? Celebrating a mitzvah with a festive meal is a tradition too. The DJ is newer. Some people take it up as adults, at thirty or sixty or eighty. There's no deadline.","screen":["`SON OR DAUGHTER OF THE COMMANDMENT.` · `YOU BECOME ONE.` · `THIRTEEN FOR THE COMMANDMENTS.` · `SHOWN IN PUBLIC, THEN A MEAL.`"]},{"type":"the practice","duration":"20 sec","voice":"Three breaths with the bell. First breath: think back to being twelve or thirteen. Second breath: one thing you're responsible for now that you weren't then. Third breath: let it be yours, without the weight. Just yours.","screen":[]},{"type":"the word","duration":"10 sec","voice":"Mitzvah. A commandment, a good deed, and at a bar or bat mitzvah, a responsibility that becomes yours. Sixth word.","screen":["`MITZVAH — your 6th word`."]},{"type":"the carry","duration":"10 sec","voice":"Your line: it's yours now. The day comes on its own; the ceremony just says so out loud. Day six.","screen":[]},{"type":"the close","duration":null,"voice":"","screen":["`DAY 6 COUNTS.` → `TOMORROW: AN ARGUMENT FOR THE SAKE OF HEAVEN.`"]}]},"7":{"day":7,"title":"an argument for the sake of heaven","word":"machloket","hook":"a holy book that writes down its arguments, and keeps the losing side","length":"3:00","carry":"argue to learn, not to win","segments":[{"type":"the bell","duration":null,"voice":"","screen":["`DAY SEVEN.`"]},{"type":"review","duration":"10 sec","voice":"Yesterday: mitzvah, a responsibility that becomes yours. Your line was it's yours now. Six words this week: the mezuzah, the candles, mensch, seder, the glass, mitzvah. Today, the seventh.","screen":["`MEZUZAH · CANDLES · MENSCH · SEDER · THE GLASS · MITZVAH`"]},{"type":"the hook","duration":"25 sec","voice":"Open a page of the Talmud, the great library of the rabbis' discussions, and you'll find something you might not expect in a holy book: arguments. One rabbi says this. Another says no, that. Then a third weighs in. And very often the side that lost is written down too, and kept, for centuries. A holy book that saves its disagreements.","screen":["`A PAGE OF THE TALMUD.` → `ONE SAYS THIS, ONE SAYS THAT.` → `THE LOSING SIDE, KEPT.`"]},{"type":"the teach","duration":"1 min 10","voice":"Machloket means a disagreement. And there's a famous pair at the center of it: Hillel and Shammai, two great teachers about two thousand years ago, and the schools of students who followed them. You met Hillel on day three. The two schools argued for years, about prayer, about Shabbat, about how to live. Finally, the story goes, a voice from heaven settled it: both of these are the words of the living God. And the law follows Hillel. Wait. Both are right, but one wins? Why Hillel? Because, the story says, his students were kind and humble. They taught Shammai's view along with their own, and even taught Shammai's first. An old teaching names the difference between a good argument and a bad one. An argument for the sake of heaven will last, and its example is Hillel and Shammai. An argument to win, to beat someone, won't. Remember the slanted mezuzah from day one: an argument where nobody had to lose. So when Jews argue over a text, often out loud, in pairs, across a table, it isn't a lack of faith. It's how people learn.","screen":["`MACHLOKET — A DISAGREEMENT.` · `HILLEL AND SHAMMAI.` · `BOTH ARE THE WORDS OF THE LIVING GOD.` · `FOR THE SAKE OF HEAVEN.`"]},{"type":"the practice","duration":"20 sec","voice":"Three breaths with the bell. First breath: someone you disagree with. Second breath: the best version of their side, put as fairly as you can. Third breath: notice that you can hold it without letting go of your own.","screen":[]},{"type":"the word","duration":"10 sec","voice":"Machloket. A disagreement, and in this tradition a way of learning: argued for the sake of heaven, with both sides kept. Seventh word: a week's worth.","screen":["`MACHLOKET — your 7th word`."]},{"type":"the carry","duration":"10 sec","voice":"Your line: argue to learn, not to win. That's week one: seven words, and what's inside each. Tomorrow, a man who argued with God himself.","screen":[]},{"type":"the close","duration":null,"voice":"","screen":["`DAY 7 COUNTS.` → `TOMORROW: THE MAN WHO ARGUED WITH GOD ABOUT SODOM.`"]}]},"8":{"day":8,"title":"the man who argued with God about Sodom","word":"Abraham","hook":"the man who argued with God about Sodom","length":"6:45","carry":"you're allowed to argue","segments":[{"type":"the bell","duration":null,"voice":"","screen":["`DAY EIGHT.`"]},{"type":"review","duration":"20 sec","voice":"Yesterday: machloket, rabbis arguing for the sake of heaven. Your line was argue to learn, not to win. Week two starts with a bigger argument. Day eight.","screen":["`ARGUE TO LEARN, NOT TO WIN.` → `WEEK TWO.`"]},{"type":"the hook","duration":"50 sec","voice":"An old man is sitting at the door of his tent in the heat of the day. Three visitors arrive, he feeds them, and as they leave, God tells him something. The cities of the plain, Sodom and Gomorrah, have done terrible things, and God is going to see about them. He could bow, or nod, or quietly go back inside. He doesn't. The text says he comes closer. And then he starts to argue. With God. Today: an argument with God, and the man who made it.","screen":["`A TENT IN THE HEAT OF THE DAY.` → `GOD SPEAKS OF SODOM.` → `ABRAHAM COMES CLOSER.`"]},{"type":"the teach","duration":"2 min 15","voice":"Abraham. Born Abram, the text says, and renamed when God promised he'd be the father of many nations. Jews call him Avraham avinu, Abraham our father. And here, he bargains. What if there are fifty good people in the city? Will you sweep them away with the rest? Then comes the line at the center of the story: \"Shall not the Judge of all the earth do justly?\" That takes nerve. He's holding God to God's own standard. And God answers: for fifty, I'll spare it. So Abraham keeps going. Forty-five? Forty? Thirty? Twenty? Each time he gets more polite, and more stubborn. He calls himself \"but dust and ashes,\" and still doesn't back down. He stops at ten. Why ten? The text doesn't say. Some teachers note that ten is the smallest number that makes a community. In the end, ten good people aren't found. But the argument is never scolded. God listens to every round. And Abraham isn't the last. Moses argues with God later. So does Job. The psalms complain out loud. There's a Hasidic story of a rabbi who put God on trial on behalf of his people. Arguing with God, in this tradition, isn't the opposite of faith. It's one of the ways faith talks.","screen":["`ABRAM BECOMES ABRAHAM.` · `WHAT IF THERE ARE FIFTY?` · `FROM FIFTY DOWN TO TEN.` · `ARGUING IS HOW FAITH TALKS.` · then, large: `SHALL NOT THE JUDGE OF ALL THE EARTH DO JUSTLY?`"]},{"type":"the practice","duration":"1 min 30","voice":"Forty-five seconds, with the bell. Bring to mind one thing that seems unfair to you right now, in your life or in the world. Don't smooth it over. Say it plainly, inside. If you pray, you can say it to God the way Abraham did: direct, respectful, and not backing down. If you don't, say it honestly to yourself.","screen":[]},{"type":"the word","duration":"25 sec","voice":"Abraham. Father of many nations, who argued with God on behalf of strangers. Eighth word.","screen":["`ABRAHAM — your 8th word`."]},{"type":"the carry","duration":"15 sec","voice":"Your line: you're allowed to argue. Direct, respectful, and on behalf of someone else: that's how Abraham did it. Day eight.","screen":[]},{"type":"the close","duration":null,"voice":"","screen":["`DAY 8 COUNTS.` → `TOMORROW: WRESTLING ALL NIGHT, LIMPING, RENAMED.`"]}]},"9":{"day":9,"title":"wrestling all night, limping, renamed","word":"Jacob","hook":"wrestling all night, limping, renamed","length":"6:45","carry":"you'll limp and you'll be blessed","segments":[{"type":"the bell","duration":null,"voice":"","screen":["`DAY NINE.`"]},{"type":"review","duration":"20 sec","voice":"Yesterday: Abraham, bargaining with God from fifty down to ten. Your line was you're allowed to argue. Today, his grandson goes further. Day nine.","screen":["`YOU'RE ALLOWED TO ARGUE.` → `DAY 9.`"]},{"type":"the hook","duration":"50 sec","voice":"It's the night before the hardest meeting of his life. Twenty years ago Jacob cheated his twin brother Esau out of their father's blessing and ran. Now he's coming home, and Esau is on the road with four hundred men. Jacob sends his family across a river called the Jabbok and stays behind, alone, in the dark. And then, the text says, a man wrestles with him until the break of dawn. No name. No warning. No explanation. Just two bodies in the dirt all night long. Today: the wrestler, and the name the whole people still carries.","screen":["`TWENTY YEARS AFTER THE LIE.` → `ALONE AT THE RIVER.` → `A MAN WRESTLES HIM TILL DAWN.`"]},{"type":"the teach","duration":"2 min 15","voice":"Jacob. In Hebrew, Yaakov, from the word for heel, because he was born holding on to his twin brother's heel. He's been grabbing ever since. Who is he fighting? The text calls him a man. The prophet Hosea calls him an angel. Jacob himself names the place Peniel, the face of God, because he says he saw God face to face. One old midrash says it was Esau's angel. The Torah doesn't settle it, and maybe that's the point: some nights you can't tell who you're fighting. As dawn comes, the stranger can't win, so he touches Jacob's hip and wrenches it. And Jacob, hurt, still won't let go: \"I will not let thee go, except thou bless me.\" The blessing is a new name. \"Thy name shall be called no more Jacob, but Israel; for thou hast striven with God and with men, and hast prevailed.\" Israel: one who wrestles with God. A whole people named not for someone who obeyed quietly, or never doubted, but for someone who fought all night and held on. And he doesn't walk away clean. The sun rises, and Jacob is limping. The Torah says that's why the children of Israel don't eat the sinew of the thigh, and observant Jews still don't. A food law that remembers an injury. He's hurt, and he's renamed, in the same morning.","screen":["`YAAKOV — THE ONE WHO GRABS THE HEEL.` · `A MAN, AN ANGEL, OR GOD?` · `ISRAEL — WRESTLES WITH GOD.` · `THE BLESSING AND THE LIMP.` · then, large: `I WILL NOT LET THEE GO, EXCEPT THOU BLESS ME.`"]},{"type":"the practice","duration":"1 min 30","voice":"Forty-five seconds, with the bell. Think of one thing you're wrestling with right now: a decision, a person, a question. Don't try to win it in the next minute. Just hold on to it, the way Jacob held on, and ask what blessing might be in it. If you pray, you can ask for that blessing.","screen":[]},{"type":"the word","duration":"25 sec","voice":"Jacob. The heel-grabber who wrestled until dawn, came away limping, and was renamed Israel. Ninth word.","screen":["`JACOB — your 9th word`."]},{"type":"the carry","duration":"15 sec","voice":"Your line: you'll limp and you'll be blessed. The mark and the new name came in the same morning. See you tomorrow.","screen":[]},{"type":"the close","duration":null,"voice":"","screen":["`DAY 9 COUNTS.` → `TOMORROW: \"WE WILL DO AND WE WILL HEAR\".`"]}]},"10":{"day":10,"title":"\"we will do and we will hear\"","word":"Sinai","hook":"\"we will do and we will hear\" — practice before belief","length":"6:45","carry":"do first","segments":[{"type":"the bell","duration":null,"voice":"","screen":["`DAY TEN.`"]},{"type":"review","duration":"20 sec","voice":"Yesterday: Jacob, who wrestled till dawn and was renamed Israel. Your line was you'll limp and you'll be blessed. Today, his descendants, out of Egypt, at a mountain. Day ten.","screen":["`YOU'LL LIMP AND YOU'LL BE BLESSED.` → `DAY 10.`"]},{"type":"the hook","duration":"50 sec","voice":"Three months out of Egypt, a crowd of former slaves is camped at the foot of a mountain in the desert. They've been told to wash, to wait, to keep back from the slopes. On the third morning there's thunder, lightning, a thick cloud on the peak, and the sound of a horn growing louder and louder until the whole camp is shaking. Later, what this new life with God will ask is read out loud to them. And the people give an answer, all together, that the rabbis would keep coming back to. It's two words in Hebrew, and they're in the wrong order. Today: the mountain, and the answer.","screen":["`A MOUNTAIN IN THE DESERT.` → `THUNDER, CLOUD, A HORN.` → `AN ANSWER IN THE WRONG ORDER.`"]},{"type":"the teach","duration":"2 min 15","voice":"Sinai is the mountain where, in the Torah's story, God gives the Torah to Israel. Where exactly it stood is uncertain, and the text isn't interested in the map. What matters is what happens there. After the laws are read, the people say: \"All that the LORD hath spoken will we do, and obey.\" In Hebrew: na'aseh v'nishma. We will do, and we will hear. Look at the order. The usual order runs the other way: explain it to me first, then I'll decide whether to do it. Here the doing comes first, and the understanding follows. The rabbis tell a story about that moment: when Israel put doing before hearing, a voice from heaven asked, who revealed to my children this secret that the angels use? Teachers hear in it practice before belief. You light the candles, as on day two, before you have a theory of candles. The meaning grows out of the doing. The rabbis also argued about that day, and they wrote the argument down. One teacher said the mountain was held over the people like a barrel, as if they had no real choice. Others answered that they accepted freely, and accepted again in later generations. Both readings are kept. And one more teaching: every Jewish soul, even of people not yet born, stood at Sinai, converts too. The whole people, past and future, at the foot of one mountain. Jews mark that day every year, at the festival of Shavuot.","screen":["`THE MOUNTAIN WHERE TORAH IS GIVEN.` · `NA'ASEH V'NISHMA — DO, THEN HEAR.` · `THE SECRET THE ANGELS USE.` · `EVERY SOUL STOOD THERE.` · then, large: `WE WILL DO, AND WE WILL HEAR.`"]},{"type":"the practice","duration":"1 min 30","voice":"Forty-five seconds, with the bell. Think of one good thing you've been putting off until you understand it better, or feel more ready. A call. An apology. A habit. Picture yourself doing it first, and letting the understanding come afterward.","screen":[]},{"type":"the word","duration":"25 sec","voice":"Sinai. The mountain of the covenant, where the people answered: we will do, and we will hear. Tenth word.","screen":["`SINAI — your 10th word`."]},{"type":"the carry","duration":"15 sec","voice":"Your line: do first. The understanding tends to arrive a little later. Day ten, done.","screen":[]},{"type":"the close","duration":null,"voice":"","screen":["`DAY 10 COUNTS.` → `TOMORROW: THE OLDEST LABOR LAW.`"]}]},"11":{"day":11,"title":"the oldest labor law","word":"Shabbat","hook":"the oldest labor law; a day the world can't have","length":"6:45","carry":"stop. it's already Friday.","segments":[{"type":"the bell","duration":null,"voice":"","screen":["`DAY ELEVEN.`"]},{"type":"review","duration":"20 sec","voice":"Yesterday: Sinai, and the answer we will do, and we will hear. Your line was do first. On day two you lit the candles; today, the day they open. Day eleven.","screen":["`DO FIRST.` → `DAY 11.`"]},{"type":"the hook","duration":"50 sec","voice":"A phone on a Saturday looks like a phone on a Tuesday. Work email. The group chat. A sale that ends tonight. The world never really closes anymore. Now take a family that, every week, from Friday sunset until the stars come out on Saturday, lets none of that in. No office. No shopping. In some homes, not even a light switch. Not a holiday, not a sick day. Just the seventh day, arriving on schedule. Some people call it the oldest labor law there is. Today: the day the world can't have.","screen":["`THE WORLD NEVER CLOSES.` → `A DAY IT CAN'T REACH.` → `THE SEVENTH DAY.`"]},{"type":"the teach","duration":"2 min 15","voice":"Shabbat comes from a Hebrew root, shin, bet, tav, that means to stop. To cease. Not first to relax, or to worship: to stop. At the end of the creation story, even God stops, and the verb there comes from the same root. English borrowed the word as Sabbath. Then the rabbis asked a practical question: what counts as work? They listed thirty-nine kinds: planting, baking, writing, building, lighting a fire, and more, drawn from the work it took to build the tent for God in the desert. So work, on Shabbat, means making and changing things, not how tired you get. Now the part that earns the nickname. The rest day is commanded twice, and the second time it gives a different reason. Not creation. Egypt. You were slaves, so rest, \"that thy man-servant and thy maid-servant may rest as well as thou.\" As well as you. Not after you. And the list of who rests keeps going: your children, the stranger in your town, even the ox and the donkey. A weekly day off, owed to everyone, written into an ancient law. Jews keep the day in different ways. Orthodox Jews keep the thirty-nine categories carefully: no driving, no cooking, no phones. Conservative, Reform and other Jews keep Shabbat in their own ways, through the candles, the meal, synagogue and a slower day, with each community drawing its own lines. The twentieth-century rabbi Abraham Joshua Heschel described Shabbat as a palace built in time rather than in space. You don't travel to it. It arrives, every week, wherever you are.","screen":["`SHABBAT — TO STOP.` · `THIRTY-NINE KINDS OF WORK.` · `REST, BECAUSE YOU WERE SLAVES.` · `A PALACE IN TIME.` · then, large: `AS WELL AS THOU.`"]},{"type":"the practice","duration":"1 min 30","voice":"Forty-five seconds, with the bell. Put one thing down with each breath. The thing you're working on. The thing you're worried about. The phone, if it's in your hand. For these few breaths, nothing is being made or fixed. That's the whole idea of Shabbat, very small.","screen":[]},{"type":"the word","duration":"25 sec","voice":"Shabbat. To stop: the seventh day, Friday sunset to Saturday stars, a rest owed to everyone in the house. Eleventh word.","screen":["`SHABBAT — your 11th word`."]},{"type":"the carry","duration":"15 sec","voice":"Your line: stop. it's already Friday. Rest isn't a prize for finishing; it's owed. Day eleven.","screen":[]},{"type":"the close","duration":null,"voice":"","screen":["`DAY 11 COUNTS.` → `TOMORROW: HELLO, GOODBYE, PEACE.`"]}]},"12":{"day":12,"title":"hello, goodbye, peace","word":"shalom","hook":"hello, goodbye, peace — and a fourth meaning: whole","length":"6:45","carry":"be whole","segments":[{"type":"the bell","duration":null,"voice":"","screen":["`DAY TWELVE.`"]},{"type":"review","duration":"20 sec","voice":"Yesterday: Shabbat, the day of stopping. Your line was stop. it's already Friday. Today, the word people say once the candles are lit: Shabbat shalom. Day twelve.","screen":["`STOP. IT'S ALREADY FRIDAY.` → `DAY 12.`"]},{"type":"the hook","duration":"50 sec","voice":"One word, three jobs. Someone walks in: shalom. Someone leaves: shalom. On a card, at the end of a letter, over a door: shalom, peace. Hello, goodbye, peace. The same word opens a conversation and closes it. But there's a fourth meaning hiding underneath the other three, and it's the one the rest of them grow from. Today, the wish folded inside the hello.","screen":["`HELLO. GOODBYE. PEACE.` → `ONE WORD, THREE JOBS.` → `AND A FOURTH MEANING.`"]},{"type":"the teach","duration":"2 min 15","voice":"Shalom is Hebrew, and it grows from a root of three letters, shin, lamed, mem, and the root's first sense isn't quiet. It's whole. Complete. Nothing missing. The same root gives shalem, whole, and a verb for paying back what you owe, so the account is square again. So peace, in this language, isn't only the absence of a fight. It's the presence of everything that should be there. A house with shalom has nothing broken in it. A body with shalom is well. In the Torah, when Jacob sends his son Joseph to check on his brothers, the Hebrew says, more or less: go and see the shalom of your brothers. How whole are they? The word runs all through Jewish prayer. The priestly blessing, said over congregations, and in some homes over the children on Friday night, ends with it: \"and give thee peace.\" Peace is the last word. And an old psalm doesn't treat peace as something that just happens to you: \"Seek peace, and pursue it.\" The rabbis noticed that verb. Other commandments, they taught, you do when the chance comes to you. Peace you go after. So when someone says shalom at a door, coming or going, underneath the hello is an old wish: may you be whole. May nothing be missing.","screen":["`SH-L-M — WHOLE, COMPLETE.` · `SEE THE SHALOM OF YOUR BROTHERS.` · `THE BLESSING ENDS IN PEACE.` · `SEEK PEACE, AND PURSUE IT.` · then, large: `MAY NOTHING BE MISSING.`"]},{"type":"the practice","duration":"1 min 30","voice":"Forty-five seconds, with the bell. Breathe slowly, and notice one place in you that feels not quite whole right now. Tired. Worried. Rushed. Don't fix it; just let it be noticed. On the last breath, if you want to, say the word, out loud or inside: shalom. Not to fix anything. Just to wish it whole.","screen":[]},{"type":"the word","duration":"25 sec","voice":"Shalom. Hello, goodbye, peace, and underneath all three: whole, with nothing missing. Twelfth word.","screen":["`SHALOM — your 12th word`."]},{"type":"the carry","duration":"15 sec","voice":"Your line: be whole. It's the wish folded inside every hello and goodbye. That's day twelve.","screen":[]},{"type":"the close","duration":null,"voice":"","screen":["`DAY 12 COUNTS.` → `TOMORROW: SIX WORDS.`"]}]},"13":{"day":13,"title":"six words","word":"the Shema","hook":"six words; the sentence a Jew says with eyes covered","length":"6:45","carry":"hear","segments":[{"type":"the bell","duration":null,"voice":"","screen":["`DAY THIRTEEN.`"]},{"type":"review","duration":"20 sec","voice":"Yesterday: shalom, and underneath the hello, whole. Your line was be whole. Today, the words rolled up inside the mezuzah from day one. Day thirteen.","screen":["`BE WHOLE.` → `DAY 13.`"]},{"type":"the hook","duration":"50 sec","voice":"A synagogue on an ordinary morning. People are reading, murmuring, some standing, some swaying. Then, at one moment, many of them do the same small thing. They raise a hand and cover their eyes. For a few seconds a room full of adults sits like children playing hide and seek. And under the hands, six Hebrew words. Some children learn it as their first prayer, at bedtime, and tradition hopes it will be among the last words a person says in this life. Six words, said at the beginning and the end. Today: the sentence under the hand.","screen":["`A HAND OVER THE EYES.` → `SIX HEBREW WORDS.` → `THE FIRST PRAYER AND THE LAST.`"]},{"type":"the teach","duration":"2 min 15","voice":"The six words are: Shema Yisrael, Adonai Eloheinu, Adonai echad. \"Hear, O Israel: the LORD our God, the LORD is one.\" They come from the Torah, from the same passage that's rolled up inside the mezuzah, and the prayer is named for its first word. Shema. Hear. But the Hebrew verb is bigger than hearing with your ears. It means listen, pay attention, understand, even take it to heart and act on it. It's not here is a fact. It's are you listening? Notice who it's said to. Not to God. To Israel, to the people, to the person saying it. It's a sentence you say to yourself, out loud, to wake yourself up. A detail about the name. The word written there is God's four-letter name, which Jewish tradition doesn't pronounce. Reading aloud, people say Adonai, my Lord, instead. And a detail about the page. In a handwritten Torah scroll, two letters in this verse are written extra large: the last letter of the first word and the last letter of the last word. Together they spell ed, the Hebrew word for witness. Saying it, some teachers say, is a kind of testimony. Why the hand over the eyes? An old story remembers that Rabbi Judah the Prince, whom tradition credits with gathering the rabbis' early teachings into one book, passed his hand over his eyes when he said it. People still do: shut out everything you can see, just for a moment, so you can hear. The same passage says to speak these words when you lie down and when you rise up. So it's said morning and night, at bedtime, and at the end.","screen":["`SHEMA — HEAR, LISTEN, TAKE IT IN.` · `SAID TO ISRAEL, NOT TO GOD.` · `TWO BIG LETTERS SPELL WITNESS.` · `A HAND OVER THE EYES, TO HEAR.` · then, large: `HEAR, O ISRAEL.`"]},{"type":"the practice","duration":"1 min 30","voice":"Forty-five seconds, with the bell. Cover your eyes with one hand, the way people do for the Shema. Let the room disappear. Breathe slowly and just listen, to whatever sound is left: a fan, traffic, your own breath. If you pray, you can say the six words on the way out. If not, let the breath out and simply hear.","screen":[]},{"type":"the word","duration":"25 sec","voice":"The Shema. Six words that begin hear, O Israel, said morning and night with the eyes covered. Thirteenth word.","screen":["`THE SHEMA — your 13th word`."]},{"type":"the carry","duration":"15 sec","voice":"Your line: hear. Not only with the ears: listen, and take it in. Day thirteen.","screen":[]},{"type":"the close","duration":null,"voice":"","screen":["`DAY 13 COUNTS.` → `TOMORROW: INTENTION.`"]}]},"14":{"day":14,"title":"intention","word":"kavanah","hook":"intention; the difference between saying and meaning","length":"6:45","carry":"mean it","segments":[{"type":"the bell","duration":null,"voice":"","screen":["`DAY FOURTEEN.`"]},{"type":"review","duration":"20 sec","voice":"Yesterday: the Shema, six words said with the eyes covered. Your line was hear. Day fourteen, the end of week two.","screen":["`HEAR.` → `DAY 14.`"]},{"type":"the hook","duration":"50 sec","voice":"Someone drives home and pulls into the driveway without remembering a single turn. Someone reads a whole page, reaches the bottom, and has no idea what it said. Someone says love you at the end of a phone call the way they'd say bye. The mouth did the job. Nobody was there. Now imagine a tradition with words it says every day, morning and night, for a lifetime. The danger isn't forgetting them. It's saying them perfectly while you're somewhere else entirely. Judaism has a word for what's missing. Today: intention.","screen":["`HOME, AND NO MEMORY OF THE DRIVE.` → `THE MOUTH DID THE JOB.` → `NOBODY WAS THERE.`"]},{"type":"the teach","duration":"2 min 15","voice":"Kavanah comes from a root that means to aim, to direct, to point something at a target. Intention is aiming your heart where your words are going. The rabbis, discussing the Shema from yesterday, said that if a person directed their heart while reading it, they had done what was asked. They also remembered that the early pious ones would wait an hour before praying, just to direct their hearts. An hour of aiming, for a few minutes of prayer. The Bible's clearest picture of kavanah is a woman named Hannah, praying at the sanctuary for a child. She speaks in her heart; only her lips move, and no sound comes out. The priest thinks she's drunk. She isn't. She's praying as hard as anyone ever has, and the rabbis learned much of how to pray from her. One old line puts it simply: the Merciful One wants the heart. Then the honest part. The rabbis argued about whether a commandment done without intention still counts, and the discussion went on for centuries. Many say yes, the act counts, but it isn't the whole thing. And one teaching holds both sides together: do the good thing even when your heart isn't in it, because from doing it without the right intention, you come to do it with the right one. That's day ten again, do first, with a promise attached. This week you met Abraham, Jacob, Sinai, Shabbat, shalom, the Shema, and now the thing that makes any of them more than motions.","screen":["`KAVANAH — TO AIM THE HEART.` · `AN HOUR OF AIMING FIRST.` · `HANNAH'S LIPS MOVE, NO SOUND.` · `DOING LEADS TO MEANING.` · then, large: `THE MERCIFUL ONE WANTS THE HEART.`"]},{"type":"the practice","duration":"1 min 30","voice":"Forty-five seconds, with the bell. Pick one short thing you say every day without thinking: thank you, good morning, love you, or, if you pray, the Shema. For the first part of the sit, just aim: who is it for, and what do you actually mean by it? Then say it once, slowly, meaning every word.","screen":[]},{"type":"the word","duration":"25 sec","voice":"Kavanah. Intention: aiming your heart where your words and actions are going. Fourteenth word. Two weeks.","screen":["`KAVANAH — your 14th word`."]},{"type":"the carry","duration":"15 sec","voice":"Your line: mean it. Two weeks in, that's the thread running through all of them. Week two, done.","screen":[]},{"type":"the close","duration":null,"voice":"","screen":["`DAY 14 COUNTS.` → `TOMORROW: THE BLESSING BEFORE EATING.`"]}]},"15":{"day":15,"title":"the blessing before eating","word":"bracha","hook":"the blessing before eating; saying thank you to the source","length":"7:00","carry":"notice before you bite","segments":[{"type":"the bell","duration":null,"voice":"","screen":["`DAY FIFTEEN.`"]},{"type":"review","duration":"20 sec","voice":"Yesterday: kavanah, aiming the heart where the words go. Your line was mean it. Week three starts at the table. Day fifteen.","screen":["`MEAN IT.` → `WEEK THREE.`"]},{"type":"the hook","duration":"50 sec","voice":"Watch someone who keeps this tradition pick up an apple. Before the first bite there's a pause, about two seconds long, and a murmur you'd miss if you weren't looking. Then the bite. Same with a glass of water. A cookie. A piece of bread at a wedding. A lot of eating happens the way scrolling does: mid-sentence, standing up, already on to the next thing. This is the opposite. A tiny speed bump between wanting something and having it. And the words inside that pause have a surprising reason behind them. Today: the two-second blessing.","screen":["`AN APPLE. A PAUSE.` → `TWO SECONDS, A MURMUR.` → `THEN THE BITE.`"]},{"type":"the teach","duration":"2 min 15","voice":"A bracha is a blessing. The standard form opens with the same words each time: baruch atah Adonai, eloheinu melech ha-olam, roughly, blessed are you, Lord our God, ruler of the world. Then the ending changes with the food. For bread: who brings forth bread from the earth. For fruit: who creates the fruit of the tree. For water, or almost anything else: by whose word all things came to be. Here's the surprise. It's not the food being blessed. The blessing is said to the source of the food. Why bother? The rabbis noticed two psalms that seem to disagree. One says: \"The earth is the LORD'S, and the fulness thereof.\" Another says: \"The heavens are the heavens of the LORD; but the earth hath He given to the children of men.\" Which is it? Does the world belong to God, or to us? Their answer: both, and the blessing is the hinge. Before the blessing, the apple is still God's. After it, it's been given to you. To eat without a blessing, they said, is a little like taking something that isn't yours yet. There's a blessing after eating too, and the Torah puts it in the order people actually eat: \"And thou shalt eat and be satisfied, and bless the LORD thy God.\" First eat, then be full, then say thanks. One rabbi, Rabbi Meir, taught that a person should say a hundred blessings every day. It sounds like a lot until you count how many things in a day get done without a second look. A hundred small pauses.","screen":["`BLESSED ARE YOU, THE SOURCE.` · `THE EARTH IS THE LORD'S.` · `BUT THE EARTH HATH HE GIVEN.` · `A HUNDRED BLESSINGS A DAY.` · then, large: `THE BLESSING IS THE HINGE.`"]},{"type":"the practice","duration":"2 min","voice":"Seventy-five seconds, with the bell. If there's something to eat or drink near you, hold it, even a glass of water. Look at it. Think of the hands it passed through to reach you: a farmer, a driver, someone stocking a shelf. If you pray, you can say a blessing you know. If not, just say thank you, to wherever it came from. Then drink, slowly.","screen":[]},{"type":"the word","duration":"25 sec","voice":"Bracha. A blessing, said to the source before you eat, and after you're full. Fifteenth word.","screen":["`BRACHA — your 15th word`."]},{"type":"the carry","duration":"15 sec","voice":"Your line: notice before you bite. Two seconds is all the pause asks for. See you tomorrow.","screen":[]},{"type":"the close","duration":null,"voice":"","screen":["`DAY 15 COUNTS.` → `TOMORROW: NOT CHARITY.`"]}]},"16":{"day":16,"title":"not charity","word":"tzedakah","hook":"not charity — justice; the box on the counter","length":"7:00","carry":"give before you feel like it","segments":[{"type":"the bell","duration":null,"voice":"","screen":["`DAY SIXTEEN.`"]},{"type":"review","duration":"20 sec","voice":"Yesterday: bracha, a blessing said to the source. Your line was notice before you bite. Today, the box on the counter. Day sixteen.","screen":["`NOTICE BEFORE YOU BITE.` → `DAY 16.`"]},{"type":"the hook","duration":"50 sec","voice":"On the counter in some Jewish kitchens sits a small tin box with a slot in the top. Nobody makes a speech about it. A kid drops in a coin after finding one on the sidewalk. A grandmother drops a few in on Friday afternoon, before she lights the candles. When it's full, it goes to a food bank, a school, a family down the street. It's easy to call that charity. But the word written on the box doesn't mean charity. It means something sharper. Today: the box on the counter, and why it isn't a favor.","screen":["`A TIN BOX WITH A SLOT.` → `COINS BEFORE THE CANDLES.` → `IT ISN'T CHARITY.`"]},{"type":"the teach","duration":"2 min 15","voice":"Tzedakah comes from tzedek: justice, righteousness, what's right. Charity, in English, sounds like a kindness you choose when you feel moved to. Tzedakah assumes something else: that part of what you have was never fully yours. It's the poor person's share, and giving it is setting things right. That's why, in Jewish law, it isn't optional. The rabbis said that even someone who lives on tzedakah should give some. The Torah says: \"Justice, justice shalt thou follow.\" The word there is tzedek, said twice. The rabbis asked why twice, and one answer is: pursue justice, and pursue it justly, even in how you give. The legal scholar Maimonides laid out eight levels of tzedakah, like the rungs of a ladder. Near the bottom: giving, but grudgingly. Higher: giving before you're asked. Higher still: giving so that neither side knows the other, so nobody has to feel ashamed. And the top rung, above all the others: helping someone so they won't need help again. A loan, a partnership, a job. Tzedakah at its best ends the need for tzedakah. Maimonides also made an observation about habit. A person who gives a thousand coins at once, he said, grows less than a person who gives one coin a thousand times, because it's the repeated act that shapes the giver. That's what the box on the counter is for. Not a feeling of generosity, waited for. A habit, built one coin at a time, until justice is just something your hands do.","screen":["`TZEDEK — JUSTICE, NOT CHARITY.` · `EVEN THE POOR GIVE.` · `EIGHT RUNGS OF A LADDER.` · `ONE COIN, A THOUSAND TIMES.` · then, large: `JUSTICE, JUSTICE SHALT THOU FOLLOW.`"]},{"type":"the practice","duration":"2 min","voice":"Seventy-five seconds, with the bell. Think of one need near you: a food bank, a neighbor, a cause you care about. Picture one small, specific amount, of money or of time. Not what feels generous. Just what's fair. Hold that amount in mind, and notice how it sits.","screen":[]},{"type":"the word","duration":"25 sec","voice":"Tzedakah. Giving as justice, the poor person's share, not a favor. Sixteenth word.","screen":["`TZEDAKAH — your 16th word`."]},{"type":"the carry","duration":"15 sec","voice":"Your line: give before you feel like it. The habit builds the giver, one coin at a time. Day sixteen.","screen":[]},{"type":"the close","duration":null,"voice":"","screen":["`DAY 16 COUNTS.` → `TOMORROW: WHY SOME PRAYERS NEED TEN PEOPLE.`"]}]},"17":{"day":17,"title":"why some prayers need ten people","word":"minyan","hook":"why some prayers need ten people","length":"7:00","carry":"some things need company","segments":[{"type":"the bell","duration":null,"voice":"","screen":["`DAY SEVENTEEN.`"]},{"type":"review","duration":"20 sec","voice":"Yesterday: tzedakah, giving as justice. Your line was give before you feel like it. Today, a count. Day seventeen.","screen":["`GIVE BEFORE YOU FEEL LIKE IT.` → `DAY 17.`"]},{"type":"the hook","duration":"50 sec","voice":"It's seven in the morning on a weekday, and a man is standing at the door of a small synagogue, looking up and down the street. Inside, eight people are waiting. He counts them again. Eight, nine with him. Then a stranger walks past with a coffee, and the man calls out, a little embarrassed: excuse me, are you Jewish? Do you have ten minutes? We need one more. In synagogues that are short a few people, scenes like this are part of the morning. Today: why some prayers can't be said until there are ten.","screen":["`SEVEN IN THE MORNING.` → `EIGHT, NINE.` → `WE NEED ONE MORE.`"]},{"type":"the teach","duration":"2 min 15","voice":"A minyan is a count: ten adults, the smallest number Jewish law calls a community. A person can pray alone, anywhere, and Jews do. But certain prayers, the ones that speak as a public, need the ten. The call to prayer, Barchu. The reading of the Torah from the scroll. And the Kaddish, including the mourner's Kaddish, said by someone who has lost a parent or a close relative. Why ten? The rabbis found it in the Torah, in a roundabout way. When twelve spies return from the promised land, ten of them bring back a frightening report, and God calls those ten a congregation. So the rabbis said: ten make a congregation. Some teachers also point back to day eight, when Abraham bargained for Sodom and stopped at ten. Who counts toward the ten is something Jews answer differently. In Orthodox communities, the minyan is ten men, thirteen and older: the age of bar mitzvah, from day six. In Reform and Reconstructionist communities, and in egalitarian Conservative ones, women count equally. Now think about what the rule does for a mourner. By custom, for eleven months after a parent dies, a traditional mourner says Kaddish every day. And because it needs a minyan, they can't say it alone. Every single day, nine other people have to show up, and answer amen. The rule makes sure grief is never carried in an empty room. There's an old teaching that when ten gather to pray, the divine presence is among them. And the tenth person doesn't have to know the words. Being there is what makes it count.","screen":["`MINYAN — A COUNT OF TEN.` · `TEN SPIES, ONE CONGREGATION.` · `WHO COUNTS, COMMUNITIES DIFFER.` · `NO MOURNER SAYS KADDISH ALONE.` · then, large: `WE NEED ONE MORE.`"]},{"type":"the practice","duration":"2 min","voice":"Seventy-five seconds, with the bell. Count your own minyan. Think of the people who would show up if you called: at a hospital, at a funeral, at a table. Name them in your head, one at a time, and see how far you get. Ten, or three, or one. Then think of someone who might not reach ten, and whose count you could join.","screen":[]},{"type":"the word","duration":"25 sec","voice":"Minyan. A count of ten, the smallest community, so some prayers are never said alone. Seventeenth word.","screen":["`MINYAN — your 17th word`."]},{"type":"the carry","duration":"15 sec","voice":"Your line: some things need company. Sometimes being the tenth is the whole job. Day seventeen.","screen":[]},{"type":"the close","duration":null,"voice":"","screen":["`DAY 17 COUNTS.` → `TOMORROW: THE DAY THE WHOLE PEOPLE SAYS SORRY TOGETHER.`"]}]},"18":{"day":18,"title":"the day the whole people says sorry together","word":"Yom Kippur","hook":"the day the whole people says sorry together","length":"7:00","carry":"you can start over","segments":[{"type":"the bell","duration":null,"voice":"","screen":["`DAY EIGHTEEN.`"]},{"type":"review","duration":"20 sec","voice":"Yesterday: minyan, the count of ten. Your line was some things need company. Today, a day when the room fills up. Day eighteen.","screen":["`SOME THINGS NEED COMPANY.` → `DAY 18.`"]},{"type":"the hook","duration":"50 sec","voice":"It's the tenth day of the seventh month, just after sunset, and the synagogue is full, with faces that don't come every week. Some are dressed in white. Some are wearing sneakers under their good clothes. The fast began before sunset, and it won't end until the stars come out tomorrow night. Then a cantor begins a melody so old and so familiar that some people cry before a word is sung, and it's sung three times, each time a little louder. From this evening to the next, a whole people will say sorry, together. Today: Yom Kippur.","screen":["`A FULL ROOM AT SUNSET.` → `WHITE CLOTHES, NO FOOD.` → `A MELODY SUNG THREE TIMES.`"]},{"type":"the teach","duration":"2 min 15","voice":"Yom Kippur means the day of atonement. The Torah sets it on the tenth day of the seventh month, in the autumn, and tells the people to afflict their souls, which the rabbis understood as a fast. So from evening to evening: no food, no water, no leather shoes, no washing for pleasure. White clothes are common, like angels, some say, who don't need to eat. The evening begins with Kol Nidre, the melody from the start, whose Aramaic words release vows. And then the confessions. They're in the plural. Not I have sinned. We have. One confession runs through the Hebrew alphabet, a wrong for every letter, and everyone says every line, lightly tapping their chest, including lines about things they personally never did. The whole community stands together as one. Nobody is singled out, and nobody gets to pretend they're above it. But the day has a limit, and the rabbis wrote it down plainly. Yom Kippur atones for what's between a person and God. For what's between you and another person, it doesn't, until you've made it right with them. So in the days before, the custom is to call, write, knock on doors, and ask forgiveness. The fast doesn't replace the phone call. In the afternoon, the story of Jonah is read: a whole city that turns around and is given another chance. As the sun goes down, the last service is called Neilah, the closing of the gates. The doors of the ark stay open, the room stands, and at nightfall one long blast of the ram's horn ends it. Then everybody goes home and eats.","screen":["`THE DAY OF ATONEMENT.` · `WE HAVE SINNED, NOT I.` · `THE FAST DOESN'T REPLACE THE CALL.` · `NEILAH — THE CLOSING OF THE GATES.` · then, large: `ONE LONG BLAST OF THE SHOFAR.`"]},{"type":"the practice","duration":"2 min","voice":"Seventy-five seconds, with the bell. Bring to mind one person you owe an apology, big or small, recent or old. Don't write the speech yet. Just picture their face, and picture the moment of saying it. Notice what makes it hard. If you pray, you can ask for the courage to make the call.","screen":[]},{"type":"the word","duration":"25 sec","voice":"Yom Kippur. The day of atonement: a day of fasting, confessing together, and closing gates. Eighteenth word.","screen":["`YOM KIPPUR — your 18th word`."]},{"type":"the carry","duration":"15 sec","voice":"Your line: you can start over. A whole people says so, out loud, every year. See you tomorrow.","screen":[]},{"type":"the close","duration":null,"voice":"","screen":["`DAY 18 COUNTS.` → `TOMORROW: RETURN, NOT REPENTANCE.`"]}]},"19":{"day":19,"title":"return, not repentance","word":"teshuvah","hook":"return, not repentance — you're not lost, you're facing the wrong way","length":"7:00","carry":"turn around","segments":[{"type":"the bell","duration":null,"voice":"","screen":["`DAY NINETEEN.`"]},{"type":"review","duration":"20 sec","voice":"Yesterday: Yom Kippur, a whole people saying sorry together. Your line was you can start over. Today, how. Day nineteen.","screen":["`YOU CAN START OVER.` → `DAY 19.`"]},{"type":"the hook","duration":"50 sec","voice":"You're driving somewhere new, and you miss the exit. The map on your phone doesn't lecture you. It doesn't tell you you're a bad driver or that you've ruined the trip. It just says: recalculating. And then, somewhere up ahead: make a U-turn. You were never lost, exactly. You were going the wrong way, and the way back was always there. English has a heavy word for what happens after we do wrong: repentance. It sounds like sackcloth and shame. The Hebrew word underneath it is much closer to that voice on the map. Today: teshuvah.","screen":["`YOU MISSED THE EXIT.` → `RECALCULATING.` → `MAKE A U-TURN.`"]},{"type":"the teach","duration":"2 min 15","voice":"Teshuvah comes from shuv, to turn, to return. It doesn't say you're broken. It says you're facing the wrong way, and you can turn around. The prophet Hosea calls out: \"Return, O Israel, unto the LORD thy God.\" Return. As if home were still there. The same Hebrew word also means an answer, a reply. Turning around is answering a call. The rabbis thought teshuvah was so basic to the world that they listed it among the things created before the world itself, before anyone had done anything wrong. The way back was built before the road. So how does a person actually turn? Maimonides lays it out plainly. Stop doing the thing. Regret it, honestly. Say it out loud, in words, not just in your head. And decide not to do it again. Then his test for complete teshuvah: you find yourself in the same situation, with the same temptation and the same strength, and this time you don't do it. Not because you can't. Because you've turned. Two bold teachings go further. One rabbi says: in the place where a person who has returned stands, even the completely righteous cannot stand. The one who turned has walked a road the others never had to walk. And another says that when teshuvah comes from love, even deliberate wrongs are turned into merits, as if the wrong turn became part of the way home. The month before the new year, and the ten days from Rosh Hashanah to Yom Kippur, are set aside for exactly this. But tradition doesn't lock the door the rest of the year. It holds it open any day.","screen":["`SHUV — TO TURN, TO RETURN.` · `BUILT BEFORE THE WORLD.` · `STOP, REGRET, SAY IT, DECIDE.` · `WHERE THE ONE WHO RETURNS STANDS.` · then, large: `RETURN, O ISRAEL.`"]},{"type":"the practice","duration":"2 min","voice":"Seventy-five seconds, with the bell. If you can, stand up and slowly turn around, all the way, so you're facing the other side of the room. Then sit. Bring to mind one habit or direction in your life that's pointing the wrong way. Not a verdict on yourself. Just the direction. Picture the U-turn. If you pray, you can ask for help turning.","screen":[]},{"type":"the word","duration":"25 sec","voice":"Teshuvah. Return: not shame, but turning around, and answering the call home. Nineteenth word.","screen":["`TESHUVAH — your 19th word`."]},{"type":"the carry","duration":"15 sec","voice":"Your line: turn around. No shame required, only the U-turn, one at a time. Day nineteen, done.","screen":[]},{"type":"the close","duration":null,"voice":"","screen":["`DAY 19 COUNTS.` → `TOMORROW: NOT A BOOK.`"]}]},"20":{"day":20,"title":"not a book","word":"Torah","hook":"not a book — a scroll, hand-written, read to the end and rolled back","length":"7:00","carry":"the world's oldest book club","segments":[{"type":"the bell","duration":null,"voice":"","screen":["`DAY TWENTY.`"]},{"type":"review","duration":"20 sec","voice":"Yesterday: teshuvah, turning around. Your line was turn around. Today, the scroll a thirteen-year-old reads from. Day twenty.","screen":["`TURN AROUND.` → `DAY 20.`"]},{"type":"the hook","duration":"50 sec","voice":"Here is a book nobody is allowed to print. Every copy is written by hand, letter by letter, with a quill, on parchment, by a scribe trained for the work. One copy can take many months. If a single letter is missing, or two letters touch, the whole thing can't be used until it's fixed. Readers don't run a finger along the writing; they follow it with a pointer, often silver. When it's carried through a room, people stand, and some reach out to kiss it. When one is too worn to use, it isn't thrown away. It's buried. Today: the scroll at the center of Jewish life.","screen":["`A BOOK NOBODY CAN PRINT.` → `MONTHS TO WRITE ONE COPY.` → `CARRIED, KISSED, BURIED.`"]},{"type":"the teach","duration":"2 min 15","voice":"Torah comes from a Hebrew root that means to aim, to point, to teach. Torah is teaching, instruction, the way pointed out. In its narrowest sense, it's the five books of Moses, Genesis to Deuteronomy, written in a scroll by a scribe called a sofer. Tradition says the scribes of old counted every letter. Wider, Torah means the whole of the Hebrew Bible. Wider still, all of Jewish teaching, down to a good answer given at a kitchen table last night. A psalm describes it like this: \"Thy word is a lamp unto my feet, and a light unto my path.\" Not a floodlight. A lamp, enough for the next step. Here's the part that makes it different from a book on a shelf. The scroll is read aloud, in public, a section at a time, from beginning to end. It's the scroll a bar or bat mitzvah reads from. And on the autumn festival called Simchat Torah, the joy of the Torah, the reader finishes the last lines of Deuteronomy, and right away, without a pause, the reading begins again at Genesis, often from a second scroll already open to the first word. People dance with the scrolls in their arms. The story is never finished. There's a teaching about the letters, too. The Torah ends with the letter lamed and begins with the letter bet. Put the end next to the beginning, and they spell lev, heart. The scroll runs in a circle, and the circle is a heart.","screen":["`TORAH — TEACHING, THE WAY POINTED OUT.` · `A LAMP, ENOUGH FOR THE NEXT STEP.` · `FINISHED, ROLLED BACK, BEGUN AGAIN.` · `LAMED AND BET SPELL HEART.` · then, large: `A LAMP UNTO MY FEET.`"]},{"type":"the practice","duration":"2 min","voice":"Seventy-five seconds, with the bell. Think of one teaching that has lit the next step for you: a line from a parent, a teacher, a book, a text you love. Not a whole philosophy. One sentence. Say it to yourself slowly, like a scribe copying it one letter at a time. Then ask where it's pointing you next.","screen":[]},{"type":"the word","duration":"25 sec","voice":"Torah. Teaching: a handwritten scroll, read from end back to beginning, year after year. Twentieth word.","screen":["`TORAH — your 20th word`."]},{"type":"the carry","duration":"15 sec","voice":"Your line: the world's oldest book club. Nobody ever finishes it, and nobody's meant to. Day twenty.","screen":[]},{"type":"the close","duration":null,"voice":"","screen":["`DAY 20 COUNTS.` → `TOMORROW: THIS WEEK'S PORTION.`"]}]},"21":{"day":21,"title":"this week's portion","word":"the parsha","hook":"this week's portion; the mountain ahead is a calendar","length":"7:00","carry":"join the cycle wherever it is","segments":[{"type":"the bell","duration":null,"voice":"","screen":["`DAY TWENTY-ONE.`"]},{"type":"review","duration":"20 sec","voice":"Yesterday: Torah, the handwritten scroll, read to the end and rolled back. Your line was the world's oldest book club. Day twenty-one, the last day of camp one.","screen":["`THE WORLD'S OLDEST BOOK CLUB.` → `DAY 21.`"]},{"type":"the hook","duration":"50 sec","voice":"This Saturday morning, in a synagogue in Buenos Aires, one in Melbourne, one in a Brooklyn basement and one in a Jerusalem neighborhood, the scroll will be opened, most weeks, to the same place. The same chapters, read in the same week, in the same order, by people who will never meet. Some people could tell you this week's portion by name without checking. It's a calendar made of text. And as camp one ends, it's also the road ahead of you on this path. Today: the portion, and where it's about to take us.","screen":["`ONE SATURDAY, EVERY CITY.` → `THE SAME CHAPTERS, THE SAME WEEK.` → `A CALENDAR MADE OF TEXT.`"]},{"type":"the teach","duration":"2 min 15","voice":"A parsha is a portion. The five books of the Torah are divided into fifty-four of them, and one is read each Sabbath, so the whole scroll is read in about a year. There are fewer Sabbaths than portions in some years, so some weeks two are read together. Each portion is named for one of its first distinctive words. The first is Bereshit, in the beginning. Then Noach, Noah. Then Lech Lecha, go forth, when Abraham leaves home. Some people date things by it, the way others use the month: that happened the week of Noach. The reading isn't only on Saturday morning. Short pieces are read on Monday and Thursday mornings too. The rabbis traced that custom back to the time of Moses, so that no one would go three days without Torah, and credited the scribe Ezra with shaping it. They even suggested a way to study it at home: read the week's portion twice, and once in translation. And here's a detail that says a lot about a scattered people. After some holidays, Israel and the rest of the world fall out of step by a week, and then quietly catch up again. The cycle bends, but it holds. That's why the carry for today is what it is. You don't start the Torah on page one when you're ready. You find out where everyone is, and you step in there. Camp one gave you twenty-one words. Next, camp two: the stories, and it begins exactly where the cycle does, at the first word, Bereshit.","screen":["`FIFTY-FOUR PORTIONS, ONE A WEEK.` · `EACH NAMED FOR AN EARLY WORD.` · `MONDAY, THURSDAY, SABBATH.` · `THE CYCLE BENDS, BUT HOLDS.` · then, large: `STEP IN WHERE EVERYONE IS.`"]},{"type":"the practice","duration":"2 min","voice":"Seventy-five seconds, with the bell, to walk back through camp one. Let the words come one by one: the mezuzah, the candles, mensch, seder, the glass, mitzvah, machloket. Abraham, Jacob, Sinai, Shabbat, shalom, the Shema, kavanah. Bracha, tzedakah, minyan, Yom Kippur, teshuvah, Torah, the parsha. Stay with whichever one pulls at you.","screen":[]},{"type":"the word","duration":"25 sec","voice":"The parsha. This week's portion, one of fifty-four, read by a whole people in step. Twenty-first word. Camp one, complete.","screen":["`THE PARSHA — your 21st word`."]},{"type":"the carry","duration":"15 sec","voice":"Your line: join the cycle wherever it is. Tomorrow, the stories begin, at the very first word. Day twenty-one.","screen":[]},{"type":"the close","duration":null,"voice":"","screen":["`DAY 21 COUNTS.` → `TOMORROW: BEFORE ANYTHING, A VOICE.`"]}]}}};
